File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Sharing Voice Palettes,  Prev: Saving And Startup,  Up: Presentation Profiles

8.12.3 Export, Back Up, And Import Palettes
-------------------------------------------

‘w’ in Voice Palettes exports effective portable entries, asking before
omitting machine-local choices.  ‘W’ backs up full ancestry and
referenced local choices.  ‘i’ imports either as an independent personal
palette under an unused name; it neither replaces ancestry nor activates
the result.  Missing installed voices are reported and retained for
replacement.  Partial import retains its proposal for Retry or Discard.

   For practice, export ‘reading-demo’ with ‘w’, review omitted choices,
and import as ‘reading-import-demo’.  Enter and preview before
activating.  An unavailable retained choice is a review request, not
proof of audio.  Delete the imported copy when no profile, child, or
rule needs it; the original is unchanged.

   Use ‘W’ when local choice data and ancestry matter.  Import still
creates a new independent palette, not an overwrite.  Neither export
contains engines or sound-pack audio, and neither is a backup of every
Emacsvox setting.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Troubleshooting,  Next: Voice Lock,  Prev: Presentation Profiles,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.13 Troubleshooting And Recovery
=================================

Start by describing the symptom and the last action that produced it.
Avoid deleting personal data, resetting all settings or reinstalling as
a first diagnostic step.  Those changes can remove both useful
configuration and the evidence needed to explain the problem.

   If speech has stopped altogether, start with *note Recovering from no
speech: (emacsvox)Recovering From No Speech.  The presentation checks
below assume a working speech route.  *note Backend diagnostics: Backend
Diagnostics And Ownership. covers further server investigation.  For the
keys in a particular Aural interface, use its ‘?’ Help.

   For a problem with otherwise working speech, choose the closest
symptom:

   • *note I saved a voice change but cannot hear it: Saved Voice Change
     Not Audible.  Check selection, the named voice, overrides and
     engine support in that order.
   • *note A setting disappeared after restarting: Settings Lost After
     Restart.  Establish what was saved and what was only selected for
     the previous session.
   • *note Only one buffer or application sounds different: Feedback
     Differs Between Buffers.  Inspect its local settings and rules
     before changing global defaults.
   • *note I cannot tell whether Apply succeeded: Unconfirmed Speech
     Changes.  Separate a completed save from worker acknowledgements
     before retrying.
   • *note I left an editor and need my unfinished work: Recovering
     Aural Changes.  Resume its own draft without changing unrelated
     drafts.

   For other unexpected feedback, inspect the current item with ‘C-e E’
or an earlier record through ‘C-e H f h’.  Use a Presentation Option for
unwanted broad optional behaviour, or inspect a narrow exception in
_Your feedback rules_ at ‘C-e H r o’.  For a missing or inappropriate
cue, validate the sound pack and inspect overrides.  For a saved setup
that no longer appears active, compare its _Active_ or _Diverged_
status.  Voice distinctions that disappear in some buffers can also come
from Voice Lock or explicit face presentation.

   A missing custom voice name is a configuration error.  Ordinary
speech keeps the text by using an unstyled voice and records a
diagnostic; it does not invent the missing palette entry.  Explain and
Aural Doctor can locate that unresolved reference.

* Menu:

* A saved voice change is not audible: Saved Voice Change Not Audible.  Trace a saved voice from palette selection to the original spoken item.
* A setting disappeared after restarting: Settings Lost After Restart.  Check the resource and startup action before recreating a missing choice.
* Feedback differs between buffers: Feedback Differs Between Buffers.  Compare local settings, integration policy and matched rules safely.
* Inspect the system with Aural Doctor: Aural Doctor Inspection.  Read configuration and backend evidence without automatic repairs.
* Review changed settings before editing again: Inspecting Changed Settings.  Compare buffer, global and saved values without changing them.
* Prepare a useful problem report: Reporting Speech Problems.  Collect a minimal reproduction and review private data before sharing.
* Recover unfinished changes: Recovering Aural Changes.  Resume drafts and distinguish saved settings from confirmed application.
* An Apply result is unconfirmed: Unconfirmed Speech Changes.  Interpret timeouts and partial results without treating them as rollback.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Saved Voice Change Not Audible,  Next: Settings Lost After Restart,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.1 A saved voice change is not audible
------------------------------------------

Use one representative item and repeat the same navigation or reading
action for each comparison.  A preview and a different action may use
different context, so changing several things at once makes the result
harder to explain.

  1. Read the editor's save and application status first.  A retained
     draft has not been saved; a partial or unconfirmed application
     needs *note its own recovery: Unconfirmed Speech Changes., not
     another speculative voice edit.
  2. Open Voice Palettes with ‘C-e H v p’ and check which palette is
     active.  Saving to the collection does not select its destination.
     Activate that palette only if you intend to use it now; otherwise
     its unchanged live sound is expected.  Return through the manager
     and menu to the source.
  3. Explain the item with ‘C-e E’.  Check that it resolves to the named
     entry you edited.  A rule may choose a different entry, or this
     kind of text may have an explicit voice rather than using
     ‘default’.
  4. Inspect the physical choice and last sample result.  An explicit
     choice outranks the shared preferred-engine policy, and that
     choice's individual tuning can mask a shared adjustment.  Then
     check availability, disablement and failures rather than repeatedly
     changing the engine order.
  5. In the voice editor, inspect _Effective sound / Where settings came
     from_.  A contextual rule may override the edited value.  Compare
     samples and reported unsupported fields to distinguish an override
     from an engine that cannot express the adjustment.

   After correcting the identified cause, return to the original item
and repeat the same action.  Confirm both the explanation and what you
hear.  If the cause remains unclear, keep the working settings and *note
prepare a problem report: Reporting Speech Problems. instead of removing
unrelated rules or voices.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Settings Lost After Restart,  Next: Feedback Differs Between Buffers,  Prev: Saved Voice Change Not Audible,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.2 A setting disappeared after restarting
---------------------------------------------

First establish whether the change was ever meant to survive restarting.
A retained draft, session rule or buffer rule does not.  Activating a
palette for a session is also separate from choosing it at startup.

  1. Identify the resource you changed: a named voice, rule, profile,
     speech control or host setting.  Reopen its own editor or manager
     and check its saved state.  Do not assume it is absent merely
     because it is not active.
  2. For a saved palette, inspect the selected startup profile.  Use
     _Use at Startup_ only when you intend to change that profile's
     palette.  Applying a Presentation Profile also retains its
     selection; a profile does not contain speech rate, engine policy,
     notification output or temporary rules.
  3. For an individual control, check exactly what that menu's Save
     records.  The *note changed-settings snapshot: Inspecting Changed
     Settings. can compare values known to Customize, but it is not a
     complete inspection of voice, rule or host-side files.
  4. If the saved value exists but is not effective, check a mode policy
     or buffer override before saving again.  For host-side settings,
     use the owning editor's application or restart procedure, not a
     voice-palette Save.

   Use *note Choose what persists: Saving And Startup. to select the
intended action.  Check the result in the current session first.  A
later restart tests startup selection; finish or deliberately save
needed work beforehand because retained drafts will end.  Do not use a
restart as the first way to investigate an unfinished draft.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Feedback Differs Between Buffers,  Next: Aural Doctor Inspection,  Prev: Settings Lost After Restart,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.3 Feedback differs between buffers
---------------------------------------

Different applications deliberately announce different information.
Compare the same kind of item and the same action before treating that
difference as a fault.  An explicit request for detail can also speak
more than automatic feedback without ignoring the saved automatic
policy.

   If the problem is too much speech, first identify what was added.
Automatic application content belongs to its focused speech settings.
Training adds explanations across buffers; inspect its current state in
‘C-e H r’ before changing it.  Extra labels can come from Presentation
Options or rules.  Speech that arrives independently may be a
notification; use *note notification routing: Notification Routing. to
understand that stream rather than treating it as part of the line you
are reading.

  1. In the affected buffer, use ‘C-e S’ to inspect its focused
     settings, or general buffer settings when no focused integration
     menu is available.  Read the current values before toggling
     anything.
  2. Use ‘C-e H d’ to compare captured-buffer values with global
     defaults and saved values known to Customize.  Return from the
     snapshot before choosing a different buffer and opening a fresh
     comparison.
  3. Explain the affected item with ‘C-e E’.  Inspect matching buffer or
     session rules, Presentation Options, Voice Lock and explicit face
     presentation.  These contributions need not be the same in another
     buffer.
  4. Change only the setting or rule that explains the difference.
     Return to the original item, repeat the action, and check an
     unaffected buffer too.  Restore the earlier value if the change
     does not address the symptom.

   *note Integration speech settings: Integration Speech Settings.
explains the different saving boundaries.  Use the application's own
chapter for automatic-speech limits and privacy; do not assume the same
key or background setting has the same effect in EAT, Shell/comint,
Notmuch and Agent Shell.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Aural Doctor Inspection,  Next: Inspecting Changed Settings,  Prev: Feedback Differs Between Buffers,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.4 Inspect the system with Aural Doctor
-------------------------------------------

Open Aural Doctor with ‘C-e H D d’.  It checks live bindings, loaded
source and byte-code, the compatibility baseline, options, profiles,
personal data, resources, spatial fallback and backend.  Opening the
report neither starts speech nor changes settings.  A row that offers a
reviewed safe repair can be acted on explicitly with ‘r’; read the
proposed effect first.

   Worker rows report current capabilities and the last inventory.
Agreement between configuration identities means the startup identities
were acknowledged; it does not prove that saved files are in use.
Missing or retired connections leave the evidence unknown.  A running
process or available engine does not establish audible playback.

   In the diagnostics menu, ‘s’ reports spatial capabilities and policy.
Lowercase ‘m’ and ‘n’ open retained local main and notification output,
reporting an absent buffer where appropriate.  Press ‘q’ in those views
to return to the selected menu item.  For reporting and logging
precautions, see *note Prepare a useful problem report: Reporting Speech
Problems.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Inspecting Changed Settings,  Next: Reporting Speech Problems,  Prev: Aural Doctor Inspection,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.5 Review changed settings before editing again
---------------------------------------------------

Press ‘C-e H d’ to inspect changes without making another one.  The
snapshot separates captured-buffer values, global defaults and saved
startup values known to Customize.  It includes loaded Emacsvox, TTS and
Omnivox options that are saved, buffer-local or nonstandard.  Saved
expressions appear as recorded.  Press ‘<RET>’ to read a complete row
with its scope, ‘g’ to refresh the snapshot, and ‘q’ to return.

   Editor and cue drafts appear separately.  The snapshot does not
reread files, query the speech host, establish that saved data is active
or include options from unloaded integrations.  Voice, rule and
host-pronunciation stores retain their own saving rules.  Return to the
owning editor or menu to change, save, apply or restart anything.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reporting Speech Problems,  Next: Recovering Aural Changes,  Prev: Inspecting Changed Settings,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.6 Prepare a useful problem report
--------------------------------------

Record the application and buffer type, the exact keys or action, what
you expected, what you heard and whether the problem repeats.  Note the
platform, Emacs and Emacsvox versions and speech backend.  Distinguish a
reported success or timeout from an audible result; neither is a
substitute for the other.  Prefer a short, non-confidential example that
reproduces the problem.

   In Aural Doctor, ‘y’ copies the report.  It does not copy spoken text
or log contents, but it can contain personal paths and configuration.
Review that copy before sharing it.  The log row identifies paths
without opening files.

   Enable detailed logging only for a reproduction whose spoken content
you are prepared to handle as sensitive data.  In the diagnostics menu,
lowercase ‘l’ toggles logging for this session, including complete
spoken text.  Note its previous state and restore that state after the
reproduction.  Capital ‘A’ opens log-file, size, retention and
buffer-visibility options in Customize.  Apply and Save there are
explicit; buffer-naming changes take effect after speech restarts.

   Review logs separately before sharing excerpts.  Remove credentials,
message content, personal paths and unrelated text; do not assume a
diagnostic file contains only technical metadata.  State which checks
you tried and their results so another person can continue the
investigation without repeating unsafe or inconclusive steps.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Recovering Aural Changes,  Next: Unconfirmed Speech Changes,  Prev: Reporting Speech Problems,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.7 Recover Unfinished Changes
---------------------------------

Press ‘C-e H p d’ to resume a retained draft directly in its owning
editor.  When several drafts exist, choose one by name; cancelling keeps
the menu open.  Opening the draft preserves its source and working
settings and saves nothing.  Finishing returns to the same menu item.
Each editor retains its own save and leave choices: the voice editor
offers Keep, Discard, or Continue editing; a rule editor can ask before
discarding an unsaved draft.

   In the common editor, ‘u’ undoes and ‘q’ offers keep, discard, or
continue.  Guided feedback ‘q’ first goes up through components, then
hides/retains the draft at the top.  To reverse an applied rule, use
‘C-e H r o’ and disable or delete it in the correct scope with ‘t~/~d’.

   Drafts last only until Emacs exits.  Discard abandons unsaved edits,
not completed saves.  The voice editor distinguishes stored settings
from successful worker application; use that distinction when deciding
to retry or make another edit.

   If saving finished but application did not, keep the result separate
from an unfinished editor draft.  Use the following procedure before
retrying.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Unconfirmed Speech Changes,  Prev: Recovering Aural Changes,  Up: Presentation Troubleshooting

8.13.8 An Apply result is unconfirmed
-------------------------------------

Apply waits for acknowledgements from each speech stream.  After an
Omnivox timeout, Workbench reports _server outcome unconfirmed_, stops
that transaction and sends no later steps.  Requests already sent may
still take effect.  A timeout does not undo the saved settings, and it
does not tell you that Omnivox rejected them.

  1. Read the result for each stream.  Partial results retain
     acknowledged streams and identify timeouts; do not describe both as
     failed when only one is unconfirmed.
  2. Check for newer staged edits before retrying.  Save them
     deliberately or cancel them; do not mistake an older reply for
     acceptance of newer work.
  3. In Workbench, ‘r’ reapplies saved engine settings and requests a
     new result.  This is an engine-settings recovery action, not a
     universal retry key in every editor.  For a palette review or voice
     save, use that view's own retained proposal and retry action.
  4. Inspect the new result and repeat the original speaking task.  If
     the result remains unconfirmed, keep that distinction in your
     problem report rather than claiming a rollback or a successful
     application.

   Do not delete the saved resource or discard unrelated drafts merely
because a worker did not answer.  *note Prepare a useful problem report:
Reporting Speech Problems. describes the evidence and privacy
precautions for further investigation.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Voice Lock,  Prev: Presentation Troubleshooting,  Up: Aural Presentation

8.14 Voice Lock And Advanced Configuration
==========================================

Voice Lock is one compatibility input to Aural Presentation, not the
entire system.  It maps Emacs faces and ‘personality’ text properties to
voices.  Faces are visual style labels that Emacsvox can use without
requiring sight.

Policy               What it controls               How to inspect or
                                                    change it
Semantic             Integration roles, events,     Explain,
presentation         states, and attributes         Presentation
                                                    Options, and
                                                    feedback rules
Explicit face        Rules deliberately matching    emacsvox-aural-toggle-face-presentation
presentation         an Emacs face                  or Aural Doctor
Voice Lock           Historical buffer              C-e H r l v locally;
                     face/personality mapping       C-e H r l V globally

   To identify Voice Lock's contribution:

  1. Move across differently styled elements, such as a comment/string
     or Org heading/link.
  2. Toggle ‘C-e d v’ in that buffer and compare the same elements.
  3. Explain with ‘C-e E’ to inspect Voice Lock/face state and
     contributing rules.

   Turning it off removes only that compatibility contribution.
Semantic presentation, explicit face rules, cues, tones, and spoken
status remain.  The global command updates existing and new buffers; a
mode can still opt out on startup.  The same controls are available at
‘C-e H r l v’ for the captured buffer and capital ‘V’ for global policy.
That submenu's ‘w’ saves only global Voice Lock through Customize.
Buffer-local Voice Lock is not captured in Presentation Profiles.

   To silence or restore the personality or mapped face at the captured
item, choose ‘s’ in that submenu.  This uses the existing local
suppression command; it can affect other text using that mapping in the
same buffer.  It does not retune the named voice, and a globally
inaudible mapping cannot be restored there.  The change lasts for the
buffer and is not saved.  Close and reopen the menu from the intended
item if its captured source has changed.

   For new changes, prefer explanation, settings menus, palettes, and
Workbench.  Legacy named voices can still use ‘-settings’ options such
as ‘voice-monotone-settings’.  Customise the option, not a generated
‘acss-...’ symbol.  Historical background is in *note Audio Formatting
And Aural CSS: (emacsvox-heritage)Audio Formatting And Aural CSS. in
_Emacspeak Heritage_.

   Named formatting voices such as ‘bolden’ and ‘annotate’ follow the
active palette.  An inherited standard entry may be implemented by a
personality's settings option within the explicit root, not a hidden
second fallback.  Explicit styles retain their own settings and rules
can change the resulting voice.

   The *note Developer Guide: (emacsvox)Integration Maintenance. covers
voice metadata, rule authoring, and integrations; *note Speech Server
Protocols: (emacsvox)Speech Server Protocols. covers delivery.  The
generated *note Emacsvox Commands And Options:
(emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Commands And Options. records exact
commands, options, and keymaps.  These complement the tasks here rather
than being prerequisites for them.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emacs Packages,  Next: Notmuch Mail,  Prev: Aural Presentation,  Up: Top

9 Applications And Integrations
*******************************

Start an application with its normal Emacs command.  Emacsvox prepares
speech support as the relevant libraries load, then presents movement,
results, and state changes through that application's structure.  Emacs
or the third-party package still owns its editing, files, network
operations, and other actions.

   Use ‘C-h m’ to learn the active mode, ‘C-h b’ to inspect its
effective keys, and ‘C-h k’ to explain one binding.  For Emacsvox's
feedback controls, ‘C-e S’ opens the current integration's focused
settings when available, otherwise general buffer settings.  ‘C-e H j’
also provides integration settings and a chooser.  These controls adjust
speech; they do not replace the application's own setup or preferences.
*note Using Online Help: Using Online Help. explains menu Help and
search.

   This chapter is a guide to maintained workflows, not a claim that
every module has a complete task guide.  The generated *note Emacsvox
Commands And Options: (emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Commands And Options.
in the _Emacsvox Reference Manual_ records public commands and options.
Consult both that reference and the application's manual when diagnosing
a problem.

   The everyday guides below cover opening and copying files, writing
and reviewing notes, and reading web pages.  They use existing speech
settings.  Start with *note Answer a prompt and return to your work:
Answering Emacs Prompts. if filename entry, completion or switching from
the manual to a working buffer is unfamiliar.

   Earlier surveys of W3, Dismal, and scanner/OCR setup remain in the
*note Historical Application Survey: (emacsvox-heritage)Emacs Packages.
in _Emacspeak Heritage_.  They describe their original period and are
not current installation instructions.

* Menu:

* Work with files and directories: Working With Files.  Open a document, save a separate working copy and find it again in Dired.
* Writing and reviewing documents: Document Authoring.  Create a note, navigate its structure and verify changes before saving.
* Structured Editing::
* Browsing Structure::
* Reading web pages with EWW: Web Browsing.  Open pages, read their structure, follow links and recover your place.
* Messaging::
* Editing Code::
* Development Environment::
* Desktop Management::
* Personal Information Management::
* Desktop Applications::


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working With Files,  Next: Document Authoring,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.1 Work with files and directories
===================================

A file is stored on disk.  Visiting it in Emacs opens a _buffer_
containing its working text; editing changes that buffer until you save.
Switching to another buffer leaves the work open.  Closing a buffer does
not delete its file.

   These tasks use ordinary text files and existing writable
directories.  They do not convert Word documents or PDFs into text.
Start with speech working and finish any open menu or prompt.  *note
Answer a prompt and return to your work: Answering Emacs Prompts.
explains filename entry and the distinction between Emacs and shell
input.

* Menu:

* Open a document and check its identity: Opening A Document.  Visit the intended text file and distinguish a new file from an existing one.
* Save a separate working copy: Saving A Working Copy.  Preserve the original saved file and make later saves go to the intended copy.
* Find and inspect files with Dired: Browsing Files With Dired.  Navigate filenames, request metadata and return without changing files.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Opening A Document,  Next: Saving A Working Copy,  Up: Working With Files

9.1.1 Open a document and check its identity
--------------------------------------------

  1. Press ‘C-x C-f’.  At the file prompt, choose the existing text file
     you want to read.  Complete or type its path, then press ‘<RET>’.
     Emacs selects the file's buffer; use ‘C-e m’ to hear its name and
     mode.
  2. Press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-e l’ to read its first line.  Check that the
     content is what you expected before editing.  A missing filename
     can instead open an empty buffer for a new file.  Opening that
     buffer alone has not created a saved document: check the name
     rather than assuming your text was lost.
  3. If you need to confirm the full destination, use ‘C-h v
     buffer-file-name <RET>’.  This Help shows the pathname visited by
     the source buffer; ‘nil’ means that buffer is not visiting a file.
     Read Help as described in *note Using Online Help: Using Online
     Help., then return to the document by its buffer name with ‘C-x b’.
     Do not edit while Help is selected.

   For reading without accidental text edits, use ‘C-x C-r’ in place of
‘C-x C-f’.  That opens the file read-only.  A read-only buffer is
different from a document with unsaved changes; do not remove the
protection merely to make an unfamiliar command work.

   When you follow this task from Info or HTML, leave the manual
available and switch to the document before trying its editing commands.
In Emacs, ‘C-x b’ selects an existing buffer by name; ‘C-x C-f’ selects
a file by its path.  Those are different questions even when the buffer
and file have similar names.

   To close a document buffer, use ‘C-x k’ and check the name and any
unsaved-work prompt.  That is not a file deletion.  Unlike a Dired or
Help buffer, an ordinary text buffer does not use ‘q’ to quit: typing it
normally inserts a letter.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Saving A Working Copy,  Next: Browsing Files With Dired,  Prev: Opening A Document,  Up: Working With Files

9.1.2 Save a separate working copy
----------------------------------

Use this route before editing a document whose original you want to
preserve.  It writes the current buffer text, including unsaved edits,
to a new filename and makes that new file the buffer's saving
destination.

  1. Select the source document and check its identity.  Press ‘C-x
     C-w’, replace the proposed filename with an unused path such as
     ‘~/evox-notes-copy.txt’, and press ‘<RET>’.  Keep the appropriate
     extension for the document, for example ‘.org’ for Org notes.
     Cancel an unexpected overwrite prompt and choose another name.
  2. Read the save result and check the new destination with ‘C-h v
     buffer-file-name <RET>’ when necessary.  A permission or write
     error is not a completed save.  Keep the buffer open while
     correcting the problem; do not discard the only copy of unsaved
     text.
  3. Return to that document, make the intended changes, and press ‘C-x
     C-s’.  This saves to the new destination.  The old saved file
     remains at its old path.  You can open it separately with ‘C-x C-f’
     to compare it with the copy.

   ‘C-x C-w’ does not keep the buffer attached to both files.  After it
succeeds, later ‘C-x C-s’ saves update the new file.  The old file
retains its previously saved content, not any unsaved changes that were
written only to the copy.  Changing the filename is also not a format
converter: naming plain text with ‘.docx’ does not create a Word
document.

   To duplicate the last saved file while leaving the working buffer's
destination unchanged, use ‘M-x copy-file <RET>’ instead.  Answer its
source and destination prompts with paths.  It copies the file on disk,
not unsaved buffer edits, and does not switch you into the new copy.
Open the destination separately to work on it.  See *note Save commands:
(emacs)Save Commands. and *note Copying and naming files: (emacs)Copying
and Naming. in the GNU Emacs Manual for other saving and copying
operations.

   If Emacs reports that another program changed the original, do not
force a save or revert simply to clear the warning.  Keep your buffer,
preserve its text under an unused filename if needed, and compare the
versions before deciding which changes to keep.  *note Simultaneous
editing: (emacs)Simultaneous Editing. explains that conflict.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Browsing Files With Dired,  Prev: Saving A Working Copy,  Up: Working With Files

9.1.3 Find and inspect files with Dired
---------------------------------------

Dired is Emacs's directory browser.  Its ordinary listing is not
document text: letters such as ‘n’, ‘p’ and ‘q’ invoke commands rather
than being inserted.  Emacsvox normally speaks filenames as you move,
leaving detailed metadata for inspection on request.  Voices and cues
can distinguish directories, links and marked entries according to your
presentation settings.

  1. Press ‘C-x d’, enter an existing directory such as ‘~/’, and press
     ‘<RET>’.  In the Dired buffer, press comma, ‘,’, to hear the
     listing header.
  2. Move with ‘n’ and ‘p’ to the next or previous entry.  To reach a
     known name in a long listing, use ‘C-s’, type part of the filename,
     and press ‘<RET>’ to finish searching.  Check the filename you
     reached before acting on it.
  3. On an ordinary file, press ‘z’ to hear its size, lowercase ‘c’ for
     its modification time, and ‘/’ for permissions.  These inspect the
     selected entry without opening it.  Uppercase ‘C’ is Dired's Copy
     command, not a second way to hear the time.  Size inspection on a
     directory instead uses the external ‘du’ program and may take
     longer or be unavailable; file-type inspection with apostrophe,
     ‘'’, similarly requires the ‘file’ program.
  4. Press ‘<RET>’ on the file to visit it in Emacs.  On a directory,
     ‘<RET>’ opens that directory's listing.  Opening announces the
     destination; ‘C-e m’ lets you check the resulting buffer and mode.
     Use ‘<RET>’ here, not ‘C-j’: Emacsvox assigns ‘C-j’ and
     Control-Enter to a separate, file-type-aware opener which may
     preview or play a file instead of visiting its text.
  5. To return from the document, use ‘C-x b’ and select the directory
     buffer.  In Dired, ‘^’ goes to the parent directory and ‘g’
     refreshes the listing from disk.  Refreshing Dired does not save or
     discard an open document's edits.  Press ‘q’ in Dired to leave the
     listing; your files remain on disk.

   Looking at a file is not the same as marking it for an operation.
‘m’ marks an entry and then moves to the next; its action cue precedes
the newly selected filename.  ‘u’ unmarks an entry and also advances.
If you are inspecting a listing with existing marks, leave them alone.
Dired operations can apply to marked files rather than the row you are
hearing.

   This guide does not require deletion, renaming, changing permissions
or running commands on files.  In particular, ‘d’ flags deletion and ‘x’
executes flagged deletions; do not use either as a way to close a
listing.  Cancellation after a file operation and ordinary text undo are
not promises of filesystem recovery.  Use *note Dired: (emacs)Dired. in
the GNU Emacs Manual before performing those operations, and *note
emacsvox-dired: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-dired. for the complete
speech-command reference.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Document Authoring,  Next: Structured Editing,  Prev: Working With Files,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.2 Document Authoring
======================

Open or create a document in its normal Emacs mode.  The mode supplies
the format's editing commands; Emacsvox presents movement, edits and
structure through speech.  Voice Lock can distinguish useful faces
supplied by the mode.  The following task writes a small Org note, then
reviews it without relying on visual formatting.  Org comes with Emacs;
no mail account or additional speech engine is required.

   For copying an existing document before editing, use *note Save a
separate working copy: Saving A Working Copy.  Outlines and template
fields are covered under *note Structured editing and templates:
Structured Editing., and tables under *note Reading Markdown and Org
tables: Reading Markdown And Org Tables.

* Menu:

* Write a note with headings: Writing An Org Document.  Create a real text file and organise a short note with Org headings.
* Review structure and correct text: Reviewing An Org Document.  Read headings, reveal their contents, search and verify an edit before saving.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Writing An Org Document,  Next: Reviewing An Org Document,  Up: Document Authoring

9.2.1 Write a note with headings
--------------------------------

Unlike the tutorial's disposable buffer, this task creates a file you
can return to.  Use an unused name and a writable directory.  The name
‘~/evox-notes.org’ is an example, not a file the package supplies.

  1. Press ‘C-x C-f’, enter ‘~/evox-notes.org’ and press ‘<RET>’.  If it
     already contains work, choose another name before typing.  The
     ‘.org’ extension normally selects Org mode; check with ‘C-e m’.  If
     a personal configuration selected another mode, run ‘M-x org-mode
     <RET>’ in this new note.
  2. Type or insert the following text in the document buffer, not in
     the minibuffer or the manual.  Start each heading at the beginning
     of its line.  One asterisk and a space introduce the main heading;
     two asterisks and a space introduce a heading beneath it.

          * Project notes
          The draft is ready for review.
          ** Questions
          Check the delivery date.
          ** Decisions
          Keep a separate review copy.

  3. Press ‘M-<’ and read with ‘C-n’ and ‘C-e l’.  Verify the heading
     words and body text.  Org heading feedback can identify structure
     without reading every leading asterisk; exact character inspection
     remains available.
  4. Press ‘C-x C-s’ and read the save result.  That writes this Org
     text to the selected file.  It does not export a formatted document
     or change speech settings.  Keep the file open for the following
     review, or reopen it later with ‘C-x C-f’ and its path.

   Choose your own heading titles when writing real notes.  Org stores
the title and body as plain text, so the structure remains in the file
even when parts of it are folded in the current view.  Do not delete a
folded heading to try to reveal its body: folding is a visibility
operation, not missing content.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reviewing An Org Document,  Prev: Writing An Org Document,  Up: Document Authoring

9.2.2 Review structure and correct text
---------------------------------------

Start in the Org document, not in an Info example.  Use ‘C-e m’ to check
the selected buffer.  If the note was reopened with content folded,
first press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-u C-u C-u <TAB>’: Control-u three times,
followed by Tab.  This shows all content without rewriting it.  See
*note Global and local cycling: (org)Global and local cycling. in the
Org manual.

   At the first heading, ‘C-c C-n’ moves to the next visible heading and
‘C-c C-p’ to the previous one.  In the example, these let you review
Questions and Decisions without reading every paragraph.  Emacsvox
announces the heading you reach.  Use ‘C-e l’ to repeat it and ‘C-n’ to
begin reading its body.

   On a heading, ‘<TAB>’ cycles that subtree's visibility.  Listen to
the state feedback before moving into it.  This is different from Tab in
a table or at a completion prompt.  The show-all sequence above provides
a known starting view if the current folds become confusing.  *note
Motion: (org)Motion. documents the other heading commands, while *note
Browsing Structure: Browsing Structure. explains the speech detail for
Org headings.

   For a focused text correction in the example:

  1. Press ‘M-<’, then ‘C-s’, type ‘ready’ and press ‘<RET>’ to finish
     the search.  Search moves point; the subsequent typing will occur
     there, not in a separate search box.  Press ‘C-e l’ to confirm the
     sentence.
  2. Point is immediately after ‘ready’.  Press ‘M-b’ to move to its
     beginning, then ‘M-d’ to remove that word.  Type ‘prepared’.  The
     surrounding text should now read ‘The draft is prepared for
     review.’.
  3. Read the whole line again before saving.  If the edit is wrong,
     press ‘C-g’ in the document to deactivate any selection, then run
     ‘M-x undo-only <RET>’ and inspect the line.  Repeat ‘undo-only’
     only as far as needed; removing the old word and inserting the new
     one can be separate steps.  Unlike ordinary ‘C-/’, ‘undo-only’ does
     not redo an earlier undo merely because you paused to read between
     corrections.  See *note Recover a text edit before saving:
     (emacsvox)Recovering Text Edits. for the full recovery procedure.
  4. Press ‘C-x C-s’ only when the text is correct.  Switch to another
     buffer and back with ‘C-x b’ to continue working; leaving the note
     does not require closing its buffer.

   If a search fails, check the text and the starting position.  ‘<RET>’
ends the search at its current position; ‘C-g’ can remove a failing
suffix before aborting the search, so listen for whether it is still
active.  Finish or cancel search before issuing editing commands.  Keep
the desired note selected when saving: a browser page, Help buffer and
document do not share Save semantics.

   An edit made after a successful save needs another save to replace
that file's contents.  Undo also changes the buffer; it does not by
itself restore the file on disk.  For a separate review version, use
*note Save a separate working copy: Saving A Working Copy. rather than
renaming the buffer and assuming that changed its destination.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Structured Editing,  Next: Browsing Structure,  Prev: Document Authoring,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.3 Structured Editing And Templates
====================================

In Outline mode, heading navigation speaks the heading you reach and
visibility commands announce what was hidden or exposed.  Start a
dedicated outline with ‘M-x outline-mode’, or enable
‘outline-minor-mode’ in a suitable document.  See *note Outline Mode:
(emacs)Outline Mode. in _The GNU Emacs Manual_ for the editor and *note
emacsvox-outline: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-outline. in the _Emacsvox
Reference Manual_ for its speech support.

   Tempo supplies template fields for modes and packages that use it.
Emacsvox speaks inserted text and the line reached when moving between
template marks.  The package supplying the template also supplies its
insertion commands.  Consult that package's mode help and *note
emacsvox-tempo: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-tempo. in the _Emacsvox
Reference Manual_.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Browsing Structure,  Next: Web Browsing,  Prev: Structured Editing,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.4 Browsing Structure
======================

Use the active mode's structure when it matches the task: Info has nodes
and menus, Outline has headings and visibility, and EWW exposes HTML
headings, links, lists, tables, and other document elements.  The
following sections explain Emacsvox's reading of headings, tables, and
application lists.  These reading controls are distinct from commands
that edit the underlying content.

* Menu:

* Reading Markdown and Org tables: Reading Markdown And Org Tables.  Read cells, rows and columns without editing the source table.

9.4.1 Reading Org headings
--------------------------

Reading a whole Org heading omits its leading stars at punctuation
levels ‘some’ and ‘none’.  Heading voices and the optional heading-level
announcement still identify the structure.  At ‘all’ punctuation, the
stars are counted, for example, "3 star" before a level-three heading.
An empty heading is spoken as "Empty heading" when its stars are
omitted.

   Word reading and partial-line reading count the stars actually
selected.  Starting at the second star of a level-three heading
therefore says "2 star".  Word movement includes the star count when it
reaches the first heading word, in either direction, without changing
the usual cursor position.  Moving through the remaining title words
does not repeat that count or heading-level announcement.  Character
reading and spelling retain the individual stars.  Collapsed headings
have a collapse cue at every level; the large-movement cue belongs to
Org structure navigation.

   Counts preserve the configured character name without adding a plural
ending.  At ‘all’ punctuation, the speech server names ‘*’, so an active
Omnivox pronunciation of "asterisk" produces "3 asterisk".  Saved
punctuation-editor changes take effect after restarting speech.
Explicit word and partial-line reads at ‘some’ or ‘none’ use
‘tts-character-to-speech-table’, the Emacs table also used for character
reading.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading Markdown And Org Tables,  Up: Browsing Structure

9.4.2 Reading Markdown and Org tables
-------------------------------------

In a Markdown or Org table, press a Control-Meta arrow to enter table
reading and move immediately.  Left/right moves by cell; up/down moves
by logical row.  The first movement announces table reading and the
destination together.  Outside supported tables, these arrows retain
their existing bindings, including inside code examples.

   ‘C-e C-t C-r’ enters without moving when the default speech prefix is
used.  It announces the dimensions and current cell.  Press ‘q’ or the
same command to restore ordinary editing keys.  From prose, ‘C-e C-t ]’
and ‘C-e C-t [’ find and enter the next or previous table.  Inside table
reading, ‘t’ and ‘T’ do the same; code examples are skipped.

   While table reading is active:

   • Up/down and Control-Meta-up/down move by logical row, retain the
     column, and read the complete destination row.
   • Control-Meta-left/right moves between cells.  Plain left/right
     retains character movement.  ‘<TAB>’ and ‘S-<TAB>’ move between
     cells without editing; ‘<RET>’ or ‘<SPC>’ reads the current cell.
   • ‘r’ reads the row and ‘c’ the column.  ‘.’ announces coordinates
     and ‘=’ gives dimensions.
   • ‘a’ changes column titles, row titles, or data/title order for this
     buffer.  The ‘emacsvox-table-reader’ customisation group sets
     defaults.
   • ‘w’ or ‘k k’ copies the cell, ‘k r’ copies the row with tabs
     between cells, and ‘k c’ copies the column with newlines between
     cells.
   • Meta-up/down leaves directly for preceding/following prose.
     Up/down also leaves across a vertical table edge when content
     exists beyond it; otherwise point stays in the table.

   A horizontal edge or a missing cell in a shorter row leaves point
unchanged and announces the boundary.  Separators are skipped.  A
separator immediately after the first row identifies column titles;
otherwise that row is data.  Coordinates distinguish the header from
one-based data rows.  Empty cells are spoken as “blank.” Wrapping never
shortens a logical cell's speech or copy.

   Reading does not align tables, create rows, or evaluate Org formulas.
Copies use cell values: ordinary links become their labels and inline
code becomes its literal text.  Use the host mode to copy exact markup
or formulas.  This reader covers simple pipe tables; HTML spans, nested
tables, and spreadsheet-specific structures have separate interfaces.

   Leaving restores ordinary mode keys.  Other editing commands retain
their normal meaning, and an edit ends the reading context.  Org's
table-editing feedback and Markdown's markup-stripping speech option
remain independent.  Agent Shell has automatic contextual table entry
and its own preferences.  In Agent Shell, Control-Meta-up/down reads the
destination _cell_, whereas plain up/down reads the destination _row_.
Do not assume identical spoken units merely because the keys are
similar; see *note Agent Shell: Agent Shell.

9.4.3 Reading application lists
-------------------------------

In an application built on Tabulated List mode, use a Control-Meta arrow
or ‘C-e C-t C-r’ to enter shared reading.  Ordinary application
navigation stays unchanged until you enter.  Within reading, up/down
reads complete logical rows, Control-Meta-left/right reads cells, and
plain left/right inspects characters.  ‘q’ leaves reading and restores
the application's usual navigation.

   Application actions take precedence over other shared reader keys.
Opening, refreshing, sorting, searching, and editing keep their
commands, including ‘<RET>’ on a button.  When a shared key is occupied,
use ‘M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-cell’, ‘M-x
emacsvox-table-reader-speak-row’, ‘M-x
emacsvox-table-reader-speak-column’, ‘M-x
emacsvox-table-reader-speak-context’, or ‘M-x
emacsvox-table-reader-speak-dimensions’.  The matching
‘emacsvox-table-reader-copy-cell’, ‘emacsvox-table-reader-copy-row’ and
‘emacsvox-table-reader-copy-column’ commands copy logical values.

   Speech and copies use complete entry text even when a displayed
column is truncated.  Column headings come from the application, not the
first item.  Coordinates count visible data rows; hidden rows are
excluded and empty cells say “blank.” Movement stops at the first or
last row.  A refresh ends reading; enter again to inspect the refreshed
list.  Custom table printers, image-only cells, and duplicate column
names are not supported by this reader.

9.4.4 Browsing CSV data
-----------------------

Use ‘emacsvox-table-find-csv-file’ to open a CSV file in the table
browser, or ‘emacsvox-table-view-csv-buffer’ for the current buffer's
CSV data.  Quoted commas, doubled quotation marks, and multiline fields
remain single logical cells.  Blank records outside quoted fields are
skipped; empty fields remain cells.  Empty input or malformed quoting
produces an error before creating a view.

   Browsing preserves source text.  If the file is already open, the
view uses that buffer's current contents, including unsaved edits,
without replacing the source buffer.  Shared table controls are active
immediately:

   • Up/down and Control-Meta-up/down move by logical row and read the
     whole row.
   • Control-Meta-left/right and ‘<TAB>~/~S-<TAB>’ move by cell.  Plain
     left/right and ‘C-b~/~C-f’ move by character; ‘C-p~/~C-n’ move by
     logical row.
   • ‘<RET>’ or ‘<SPC>’ reads the cell; ‘r’ reads the row and ‘c’ the
     column.
   • ‘.’ gives coordinates and ‘=’ dimensions.  The first row supplies
     column titles and is announced separately from data rows.
   • ‘w’ or ‘k k’ copies the cell, ‘k r’ copies the row with tabs
     between cells, and ‘k c’ copies the column with newlines between
     cells.  ‘K’ copies the whole table to the clipboard used by
     ‘emacsvox-table-paste-from-clipboard’.
   • ‘a’ changes column titles, row titles, or data order for this view
     using shared reader preferences.  ‘q’ closes the view.

   These replace the old browser cell-arrow, header-reading,
automatic-speech, and single-key table-copy bindings.  The old local
aliases ‘b’, ‘n’, ‘p’, ‘A’, ‘B’, ‘E’, and ‘T’ are removed; speech-prefix
table commands remain available.

   Search with ‘s’, ‘R’ for a row, ‘C’ for a column, or ‘h’ for headers.
‘#’ sorts on the current column and ‘M-<~/~M->’ jumps to the top or
bottom.  At an edge, movement announces the boundary and leaves point in
place.

   Filters remain on ‘f’ for a row and ‘g’ for a column; a prefix
argument requests a new filter.  Filter indexes and the ‘j’ jump prompt
retain zero-based model coordinates, including the header, although
spoken coordinates count data rows and columns from one.  For example,
the row filter ‘(0 "value" 2)’ reads the first column, “value”, and the
third column.  ‘M-l’ and ‘M-s’ load and save filters.  Filters are
explicit inspections; shared row movement always reads the whole row.
Sorting preserves filters and speech preferences.  Use ‘C-h m’ for the
full browser key map.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Web Browsing,  Next: Messaging,  Prev: Browsing Structure,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.5 Web Browsing With EWW
=========================

EWW is Emacs's built-in text-oriented web browser.  Emacsvox adds speech
and navigation using the page's HTML structure.  These commands act on
the rendered page, not on the manual you are reading now.  Start with a
speaking session and use a public page for practice; a site requiring
JavaScript may need your ordinary external browser instead.

   The keys below include Emacsvox's EWW bindings, which differ from
unmodified EWW.  In particular, lowercase ‘b’ moves to the previous
link, lowercase ‘s’ requests a readable view, and ‘?’ is not the
settings menu's Help key.  Use ‘C-h m’ or ‘C-h k’ to inspect the running
buffer.  Help can select its own buffer and remove other windows.
Return to the page with ‘C-x b’ and check its name with ‘C-e m’ before
using the browser keys below.  The upstream *note EWW guide: (emacs)EWW.
and *note emacsvox-eww: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-eww. reference
cover the rest of the interface.

* Menu:

* Open a page and establish your place: Opening A Web Page.  Enter an address, wait for rendering and identify the selected browser buffer.
* Read by heading, paragraph or search: Navigating A Web Page.  Use HTML structure when it is present and text search when it is not.
* Follow a link and return to the article: Following Web Links.  Inspect a link, follow it and distinguish browser history from buffer selection.
* Recover a cluttered or incomplete page: Recovering A Web Page.  Restore a reading view, handle missing content and keep browser settings distinct.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Opening A Web Page,  Next: Navigating A Web Page,  Up: Web Browsing

9.5.1 Open a page and establish your place
------------------------------------------

  1. Run ‘M-x eww <RET>’.  At its next prompt, enter a web address, for
     example ‘https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/’, and press ‘<RET>’.
     Text that EWW does not recognise as an address is sent to its
     configured search service; do not enter private document text as a
     practice search.
  2. Wait for loading and rendering to finish before navigating the
     page.  An opening cue means the command started, not that the
     server returned the expected article.  Read the page title and any
     error or login message.  Emacsvox normally names the buffer after
     the page title, so do not assume the buffer is called ‘*eww*’.
     Press ‘C-e m’ to identify it.
  3. Press ‘M-<’ and use ‘C-n’ or ‘C-e l’ to read.  Keep the browser
     buffer's name in mind when switching to notes or Help.  ‘C-x b’
     returns to that existing buffer; running ‘eww’ again starts another
     browsing operation.

   To request a separate browser buffer instead of reusing the default
one, start with ‘C-u M-x eww <RET>’.  This is an intentional use of the
prefix argument explained in *note Read the key instructions: Emacs Key
Notation.  It lets you keep the earlier page available while opening
another.  A title can change after navigation; check the new buffer name
before using it in a later switch.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Navigating A Web Page,  Next: Following Web Links,  Prev: Opening A Web Page,  Up: Web Browsing

9.5.2 Read by heading, paragraph or search
------------------------------------------

In the EWW page buffer, period, ‘.’, moves to the next supported heading
and comma, ‘,’, to the previous one.  ‘1’ through ‘4’ find the next
heading at the corresponding level; ‘M-1’ through ‘M-4’ go backwards.
These are HTML heading levels, not numbered paragraphs.  A page without
suitable heading markup cannot be navigated as though that structure
existed.

   Use ‘]’ and ‘[’ to move between paragraphs.  ‘M-<SPC>’ requests the
contents of the current HTML element.  Section-navigation autospeak can
read more than a heading or a line, depending on your setting; use
explicit line or element reading when you want to control the amount.
Stop current speech with ‘C-e s’ without disabling later feedback.

   For known words, press ‘C-s’, type the text, and press ‘<RET>’ to
finish at the match.  Repeat ‘C-s’ while searching to find another
occurrence.  This is Control-s, not the unmodified ‘s’ key used for a
readable view.  Search covers the current rendered buffer; it does not
submit a web search or find text that was never loaded into the page.

   Use the original page when checking whether information is genuinely
absent.  A readable or filtered view can omit menus, tables or other
useful material.  The recovery procedure below explains restoring the
page without treating a filter as an edit to the website.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Following Web Links,  Next: Recovering A Web Page,  Prev: Navigating A Web Page,  Up: Web Browsing

9.5.3 Follow a link and return to the article
---------------------------------------------

Use lowercase ‘f’ to reach the next link and lowercase ‘b’ for the
previous one.  Emacsvox presents link navigation; ‘C-e l’ reads its
surrounding line.  The link's label is not necessarily its destination
address.

   On a link, ‘k’ copies its address to Emacs's kill ring, the text
store used by ‘C-y’.  To inspect that address as text, press ‘C-x b’,
enter an unused buffer name such as ‘evox-web-address’, and press
‘<RET>’.  If that name already contains work, choose another.  Run ‘M-x
text-mode <RET>’, press ‘C-y’ to insert the address, then ‘C-e l’ to
read it.  Return to the browser by name with ‘C-x b’.  This temporary
buffer need not be saved.  Do not paste into an important document or a
web form merely to inspect a URL.  Between copying and pasting, avoid
‘C-e a’ and ‘C-e f’: repeating a message or reading a filename also adds
text to the kill ring and can replace the address as the next paste.
Check the pasted text; *note Choose text copied earlier:
(emacsvox)Choosing Earlier Copied Text. explains retrieving an older
entry.

   To copy the page's own address regardless of point, use ‘M-x
eww-copy-page-url <RET>’ instead.  Emacsvox's ‘k’ is context-sensitive
and means the link address when on a link.

   Press ‘<RET>’ on the intended link to follow it.  After the new page
loads, press lowercase ‘l’ to go back in that EWW buffer's history and
‘r’ to go forward again.  Emacsvox announces URL navigation through the
page header.  Check your position with line reading; if necessary,
search for a distinctive phrase from the article.  History is limited
and a page can change, so do not assume the exact passage is always
restored.

   To follow a link in a separate buffer, use ‘M-<RET>’.  Return to the
original page with ‘C-x b’; that is a buffer change rather than a
history step in the new buffer.  Use ‘q’ in an ordinary EWW page to
leave its view.  Uppercase ‘Q’ is Emacsvox's buffer-killing command and
is not required for this task.

   To keep a website for a future session, run ‘M-x eww-add-bookmark
<RET>’ on the wanted page.  ‘M-x eww-list-bookmarks <RET>’ opens the
saved list; select a bookmark and press ‘<RET>’ to visit it.  These are
website bookmarks, not the Emacsvox ebook-position marks on ‘m’, and
they do not promise to preserve an exact reading position on a changing
page.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Recovering A Web Page,  Prev: Following Web Links,  Up: Web Browsing

9.5.4 Recover a cluttered or incomplete page
--------------------------------------------

In a page buffer, lowercase ‘s’ runs EWW's readable-view command.  It
tries to isolate the main text; it is not the same action as ‘s’ inside
the focused settings menu.  To return from the filtered view, run ‘M-x
emacsvox-eww-restore <RET>’.  This restores the retained canonical view
rather than changing the site.  The command is also on ‘<DEL>’, but its
name avoids differences in how keyboards report Backspace and Delete.

   If the retained view is unavailable or you need fresh content, ‘g’
reloads the current address.  Reloading makes a new request and can lose
form input or change the page and reading position.  Save needed notes
and finish or abandon forms deliberately before reloading.  Search for a
known phrase afterwards rather than assuming the old screen position
still identifies the passage.

   For content that requires JavaScript or another unsupported browser
feature, use ‘M-x eww-browse-with-external-browser <RET>’ to hand the
page to the configured external browser.  The external browser and any
screen reader then provide its controls; Emacsvox's EWW keys do not
operate there.  Browser configuration, login state and
downloaded-content handling can differ.  A failed or inaccessible
website is not in itself a reason to change speech engines or reset
voices.

   For speech preferences, open ‘C-e S’ from EWW.  In that focused menu,
‘s’ toggles section-navigation autospeak and ‘i’ changes image
suppression for subsequent rendering.  The latter also changes the
shared SHR image default while preserving buffer-local overrides; it
does not reload the current page.  Lowercase ‘w’ saves those two
Emacsvox defaults, ‘A’ opens advanced settings, and ‘q’ returns.  These
are presentation choices, not EWW's site or account preferences.
Returning does not undo changes you have already made.

   The Emacs/W3 and w3m survey is historical.  Do not install either on
the strength of the Heritage manual; use EWW unless a separately
maintained integration says otherwise.  The GNU Emacs Manual's *note
EWW: (emacs)EWW. entry provides its general introduction.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Messaging,  Next: Editing Code,  Prev: Web Browsing,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.6 Messaging
=============

For indexed mail search, asynchronous results, threads, attachments, and
privacy controls, follow *note Notmuch Mail: Notmuch Mail.  For another
mail or messaging client, start with its own manual and mode help, then
find the corresponding Emacsvox module in the generated reference.  The
presence of a module is not a substitute for configuring the underlying
account or service.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Code,  Next: Development Environment,  Prev: Messaging,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.7 Editing Code
================

Programming modes provide language-aware movement, indentation,
compilation, and navigation.  Emacsvox speaks these operations and uses
Voice Lock to distinguish comments, strings, warnings, and language
constructs where the mode exposes them.  Use the language mode's help
for editing commands and its named integration in the generated
reference for speech details.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Development Environment,  Next: Desktop Management,  Prev: Editing Code,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.8 Development Environment
===========================

Compilation, diagnostics, project navigation, version control,
debuggers, and language servers can share the Emacs workspace.
Configure each through its own manual and verify its operation before
combining it with other components.  Emacsvox supplies speech to many of
them; their underlying operations remain owned by those tools.  *note
Agent Shell: Agent Shell. provides the separate guide to AI-agent
sessions and their permission, feedback, and review controls.

9.8.1 Magit
-----------

Magit navigation speaks the section or line you reach.  Empty and
whitespace-only lines use the normal line tones.  Commit hashes are
silent by default; changing the voice assigned to ‘magit-hash’ affects
navigation and explicit reading consistently.  Moving the section
highlight preserves that choice.

   After successful staging or unstaging in a Magit view, speech reads
where point lands after the refresh.  Failures remain explicit.  Staging
and unstaging from an ordinary file buffer still confirm their outcome.
Branch checkout, branch and remote renaming, remote removal, and stash
creation announce results.  Repository-list fetching reports partial
failures.  Background completion uses the notification stream; entering
status and navigating use the main stream.

   Visibility commands describe collapsed, expanded, or partly expanded
sections.  Copying a heading without a value reports that there is
nothing to copy rather than presenting an old kill-ring entry as a new
copy.

   Magit uses Transient menus for Push, Fetch, Commit, Branch, and other
actions.  Opening one speaks a short introduction and navigation
guidance.  Its automatic shortcut list remains visible without being
read on the notification stream.

   Up/down browses items and left/right moves between sections.  The
shared navigation preferences apply to Magit as well as Emacsvox menus:
wrapping within a section is the default, and continuous navigation or
stopping at section edges can be selected.  Announcement order can be
item first, section first, or dependent on the movement.  Detailed
command descriptions are enabled by default; value adjustments still
give brief feedback.  Configure these choices through ‘C-e H s n’, as
described in *note Getting started with settings menus: (emacsvox)Aural
Home.

   An item announcement identifies its key, relevant value, and
description; capital keys are named explicitly.  Section announcements
include the heading and position according to the selected policy.  Do
not expect the same section-name repetition after changing that policy.

   Use ‘C-j’ or ‘M-<RET>’ to activate a selected Magit item, or type its
shortcut.  ‘?’ followed by the shortcut opens Help.  ‘C-g’ closes the
current menu or leaves its help mode.  Unlike Emacsvox's own settings
menus, ‘q’ is not a universal quit key in Transient applications.  An
unavailable key gives brief recovery guidance on the main stream.

   Browsing only selects an item.  Activating an action can perform a
Git operation, while activating a switch changes an argument for the
next action.  Listen to the description before activation.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Management,  Next: Personal Information Management,  Prev: Development Environment,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.9 Desktop Management
======================

Buffers, windows, frames, and tabs organise the workspace.  ‘C-x b’
selects a buffer; specialised buffer-list and tab interfaces provide
their own mode help.  *note Audio Desktop: Audio Desktop. explains the
common navigation model so it need not be learnt again for each
application.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Personal Information Management,  Next: Desktop Applications,  Prev: Desktop Management,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.10 Personal Information Management
====================================

Calendar, Org, and related packages define their data formats and
workflows.  Emacsvox presents navigation, state changes, and structured
content when those modes are active.  Begin with the application's
manual and live help, then consult its matching integration in the
generated reference.

   Org's focused menu at ‘C-e S’ includes its table-editing feedback
choices.  Those are separate from the shared reading mode above: the
focused menu's global default does not replace an existing buffer-local
choice, and the shared reader retains its own title and speaking
preferences.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Desktop Applications,  Prev: Personal Information Management,  Up: Emacs Packages

9.11 Desktop Applications
=========================

9.11.1 Terminal Applications
----------------------------

Use ‘M-x shell’ for ordinary line-oriented shell commands.  For a full
terminal, follow *note EAT Terminal Access: EAT Terminal Access., or use
built-in *note Term: Running Terminal Based Applications. when no
additional package is wanted.  Their input, review, and asynchronous
feedback models differ; choose the appropriate chapter rather than
transferring terminal keys from one application to another.

   In Shell/comint, ‘C-e S’ opens focused speech settings.  ‘s’ toggles
automatic output speech and ‘b’ toggles background monitoring.
Background reading needs automatic speech enabled.  Both settings affect
the captured buffer for its lifetime; this menu has no Save action.  ‘q’
returns to the originating entry or closes a directly opened focused
menu.

   In WSL Shell buffers, command-name completion skips Windows drive
directories by default.  Scanning large directories such as
‘C:\Windows\System32’ can otherwise pause Emacs.  Windows programs still
run by name, and explicit filename completion, including ‘/mnt/c/’
paths, remains available.  To include Windows command names again, use
‘M-x customize-option <RET> emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands
<RET>’.  This option does not change native Windows or remote Shell
buffers.

9.11.2 Forms Mode
-----------------

Forms presents records from a structured text file one at a time.
Emacsvox speaks fields, record movement, searches, and state changes.
Forms requires a format specification; see *note Forms Mode:
(emacs)Forms Mode. in _The GNU Emacs Manual_ for setup and *note
emacsvox-forms: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-forms. in the _Emacsvox
Reference Manual_ for speech.

9.11.3 OCR And PDF-To-Text With PaddleOCR
-----------------------------------------

Emacsvox uses PaddleOCR PP-StructureV3 to turn an image or PDF into
UTF-8 Markdown text.  It recovers reading order and represents
recognised tables as Markdown or embedded HTML.  Keep ‘.md’ output when
structure matters; Markdown is plain text and can be named ‘.txt’ when
its lightweight markup is acceptable.

   This route is intended for scanned or image-only PDFs.  For a
born-digital PDF with a trustworthy text layer, extracting that layer is
faster and usually more exact than OCR.  Recognition still depends on
scan quality; upright, high-contrast pages at 300 DPI are a useful
starting point.

  1. Prepare the isolated OCR environment

     The supported baseline is CPU-only x86-64 WSL2 or GNU/Linux.
     Paddle's Linux wheel requires an AVX-capable x86-64 processor and
     Python 3.9 through 3.13.  Do not use Ubuntu 26.04's Python 3.14 for
     this environment.  The commands below install Python 3.13
     separately without altering Emacsvox, system Python, or another
     project's packages.  Installation uses about 1.7 GB; the first
     complete PP-StructureV3 run downloads approximately another 1.8 GB
     of models, cached for later runs.

     Follow the uv installation guide
     (https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/) if ‘uv’
     is unavailable, then create the environment:

          uv python install 3.13
          uv venv --python 3.13 --seed \
            "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv"

     Install the tested CPU runtime and PaddleOCR's ‘all’ variant, which
     includes the document-parsing, table, and formula dependencies:

          "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
            paddlepaddle==3.3.0 \
            -i https://www.paddlepaddle.org.cn/packages/stable/cpu/
          "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
            'paddleocr[all]==3.7.0'

     From the checkout, verify that the launcher finds both packages:

          ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --check

     For a Debian installation, use the installed launcher, here and in
     the recognition commands below:

          /usr/share/emacsvox/bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --check

     The expected report names PaddlePaddle 3.3.0, PaddleOCR 3.7.0, and
     the CPU device.  A missing ‘ccache’ warning is harmless for normal
     inference.

     oneDNN, formerly MKL-DNN, is an optional CPU execution backend, not
     an OCR model.  The launcher leaves it disabled because PaddlePaddle
     3.3.0 has an upstream PIR-to-oneDNN conversion defect
     (https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/Paddle/issues/77340).  In the
     tested pipeline this aborts initialisation at the formula model,
     although that model is not itself at fault.  Standard CPU kernels
     keep every recognition feature enabled, including tables and
     formulas.  The trade-off is speed, not capability.

  2. Recognise and read a document

     For one image, redirect the clean Markdown result to a file.  Model
     downloads and progress go to standard error, not the recognised
     text:

          ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/page.png" > page.md

     PDF input uses the same command.  Paddle renders and recognises
     every page, then joins them in reading order:

          ./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/scanned-document.pdf" > document.md

     A large PDF can require substantial CPU time: table, layout,
     formula, orientation, and unwarping models are all active by
     default.

     Inside Emacsvox, press ‘C-e C-o’ or run ‘M-x emacsvox-ocr <RET>’.
     In the OCR buffer, ‘o’ selects an image or PDF.  Recognition runs
     asynchronously and adds its result as a new page when complete.
     Listen for the completion cue, then use ‘<SPC>’ to read the current
     result, ‘s’ to save it, or ‘w’ to save the whole OCR buffer.  ‘[’
     and ‘]’ move between results.  ‘d’ opens the per-user OCR
     directory.  Progress and errors are retained separately in
     ‘*Emacsvox OCR Errors*’.

  3. Configure recognition and diagnose the adapter

     Use ‘M-x customize-group <RET> emacsvox-ocr <RET>’ for ordinary
     Emacs configuration rather than shell environment variables.  The
     bundled adapter provides typed options:

        • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-python’ selects an exact Python
          executable; ‘nil’ uses the isolated installation above.
        • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-language’ and
          ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-device’ choose recognition language
          and device.
        • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-table-recognition’ and
          ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-formula-recognition’ control the
          structural models; both default to enabled.
        • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-orientation’,
          ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-unwarping’, and
          ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-text-line-orientation’ control
          image correction.
        • ‘emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-enable-mkldnn’ enables oneDNN/MKL-DNN
          acceleration, not an OCR model.  Keep it disabled for the
          tested PaddlePaddle 3.3.0 CPU setup on WSL.

     These options apply only when ‘emacsvox-ocr-engine’ names the
     bundled adapter.  A different engine retains the generic interface.
     ‘emacsvox-ocr-engine-options’ is an advanced list of literal
     arguments; for the bundled adapter it follows, and can override,
     the typed options.

     For standalone shell or automation use, ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_VENV’
     changes the environment directory, ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_PYTHON’
     selects an executable, and ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_LANGUAGE’ or
     ‘EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_DEVICE’ changes defaults.  See
     ‘./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --help’ for switches.  These environment
     settings are not the primary Emacs configuration interface.

     Direct SANE scanner-acquisition commands remain for compatibility
     but are not part of this tested WSL baseline.  Scan with a Windows
     application and select the resulting image or PDF with ‘o’ when
     that is the available acquisition route.  Scanner configuration is
     independent of PaddleOCR.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Mail,  Next: Agent Shell,  Prev: Emacs Packages,  Up: Top

10 Notmuch Mail
***************

Notmuch provides the mail index and Emacs interface.  Install compatible
command-line and Emacs components, then configure the mail store and
database using the Notmuch Emacs manual
(https://notmuchmail.org/doc/latest/notmuch-emacs.html).  Emacsvox does
not fetch or send mail, create the database, or replace Notmuch's
commands.  It adds structured speech to Hello, Search, and Show; Tree
has more limited support, described below.

   Follow this chapter to search, read, and manage mail with speech,
then adjust which information is spoken automatically.  The generated
*note emacsvox-notmuch: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-notmuch. section in
the _Emacsvox Reference Manual_ provides the complete command and option
reference.

* Menu:

* Start Notmuch: Starting Notmuch.  Open Hello or start a query with the speech integration loaded.
* Use Hello and start a search: Notmuch Hello And Searches.  Choose a search and understand asynchronous results and refreshes.
* Navigate and inspect Search results: Notmuch Search Results.  Read thread summaries, inspect full fields, and open a result.
* Read a thread and its MIME parts: Notmuch Messages And Attachments.  Read messages, inspect attachments, and choose explicit MIME actions.
* Change tags and archive state: Notmuch Tags And Archiving.  Hear the actual outcome of tag and archive commands.
* Choose fields, status, and privacy: Notmuch Speech And Privacy.  Set spoken fields, automatic limits, completion policy, and privacy.
* Know the support boundaries: Notmuch Support Boundaries.  Distinguish supported views, limited Tree support, and composition.
* Troubleshoot the live integration: Notmuch Troubleshooting.  Check live keys, package identity, and the speech diagnostics.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Starting Notmuch,  Next: Notmuch Hello And Searches,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.1 Start Notmuch
==================

Run ‘M-x notmuch’ to open Hello.  With the standard Emacsvox global maps
loaded, ‘C-' m’ runs ‘notmuch-search’ directly and asks for a query.
Emacsvox loads its integration when Notmuch loads and marks new Hello,
Search, Show, and Tree buffers with Notmuch semantic context.

   Loading speech support does not alter mail, tags, files, or Notmuch
configuration.  Notmuch owns database, tag, archive, attachment, and
composition operations; Emacsvox presents the result of the action you
invoke.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Hello And Searches,  Next: Notmuch Search Results,  Prev: Starting Notmuch,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.2 Use Hello and start a search
=================================

Moving among Hello widgets speaks each saved search's label and
displayed message count, or identifies the button or link reached.  Use
these common Hello commands:

‘TAB’
     ‘widget-forward’ reaches the next search, field, button, or link.
‘<backtab>’
     ‘widget-backward’ reaches the preceding widget.
‘RET’
     ‘widget-button-press’ activates the widget; on a saved search it
     starts the query.
‘j’
     ‘notmuch-jump-search’ chooses a configured search.
‘g’
     ‘notmuch-refresh-this-buffer’ refreshes Hello.
‘m’
     ‘notmuch-mua-new-mail’ starts composition, handled by Message mode
     and the separate ‘emacsvox-message’ integration.

   Press ‘?’ in a Notmuch view or ‘C-h m’ for the bindings supplied by
your installed Notmuch version.  A search can display its first result
before all rows finish rendering, so an initial message announcement and
a later count need not arrive together.

10.2.1 Understand asynchronous search completion
------------------------------------------------

The default ‘adaptive’ policy gives useful early feedback without
rereading content after you have moved on.  For a search you start,
other than a refresh, a progress cue is given while its buffer remains
selected.  The first complete result can then be spoken while the search
continues, using the same summary, voices, and status cues as thread
navigation.  This never moves point or reads an empty row.

   When the new search remains selected, you have issued no command in
it, and no input is pending, the first result is spoken once on the main
stream.  Completion reports the final thread count on the notification
stream without repeating that result.  If completion occurs before an
early result could be announced, it reports the count and result at
point, or a zero-thread summary.

   If you navigate, invoke another command, or leave before completion,
the later announcement is only a generic count on the notification
stream.  It does not reread the selected row or disclose the query,
sender, recipients, subject, tags, or attachment metadata.  Killing the
owning search buffer silently cancels that delivery.

  1. Refresh an existing result

     An explicit refresh differs from a new search.  It queues only the
     generic count on the main stream, without interrupting speech
     already playing.  Navigation can interrupt this announcement,
     including after you leave the search buffer before the refresh
     finishes.  Refresh completion never rereads the selected row.

  2. Choose less automatic feedback

     ‘summary’ gives a generic count and completion cue; ‘cue’ gives
     only the cue; ‘silent’ suppresses successful completion.  These
     policies use the same main-stream selection for refreshes.  Other
     search summaries use the notification stream.  Failures remain
     audible and generic with every policy; refresh failures also use
     the main stream.  The deliberate global refresh-all command remains
     silent.

     Use the focused settings described under *note Notmuch fields and
     privacy: Notmuch fields and privacy. to choose a policy.  *note
     Speech Output And Notifications: (emacsvox)Aural Speech Output.
     explains the general stream behaviour; the refresh rules above are
     specific to this integration.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Search Results,  Next: Notmuch Messages And Attachments,  Prev: Notmuch Hello And Searches,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.3 Navigate and inspect Search results
========================================

Thread movement reads configured semantic fields and status cues.  At a
boundary, speech identifies the beginning or end instead of repeating
the current row.  Emacsvox makes the arrow keys equivalent to Notmuch's
next and previous thread commands.

‘n’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-next-thread’.
‘<down>’
     Also run ‘notmuch-search-next-thread’.
‘p’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-previous-thread’.
‘<up>’
     Also run ‘notmuch-search-previous-thread’.
‘RET’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-show-thread’.  Show lands on a useful visible
     body line or leaf MIME part and presents the opened message.
‘SPC’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-scroll-up’.  This scrolls the viewport rather
     than selecting a semantic thread.
‘C-c C-p’
     Run ‘emacsvox-notmuch-speak-search-details’ to read all configured
     fields for the result at point without automatic content limits.
‘+’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-add-tag’.
‘-’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-remove-tag’.
‘*’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-tag-all’ for the selected results.
‘a’
     Run ‘notmuch-search-archive-thread’.
‘g’
     Run ‘notmuch-refresh-this-buffer’; completion follows the
     interruptible refresh policy above.

   Search defaults to authors, subject, relative date, matched and total
message counts, and ordinary tags.  Status tags assigned auditory cues
are not repeated as words.  *note Notmuch fields and privacy: Notmuch
fields and privacy. explains omission and ordering of sensitive fields.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Messages And Attachments,  Next: Notmuch Tags And Archiving,  Prev: Notmuch Search Results,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.4 Read a thread and its MIME parts
=====================================

Opening a thread selects its newest unread message among the open
messages matching the search.  If there is none, Notmuch's initial
selection is retained.  Emacsvox speaks the selected message's summary
and first visible body line, skipping hidden alternative bodies.  When
no body text is visible, point falls back to a leaf MIME-part button
where possible.  That button announces a bounded filename, content type,
and size.

   Line reading speaks a visible sender row even if the preceding
message ends with folded quoted text; it does not unfold the quote.  In
a short graphical window, Down advances through ordinary body text
before speaking the next line, even when the current line is only partly
visible.  Images or text taller than the window retain Emacs's partial
scrolling.

‘n’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-next-open-message’.
‘p’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-previous-open-message’.
‘N’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-next-message’, including closed messages.
‘P’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-previous-message’, including closed messages.
‘SPC’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-advance-and-archive’.  Within a message, speech
     reads the newly visible page; at a message transition it reads the
     new message; at the thread end it reports the actual archive
     outcome and destination.
‘DEL’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-rewind’ and read the resulting page or message.
‘RET’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-toggle-message’ and cue the resulting visibility.
‘M-RET’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-open-or-close-all’ to open every message and
     report the state and total, for example "Opened all 5 messages."
     ‘C-u M-<RET>’ closes every message and reports the corresponding
     total.
‘C-c C-p’
     Run ‘emacsvox-notmuch-speak-show-position’ for the message ordinal,
     total, and full configured details.  On a MIME-part button it
     instead reads the complete part description, including its full
     filename.
‘TAB’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-next-button’ and identify the button or MIME
     part.
‘<backtab>’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-previous-button’ and identify the button or MIME
     part.
‘w’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-save-attachments’.  Speech confirms when the
     command returns, but Notmuch exposes no per-attachment result here.
     The confirmation therefore cannot distinguish partial skips.
‘. s’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-save-part’ on the current MIME part.
‘. v’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-view-part’.
‘. o’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-interactively-view-part’ and choose an external
     action.
‘. m’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-choose-mime-of-part’.

   Viewing, saving, overwriting, or launching a MIME part is an explicit
Notmuch operation.  Navigation and detail inspection never open an
attachment or an external viewer by themselves.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Tags And Archiving,  Next: Notmuch Speech And Privacy,  Prev: Notmuch Messages And Attachments,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.5 Change tags and archive state
==================================

Emacsvox compares displayed tags before and after an interactive change
and reports actual additions and removals.  Adding an existing tag or
removing an absent tag reports "Tags unchanged".  Errors and cancelled
commands do not produce success feedback.

   These Show commands receive result-aware feedback:

‘+’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-add-tag’ on the current message.
‘-’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-remove-tag’.
‘*’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-tag-all’ across the thread.
‘a’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-archive-message-then-next-or-next-thread’.
‘A’
     Run ‘notmuch-show-archive-thread-then-next’.

   Archive feedback distinguishes a real change, unchanged archive tags,
and an unset ‘notmuch-archive-tags’.  Movement wrappers combine the
result instead of duplicating the feedback of nested tag or archive
commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Speech And Privacy,  Next: Notmuch Support Boundaries,  Prev: Notmuch Tags And Archiving,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.6 Choose fields, status, and privacy
=======================================

From a Notmuch buffer, press ‘C-e S’ for focused speech settings, or
reach it through ‘C-e H j c’.  The same menu is available from Hello,
Search, Show, and Tree.  Availability of settings in Tree does not add
Tree-specific navigation or message formatting.

   ‘c’ opens explained completion choices.  Arrows browse without
applying, ‘<RET>’ applies and returns, and ‘q’ cancels.  ‘?’ followed by
a choice's key opens spoken Help; ‘q’ returns to that browsed choice.
In the parent menu, ‘f’ and ‘b’ control per-field character and UTF-8
byte limits, while ‘t’ and ‘B’ control total limits.  Shift-left/right
adjusts a selected limit by 100; ‘<RET>’ accepts an exact positive
integer or ‘none’.  Disabling one limit leaves the others active.  ‘w’
saves these four limits and completion policy as the five displayed
global defaults, not every Notmuch option.

   For ordered search/message fields, use ‘s’ and ‘m’.  ‘i’ and ‘I’ open
the status-tag and fallback-cue maps.  These linked Customize editors
retain custom formatter functions and use their own Apply, Save, and
Reset actions.  Returning with ‘q’ does not save them.  ‘p’ opens
Presentation Options and ‘A’ advanced settings, including separators.
The full group is also available through ‘M-x customize-group <RET>
emacsvox-notmuch <RET>’.  These speech settings do not alter Notmuch's
database or its own display configuration.

10.6.1 Choose the information in a spoken summary
-------------------------------------------------

‘emacsvox-notmuch-search-result-fields’
     Defaults to ‘(authors subject date count tags)’.  Remove sensitive
     fields, reorder them, or add a formatter function.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-show-message-fields’
     Defaults to ‘(from date to cc tags attachments)’.  Show omits the
     subject but includes To and Cc; remove ‘to’, ‘cc’, or other fields
     when privacy requires it.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-search-field-separator’
‘emacsvox-notmuch-show-field-separator’
     Set the text between configured fields.  Both default to comma and
     space.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-search-status-icons’
‘emacsvox-notmuch-show-status-icons’
     Map unread, replied, forwarded, flagged, and personal tags to cues
     or silence.  Removing an entry makes that status available to
     ordinary tag speech.  When ‘mail-message-status-cues’ is enabled in
     Presentation Options, its rules own the shared semantic status
     cues; the maps still suppress the corresponding spoken status tags.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-search-completion-style’
     Select ‘adaptive’, ‘summary’, ‘cue’, or ‘silent’ using the
     completion behaviour described earlier.

10.6.2 Understand limits and full inspection
--------------------------------------------

Mail-controlled text is bounded before automatic speech and Aural
history.  Defaults are 256 characters and 1,024 UTF-8 bytes per field,
then 1,000 characters and 4,096 bytes for the complete automatic
presentation.

‘emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-character-limit’
‘emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-byte-limit’
     Bound each automatic field before formatting and submission.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-character-limit’
‘emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-byte-limit’
     Bound the final automatic Notmuch presentation.
‘emacsvox-notmuch-mime-node-limit’
‘emacsvox-notmuch-mime-depth-limit’
     Default to 4,096 cons nodes and depth 64, limiting inspection of
     malformed, cyclic, broad, or deeply nested MIME metadata.  An
     incomplete scan reports a conservative attachment lower bound, not
     a claim that no attachment exists.

   A shortened Search or Show announcement explains what was omitted and
points to ‘C-c C-p’ for full configured details.  A shortened Hello item
points to ‘<RET>’.  Explicit Search and Show inspection deliberately
bypasses automatic content limits; use it only when speaking the full
configured fields is appropriate.  These limits are not a general
privacy guarantee for every explicit command or another application's
output.

10.6.3 Understand retained and notification content
---------------------------------------------------

Main-stream Notmuch presentations can enter bounded Aural history.  By
default, Emacsvox retains at most 20 presentations and 4,096 UTF-8 bytes
of speech preview per record.  Records contain frozen data, source
names, and positions, but never retain source buffers.  Inspect them
through ‘C-e H f h’ or ‘M-x emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback’.  Set
‘emacsvox-aural-presentation-history-limit’ to zero to disable this
history.

   Generic asynchronous completion contains no query or message
metadata, but a notification-stream completion may be retained by the
notification subsystem.  Choose ‘cue’ or ‘silent’ if even a generic
thread count is unwanted.  Review notification-device and logging
settings separately from mail field choices.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Support Boundaries,  Next: Notmuch Troubleshooting,  Prev: Notmuch Speech And Privacy,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.7 Know the support boundaries
================================

Hello
     Widget navigation, view lifecycle, and searches have
     Notmuch-specific feedback.
Search
     Results, asynchronous lifecycle, tags, archiving, opening, and
     configured detail inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
Show
     Messages, MIME parts, pages, visibility, tags, archiving, and
     explicit inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
Tree
     Receives ‘emacsvox-aural-module’ context only.  Core speech can
     operate, but this module has no Tree-specific row formatter,
     navigation, mutation, visibility, or asynchronous lifecycle.
Composition
     Standard Message mode owns composition.  Speech after new-mail,
     reply, forward, or resume transitions belongs to
     ‘emacsvox-message’, not the Notmuch integration.

   There is no independent Notmuch speech-disable or unload command.
Its advice, hooks, and Search arrow bindings remain installed for the
Emacs session after loading.  Repeated loading is idempotent and does
not duplicate them.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Notmuch Troubleshooting,  Prev: Notmuch Support Boundaries,  Up: Notmuch Mail

10.8 Troubleshoot the live integration
======================================

Use ‘?’ or ‘C-h m’ in the affected view to check its current keys and
‘M-x notmuch-version’ to identify the package.  Consult Notmuch's own
manual for its behaviour before treating a database or display problem
as a speech problem.

   For bindings, loaded source versus byte-code, presentation rules,
sounds, or backend issues, open Aural Doctor through ‘C-e H D d’ or ‘M-x
emacsvox-aural-doctor’.  Recent feedback at ‘C-e H f h’ or ‘M-x
emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback’ shows the bounded presentation
submitted for an action.  These diagnostics do not query or modify mail.

   For source-level work, *note Integration Maintenance: Integration
Maintenance. contains the build, test, renderer, and asynchronous
lifecycle contracts.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell,  Next: EAT Terminal Access,  Prev: Notmuch Mail,  Up: Top

11 Agent Shell
**************

Agent Shell is an Emacs package for interactive AI-agent sessions.
Emacsvox adds speech and structured navigation to its prompts,
responses, permissions, and other activity.  Agent Shell remains
responsible for providers, sessions, prompt editing, and permission
decisions.  Install and configure Agent Shell using its own
documentation (https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell); Emacsvox
requires version 0.85.3 or newer.

   This chapter follows a session from writing a prompt through
receiving and reviewing an answer, then explains speech choices and
recovery.  The generated *note emacsvox-agent-shell:
(emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-agent-shell. section in the _Emacsvox
Reference Manual_ lists the complete Emacsvox command and option
interfaces.

* Menu:

* Start and control a session: Starting An Agent Shell Session.  Write prompts, follow responses, steer work, and manage queued input.
* Answer an agent's question: Answering Agent Questions.  Review and complete an optional structured question form.
* Choose automatic speech and privacy: Agent Shell Speech And Privacy.  Choose session feedback, background disclosure, and automatic limits.
* Respond to urgent permissions: Agent Shell Permissions.  Find and decide a permission request without confusing routine speech.
* Inspect a response on demand: Reading Agent Responses.  Read a completed response in full or request a structural overview.
* Navigate transcript blocks: Agent Shell Transcript Navigation.  Move among responses, prompts, headings, links, tools, and errors.
* Read and copy source blocks: Agent Shell Source Blocks.  Read logical code, copy it, and open a change review.
* Navigate and copy rendered tables: Agent Shell Tables.  Read table cells, rows, and columns, and understand local key conflicts.
* Recover from interruption or failure: Agent Shell Recovery.  Interrupt work, inspect the previous response, and recover the session.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Starting An Agent Shell Session,  Next: Answering Agent Questions,  Up: Agent Shell

11.1 Start and control a session
================================

Load Emacsvox and start Agent Shell with ‘M-x agent-shell’.  Emacsvox
loads its integration after Agent Shell and activates both new and
already existing shell buffers.  Run ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable’
only when manually re-enabling support after it was disabled or while
diagnosing a live reload.

11.1.1 Write and edit prompts
-----------------------------

Compose and submit prompts with Agent Shell's normal editing commands.
The live prompt retains Emacs undo.  Interactive text yanks speak the
inserted text, including when a prefix argument selects a different
kill-ring entry.  Image attachment keeps Agent Shell's existing
feedback.  Submitted transcript text is history rather than editable
prompt input.  Press ‘C-c C-c’ to ask Agent Shell to interrupt an active
request; its normal confirmation setting still applies.

   With Agent Shell's persistent prompt enabled, you can keep typing
while the agent works.  Brackets and navigation letters remain ordinary
input at that prompt.  Reading the live prompt says “Agent busy” while
the agent works.  A pending permission is announced as “Waiting for
permission”; a pending question says “Waiting for an answer.” These cues
also precede the first line of a draft; later lines keep ordinary
reading feedback.  “Ready for input” is reserved for an empty prompt
when the agent is idle.

   Quoting a selected region, sending source context, dropping files,
and inserting shell-command output can add to that live draft while the
agent works.  These insertions do not submit the draft.  If there is no
live prompt while busy, Agent Shell offers its queue editor instead.
Use ‘M-x yank-media’ to attach a clipboard image on a display that
supports native clipboard access.  Image previews survive queueing and
steering.  The animated busy marker does not add repeated speech;
reading the prompt continues to give its current state.

   In the viewport prompt editor, continuing a list announces its bullet
or next number and indentation.  Indenting and dedenting announce the
new indentation; leaving an empty list item announces that the list
ended.

11.1.2 Follow a response and steer a running turn
-------------------------------------------------

Emacsvox speaks routine content only at complete turn boundaries.  A
network pause does not make a partial answer sound complete.  A
cancelled, refused, or failed turn reports its outcome without replacing
the last successfully completed response.

   In the viewport editor, Emacsvox announces whether a prompt was
submitted, queued, or sent for steering, and whether the editor stays
open.  The normal and override send commands follow Agent Shell's
configured busy routes, including queueing when the provider does not
support steering.  “Steering sent” confirms dispatch; the agent's
acceptance or refusal follows separately.

   When you use ‘M-x agent-shell-prompt-steer’, Emacsvox confirms
whether the agent accepted the steer, declined it, or started a separate
turn.  A separate turn is announced as still in progress; it is not
reported as complete until Agent Shell supplies a completion boundary.
Server-pushed turns, when supported by the provider, follow the same
completed-response speech policy.

11.1.3 Work with queued prompts
-------------------------------

Queued prompts announce when they start and how many remain.  Recent
Agent Shell versions merge queued prompts into one turn by default;
Emacsvox announces how many prompts started together.  Set
‘agent-shell-prompt-queue-merge’ to nil to send them one turn at a time.
After cancellation, Agent Shell asks whether to continue with the queue.
If you decline, or a failed turn leaves prompts waiting, the queue
announcement gives its count and the command ‘M-x
agent-shell-prompt-queue-resume’.  Queue-start feedback follows the
session's speech level; paused-queue warnings follow
‘emacsvox-agent-shell-signal-processing’.  Agent Shell also asks before
killing a shell or exiting Emacs when queued prompts would be lost.
Declining that question keeps the prompts available.

11.1.4 Check session settings and status
----------------------------------------

Model, mode, thought-level, and other session setting changes announce
their confirmed values.  Background setting announcements identify the
session.  When grouped choices share a name, confirmation includes the
group shown by Agent Shell's completion menu.

   Use ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-header’ to hear the full session
header.  When ‘agent-shell-show-cost-indicator’ is enabled and the
provider reports a positive cost, this includes the cumulative cost with
Agent Shell's currency and rounding.  Brief announcements when entering
a session stay concise.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Answering Agent Questions,  Next: Agent Shell Speech And Privacy,  Prev: Starting An Agent Shell Session,  Up: Agent Shell

11.2 Answer an agent's question
===============================

Some providers ask you to complete a structured form rather than answer
in ordinary prompt text.  Agent Shell's form support is experimental and
off by default.  To try it, set
‘agent-shell-elicitation--experimental-feature-enabled’ to non-nil after
loading Agent Shell and start a new session so the agent learns that
forms are supported.  This upstream experimental setting may change.

   When a form arrives, Emacsvox announces that an answer is needed and
reads the question.  Automatic question feedback follows the session's
speech level; ‘quiet’ suppresses it.  Explicit reading and actions still
speak.

   Use ‘<TAB>’ and ‘S-<TAB>’ to move between controls in the shell or
viewport.  Emacsvox reads the field name, whether it is required, and
its current value or selection state.  Press ‘<RET>’ to select an
option, toggle a checkbox, or edit a text or number field.  Changes are
confirmed after they take effect.  When an option has a preview, ‘?’
opens or closes it; opening it reads its contents.  The preview key
applies only to controls that offer a preview.

   Choose _Submit_ to send the answers or _Decline_ to decline the
question.  Missing required answers are reported without announcing
successful submission.  ‘C-c C-c’ interrupts the running request,
including any pending form.  To return to the latest question, use ‘M-x
agent-shell-elicitation-jump-to-latest-form’.  The next-field and
previous-field commands are also available through ‘M-x’.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Speech And Privacy,  Next: Agent Shell Permissions,  Prev: Answering Agent Questions,  Up: Agent Shell

11.3 Choose automatic speech and privacy
========================================

The focused session defaults to ‘response’: completed answers and
routine completion feedback are presented, while thought, plan, and tool
chatter is suppressed.  An unfocused session defaults to ‘notify’: its
notification names the session but does not disclose response or tool
content.

‘full’
     Speak responses plus configured thought, plan, and tool detail.
     Full tool output can contain commands, file contents, or other
     sensitive provider data.
‘response’
     Speak completed agent responses while suppressing routine thought,
     plan, and tool content.
‘notify’
     Present routine completion without reading the response body.
‘quiet’
     Suppress routine automatic feedback.  Explicit inspection remains
     available.
‘auto’
     Use ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-foreground-speech-level’ or
     ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-background-speech-level’ according to focus.

   Press ‘C-c C-q’ to run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-select-speech-level’ and
choose the current session's level, including ‘auto’.  Press ‘C-c C-S-q’
to run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-select-background-speech-level’ and change
the shared background default.  ‘M-x
emacsvox-agent-shell-cycle-speech-level’ cycles toward less routine
speech; use a prefix argument to restore ‘auto’.

11.3.1 Adjust speech in the settings menu
-----------------------------------------

Press ‘C-e S’ in a shell or its viewport for focused Emacsvox settings.
The same menu is available through ‘C-e H j c’ and the integration
chooser.  ‘s’ opens choices for the temporary session override.  ‘f’ and
‘b’ open foreground and background defaults; ‘t’ and ‘o’ open thought
and tool-output choices.  Each list starts on the current value and
explains what the choices do.  Browse with arrows, press Enter to apply
and return, or ‘q’ to cancel.  Press ‘?’ followed by a choice's key for
spoken Help about its effect and scope; ‘q’ returns from Help.  ‘? ?’
opens the menu overview.

   The ‘p’, ‘c’ and ‘l’ controls toggle permission speech, tool-call
announcements and processing cues.  Thought and tool content still
depend on the effective speech level.

   The ‘a’, ‘n’ and ‘k’ controls set automatic-content, line-navigation
and block-navigation character limits.  Enter reads an exact positive
integer or ‘none’; Shift-left/right adjusts by 100.  For line
navigation, ‘none’ restores the core long-line policy.  For the other
limits it allows complete content.  ‘w’ saves the displayed global
defaults through Customize, excluding the session override.  ‘A’ opens
advanced settings; ‘q’ returns.  From the direct ‘C-e S’ entry, ‘q’
closes the menu and leaves you in the captured buffer.

   Thought content defaults to an icon under
‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-thought-process’.  Tool feedback is
separately controlled by ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-tool-calls’ and
‘emacsvox-agent-shell-tool-output-verbosity’.  Selecting background
‘response’ or ‘full’, thought ‘speak’, or full tool output is an
explicit disclosure choice.

   At ‘full’ speech level, tool verbosity ‘full’ reads the tool's
rendered output, including Codex command output and textual diffs.
Changed output can be read even when the tool's status has not changed;
identical updates are suppressed.  Tool verbosity ‘status’ plays status
cues as tools start and finish.  Tool updates, steering outcomes, queue
changes, and confirmed setting changes use the notification lane,
leaving navigation speech independent.

11.3.2 Understand automatic speech limits
-----------------------------------------

Automatic response, tool, error, permission, and similar text is limited
by ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-automatic-content-max-characters’, which
defaults to 2,000 characters.  A shortened presentation gives exact
omitted character and line counts and tells you where to inspect the
full content.  The complete text rendered by Agent Shell stays in its
buffer; this speech limit is applied before Aural compiles the automatic
speech copy.  Agent Shell separately shortens tool output lines longer
than 32,768 characters, keeping their beginning and end with an
omitted-character count.  Full tool speech reads that notice; the
removed middle is not available by reading the buffer.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Permissions,  Next: Reading Agent Responses,  Prev: Agent Shell Speech And Privacy,  Up: Agent Shell

11.4 Respond to urgent permissions
==================================

Permission requests are separate from routine speech levels because they
can block the agent and require a decision.  With
‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-permissions’ enabled, a request interrupts
the appropriate speech stream, identifies the session and requested
operation, and announces its choices.  Provider-controlled descriptions
and choice labels are bounded, but the actual permission controls remain
in Agent Shell.

   If the provider supplies new operation details after asking
permission, Emacsvox announces the changed description or input.
Identical updates and routine output do not repeat the warning.  The
permission controls remain the place to inspect and decide the request.

   If the request belongs to another session, switch to the named
buffer.  Use ‘M-x agent-shell-jump-to-latest-permission-button-row’ to
find its latest permission row, or use Agent Shell's ‘<TAB>’ and
‘S-<TAB>’ item navigation.  Move among the buttons and press ‘<RET>’, or
use a direct choice key displayed by Agent Shell.  Choice keys are
supplied by Agent Shell and the provider, so the visible permission row
is authoritative.

   Error blocks remain exceptional feedback rather than routine
transcript chatter.  Lifecycle error announcements follow
‘emacsvox-agent-shell-signal-processing’; rendered error content remains
available in the transcript even when routine response speech is quiet.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reading Agent Responses,  Next: Agent Shell Transcript Navigation,  Prev: Agent Shell Permissions,  Up: Agent Shell

11.5 Inspect a response on demand
=================================

These commands work from the shell and its associated viewports:

‘C-c r’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-last-response’.  It reads the
     latest successfully completed response in full without moving
     point.  This deliberate operation does not inherit the automatic
     2,000-character limit.  While a new turn is busy it explicitly
     identifies and reads the prior completed response.
‘C-c R’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-response-overview’.  It reports
     line, heading, source-block, and table counts plus a bounded
     opening preview.

   These commands remain usable at ‘notify’ and ‘quiet’.  If no turn has
completed, they say that no completed response is available rather than
presenting a streaming partial or failed answer.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Transcript Navigation,  Next: Agent Shell Source Blocks,  Prev: Reading Agent Responses,  Up: Agent Shell

11.6 Navigate transcript blocks
===============================

Agent Shell responses contain semantic prompt, response, thought,
activity, tool, plan, permission, error, source-block, and table
locations.  Emacsvox navigation reports the block type, visibility, and
a bounded body preview.

   Up and Down play the ellipses cue when you enter a collapsed heading,
such as Capabilities, Models, or Notices.  Wrapped continuation rows do
not repeat the cue; leaving and returning to the heading plays it again.
The optional block visibility cue replaces this default cue.  Reading
the current line with ‘C-e l’ continues to indicate hidden content.

‘h / H’
     Use ‘h’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-heading’) or ‘H’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-heading’).  Move to the next or
     previous visible Markdown heading and speak its text.
‘k / K’
     Use ‘k’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-source-block’) or ‘K’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-source-block’).  Move to the next
     or previous source block and announce its language and line count.
     Inside a table, lowercase ‘k’ remains the table copy prefix; use
     ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-next-source-block’ to jump forward to
     code from there.
‘u / U’
     Use ‘u’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-user-prompt’) or ‘U’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-user-prompt’).  Move to the next or
     previous submitted user prompt, skipping the live editor.
‘j / J’
     Use ‘j’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-response’) or ‘J’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-response’).  Move to the next or
     previous agent response and speak its bounded body preview.  These
     keys skip intervening thoughts, tools, and other block types.  From
     code or a table inside an answer, they skip that enclosing answer.
‘l / L’
     Use ‘l’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-link’) or ‘L’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-link’).  Move to the next or
     previous visible rendered link and speak its label and Return
     action together.  The cursor hint remains visible without a second
     voice repeating it.  Press ‘<RET>’ to follow the link.
‘e / E’
     Use ‘e’ (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-error’) or ‘E’
     (‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-error’).  Move to the next or
     previous error or failed tool call.  Failed tools are identified by
     Agent Shell's error styling in their status label, rather than by
     words in their output.
‘]’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-at-point’ to find the next
     block of the current semantic type.
‘[’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-at-point’ to find the
     preceding block of the current semantic type.
‘C-c ]’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-of-type’, choose a type, and
     move to its next occurrence.
‘C-c [’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-of-type’, choose a type,
     and move to its preceding occurrence.

   After a successful typed jump, ‘]’ and ‘[’ repeat in that direction.
At an editable live prompt or viewport editor, bare brackets and
navigation letters insert themselves instead of taking over prompt
editing.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Source Blocks,  Next: Agent Shell Tables,  Prev: Agent Shell Transcript Navigation,  Up: Agent Shell

11.7 Read and copy source blocks
================================

Use ‘k’ or ‘K’, or typed block navigation, to reach a source block,
then:

‘C-c C-b’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-source-block’ to read the complete
     rendered source block with its language and line count.
‘C-c C-y’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-copy-source-block’ to copy the logical
     source through Agent Shell's public copy operation.

   The source commands are explicit operations and do not use the
automatic content limit.  Ordinary line navigation retains its separate
bounded speech policy.

11.7.1 Review proposed changes
------------------------------

Opening an Agent Shell change review announces its file and hunk counts
and, for a single file, its name.  The normal Diff Mode hunk navigation
remains available; opening the review does not accept or reject changes.

   These interactions expose semantic events and attributes to Aural.
You can customize speech, voices, and earcons for steering outcomes,
queue changes, list editing, setting changes, updated tool output, and
change-review entry.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Tables,  Next: Agent Shell Recovery,  Prev: Agent Shell Source Blocks,  Up: Agent Shell

11.8 Navigate and copy rendered tables
======================================

Agent Shell gives rendered Markdown tables their own navigation
commands.  Moving into a table activates a contextual table map.  It
announces the table dimensions and current cell.  Leaving the table
restores the ordinary Agent Shell keys.  Markdown and Org source buffers
offer explicit table reading with matching logical operations; see *note
Browsing Structure: Browsing Structure.  Agent Shell retains its own
table speech preferences and automatic contextual activation.  Plain
Left and Right, or ‘C-b’ and ‘C-f’, move by character within the current
cell.  ‘M-b’ and ‘M-f’, or Control-Left and Control-Right, move by word.
These commands follow wrapped text within the same cell, skipping
borders, padding, and neighbouring columns.  At the beginning or end of
the cell, they stop and announce the boundary.  Use Control-Meta-Left
and Control-Meta-Right to move between columns.  Control-Meta-Up and
Control-Meta-Down move between logical rows in the current column and
read only the destination cell.  Plain Up and Down make the same move
and read the entire destination row.  Wrapped cells are read in full,
using your table title settings.

   ‘C-n’ and ‘C-p’ retain ordinary line movement.  They can move through
wrapped cell text, but do not stay within the logical cell or skip other
columns.  Use character and word movement to follow the cell's text in
order.

   Outside editable input, press ‘t’ for the next table or ‘T’ for the
previous table.  These keys skip the current table and announce the
destination table and cell.  Like the bracket navigation keys, they
insert themselves in a live prompt or viewport editor, including while
the agent is working.

11.8.1 Understand table key conflicts
-------------------------------------

In viewport view mode, ‘t’ takes the place of Agent Shell's
thought-level shortcut; use ‘M-x
agent-shell-viewport-set-session-thought-level’ for that action.  Inside
a table, ‘r’, ‘c’, and ‘a’ read rows, read columns, and select the table
speaking method.  To invoke the displaced viewport actions there, use
‘M-x agent-shell-viewport-reply’, ‘M-x
agent-shell-viewport-reply-continue’, or ‘M-x
agent-shell-viewport-reply-again’.  In the shell transcript, table ‘r’
replaces quoting; use ‘M-x agent-shell-quote-region’ with a selected
region.  Leaving the table restores these ordinary bindings.

   Outside tables, ‘C-M-<up>’ and ‘C-M-<down>’ do nothing in Agent
Shell, including its viewport and prompt editor.  Repeating a row key
after leaving a table therefore keeps point at the exit.  These two keys
also do nothing in ordinary ‘shell-mode’ buffers.  For structural
movement there, use ‘M-x backward-up-list’ or ‘M-x down-list’.
Source-code buffers keep their usual bindings.

   Continuous speech and response-block reading omit rendered table
borders and keep the cell text.  Decorative borders therefore do not
force a switch to a different speech engine.  Literal box-drawing
characters in prose or code remain available to symbol naming and exact
character inspection.

11.8.2 Table command reference
------------------------------

‘t’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-next-table’ outside editable input.
‘T’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-table’ outside editable input.
‘C-M-<right>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-next-column’.
‘C-M-<left>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-previous-column’.
‘<down>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-next-row’ and read the destination
     row.
‘<up>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-previous-row’ and read the
     destination row.
‘C-M-<down>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-next-row-cell’ and read the
     destination cell.
‘C-M-<up>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-previous-row-cell’ and read the
     destination cell.
‘M-<down>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-exit-forward’.
‘M-<up>’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-exit-backward’.
‘SPC’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-cell’.
‘r’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-row’.
‘c’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-column’.
‘.’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-context’ for row, column, and
     table position.
‘=’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-speak-dimensions’.
‘w’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-cell’.
‘k k’
     Also run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-cell’.
‘k r’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-row’; copied cells are
     tab-separated.
‘k c’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-copy-column’; copied cells are
     separated by newlines.
‘a’
     Run ‘emacsvox-agent-shell-table-select-speaking-method’ to toggle
     column titles, row titles, or whether data is spoken before titles.

   Copy commands use logical cell values, excluding renderer padding and
Markdown borders.  Wrapped cell contents remain complete.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Agent Shell Recovery,  Prev: Agent Shell Tables,  Up: Agent Shell

11.9 Recover from interruption or failure
=========================================

If a request is taking the wrong path, press ‘C-c C-c’ and confirm the
Agent Shell interrupt.  A failed or interrupted partial response is not
cached as complete.  Press ‘C-c r’ for the preceding completed response,
or ‘C-c R’ for its overview.  Read the error block in the transcript for
the provider's details and edit the live prompt before resubmitting.

   Use ‘M-x agent-shell-restart’ when Agent Shell itself needs a fresh
process for the same project.  Use ‘M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable’ to
reinstall speech hooks after deliberately disabling them.  Neither
recovery command discards transcript files or project work.

   If you are changing the integration rather than using it, see *note
Integration Maintenance: Integration Maintenance. for its public event,
renderer, and compatibility contracts.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Terminal Access,  Next: Running Terminal Based Applications,  Prev: Agent Shell,  Up: Top

12 Speech Access To EAT
***********************

EAT (Emulate A Terminal) is a third-party terminal emulator, commonly
installed from NonGNU ELPA.  Run ‘M-x eat’ to start it; a prefix
argument creates or selects another terminal.  Follow the *note Eat User
Manual: (eat)Top. for installation, terminal setup, and EAT's complete
command reference.

   Three parts cooperate here.  EAT emulates the terminal; the shell,
router, or other program handles terminal input and completion; Emacsvox
presents the rendered result.  Emacsvox observes a bounded screen, not
the program's private state.  It does not generate shell or router
completion candidates.

   First choose an input mode appropriate to the program.  Then use
automatic feedback for current work and explicit review for content you
need to examine again.  ‘C-e S’ opens focused EAT speech settings; those
menu keys apply only while the menu is open, not to ordinary terminal
input.

* Menu:

* Choose an input mode: EAT Input Modes.  Decide which keys reach Emacs and which reach the terminal program.
* Understand automatic terminal feedback: EAT Automatic Feedback.  Follow output, prompts, editing results, and application-screen changes.
* Hear terminal-side completion: EAT Terminal Completion.  Hear completed input and inspect retained candidate or help rows.
* Review retained terminal output: EAT Screen Review.  Read retained state or inspect an immutable screen without sending input.
* Adjust and save terminal feedback: EAT Feedback Preferences.  Choose verbosity, background monitoring, and focused speech defaults.
* Follow shell prompts and command status: EAT Shell Integration.  Understand the Bash or Zsh integration and its remote-shell limits.
* Enter passwords and protect sensitive content: EAT Passwords And Privacy.  Use protected password input and understand retained terminal data.
* Control terminal access to the clipboard: EAT Clipboard Trust.  Review OSC 52 trust and the two terminal selection-control options.
* Inspect full-screen applications and diagnose silence: EAT Full-screen Applications.  Use retained review and distinguish terminal rendering from speech.
* Use EAT in Eshell: EAT In Eshell.  Understand embedded terminals and visual-command window return.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Input Modes,  Next: EAT Automatic Feedback,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.1 Input Modes And Completion Responsibilities
================================================

EAT has four input modes.  Emacsvox announces the mode name when you
switch.  The mode determines whether a key edits text in Emacs or
reaches the terminal program.

Semi-character mode
     The default.  Most keys, including ‘<TAB>’, go to the terminal,
     while prefixes such as ‘C-x’, ‘C-c’, and ‘M-x’ remain available.
     Use ‘C-q’ before a normally reserved key to send it through.  ‘C-c
     C-j’ selects this mode.
Character mode
     All supported keys go to the terminal, useful for programs needing
     Emacs-like keys.  Enter with ‘C-c M-d’ and return to semi-character
     mode with ‘C-M-m’ or ‘M-<RET>’.
Emacs mode
     Keys stay in Emacs and send no terminal input.  Use it for ordinary
     buffer motion and inspection.  Enter with ‘C-c C-e’.
Line mode
     Edit the pending line locally and send it with ‘<RET>’.  ‘<TAB>’
     invokes Emacs's ‘completion-at-point’, whose result may be
     displayed by a front end such as Corfu.  Enter from semi-character
     or Emacs mode with ‘C-c C-l’.  Line mode is not available in EAT's
     embedded Eshell terminal.

   Line mode does not create unlimited history.
‘eat-term-scrollback-size’ still limits retained text by _characters_,
not lines; ‘nil’ permits unlimited retention.  The editable draft
follows the terminal extent, and changing input modes does not change
this limit.

   In semi-character and character modes, ‘<TAB>’ belongs to Bash
Readline, a remote shell, router CLI, or the running terminal program.
Corfu is not involved.  In line mode, completion belongs to Emacs and
does not query a remote CLI.  Semi-character mode is therefore usually
appropriate for SSH and network-device sessions.  If an Emacs completion
backend blocks in line mode, ‘C-g’ cancels that Emacs operation; switch
to semi-character mode when terminal completion was intended.

   The shell must have its own completion configuration.  For example,
completing ‘git pul’ to ‘git pull’ in Bash requires suitable
programmable definitions, often supplied by ‘bash-completion’.  Emacsvox
neither installs nor substitutes for those definitions.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Automatic Feedback,  Next: EAT Terminal Completion,  Prev: EAT Input Modes,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.2 Automatic Terminal Feedback
================================

‘C-e C-q’ toggles automatic foreground feedback for this terminal; it
starts enabled.  The same control is ‘s’ in the focused settings menu.
It covers routine output, status, automatic metadata, application-screen
boundaries, and process lifecycle feedback.  Explicit reading, input and
completion feedback, and the configured terminal bell remain available.
Verbosity (‘C-e q v’) and background monitoring (‘C-e q m’) are
independent preferences.

   Automatic speech requires the selected EAT buffer and Emacs keyboard
focus.  The selected window must follow the live cursor or edit a
line-mode draft after terminal output.  Reviewing scrollback prevents
new output from interrupting you.  Another selected terminal or an
active minibuffer also suppresses foreground feedback.  In terminal-only
Emacs, or when keyboard focus cannot be reported, selection within Emacs
is the available boundary.

   Turning autospeak off or losing eligibility discards locally pending
automatic feedback.  Speech already sent to Omnivox may finish.
Returning or re-enabling speech starts with fresh output; it does not
replay missed content.  Use explicit review instead.  This policy also
applies to an embedded Eshell terminal, without changing ordinary Eshell
output.

12.2.1 Read output and follow progress
--------------------------------------

On the main screen, Emacsvox collects a burst before classifying it.
Complete new output lines are spoken once, with automatic limits of
eight lines and 1,000 characters.  Continued input rows and empty
terminal placeholders are omitted without hiding output or rereading
earlier rows.  Output remains eligible even if it scrolls beyond the
screen, including repeated identical lines; increasing scrollback is
unnecessary for this feedback.

   Wrapped output is joined and oversized bursts announce how much was
omitted.  Fresh text without a newline also speaks, keeping password
requests and confirmation questions accessible.  Carriage-return
progress and status rewrites use rate-limited, replaceable speech.
Unchanged repaints and resize-only updates remain silent.  Bells,
pastes, input-mode changes, application-screen boundaries, and process
exit are content-free semantic events rather than arbitrary screen
readings.

12.2.2 Recognise and inspect prompts
------------------------------------

When EAT shell integration identifies a ready prompt, Emacsvox gives a
cue after output instead of reading the prompt text.
‘emacsvox-eat-prompt-feedback’ defaults to ‘earcon’; choose ‘text’ to
hear the prompt or ‘silent’ to suppress it.  This option can be
buffer-local and is independent of verbosity, but automatic prompt
feedback still obeys foreground autospeak eligibility.

   Split and multiline prompts are collected until ready.  Redisplay and
typing do not repeat them.  If several prompts arrive in one burst,
feedback describes the final ready prompt.

   ‘C-e q p’ explicitly reads the last identified prompt, even with
autospeak off.  Recognition does not require margin annotations, but it
does require shell integration.  An unintegrated remote prompt or
question is ordinary output, read as text; the prompt preference does
not silence it.  The last identified prompt may still belong to the
local shell.  Use ‘C-e q l’ for the current retained cursor row in that
situation.

12.2.3 Hear input and deletion results
--------------------------------------

On the main screen in semi-character and character modes, ‘<RET>’ is
silent.  After rendered left/right movement, Emacsvox names the reached
character; after up/down it speaks the resulting input or history row
without the observed shell prompt.  ‘M-b’, ‘M-f’, and modified
left/right word movements speak the rendered text crossed.  A boundary
key that produces no movement stays silent.

   Line-mode history navigation speaks the recalled command once.  EAT's
history-item counter and input-restored message remain visible in the
echo area without speaking over the command on the notification stream.

   An echoed Space is named without rereading the prompt or line.  For
Backspace, Delete, ‘M-d’, and ‘C-k’, Emacsvox compares bounded public
snapshots before and after the key.  One unambiguous same-row removal is
read as the actual character or text removed.  Otherwise only a deletion
tone is given; adjacent prompt or screen text is not guessed as deleted
content.  Padding and trailing input blanks do not prevent an otherwise
unambiguous word deletion.

   A no-echo peer provides no visible deletion to announce.  This input
feedback requires the selected window to follow the live cursor, and is
silent during protected password entry, in an unselected terminal, and
on an alternate screen where the application owns the keys.

12.2.4 Bells and application-screen changes
-------------------------------------------

Bells retain EAT's configured sound without speaking "Terminal bell" by
default.  An aural rule can add a label by matching module ‘eat’, role
‘command-interaction’, event ‘command-terminal-bell’, and occasion
‘notification’, with speech before or after the event.  Bell
notifications remain rate-limited and obey selected-buffer, live-cursor,
and secure-input restrictions.

   On full-screen or alternate-screen applications, Emacsvox does not
read every refresh.  A cursor row reached by correlated arrow-style
navigation, or a compact high-confidence transfer of selection-like
styling, may be announced.  Ambiguous repaints remain available to
explicit review.  Inverse video or a coloured row alone is not proof of
an active item.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Terminal Completion,  Next: EAT Screen Review,  Prev: EAT Automatic Feedback,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.3 Terminal-side Completion
=============================

Before a terminal-side ‘<TAB>’ on the main screen, Emacsvox captures the
public screen and waits briefly for output to settle.  An inline
completion speaks the newly displayed field; for example, ‘~/sr<TAB>’
can speak ‘src slash’ after the shell displays ‘~/src/’.

   An ambiguous completion printed as columns or help rows is presented
as one transaction after the input line is redrawn.  Small inferred
lists speak up to eight candidates.  Router-style descriptions keep
their layout rather than being forced into a shell-token model.  Large
lists are counted and shortened for automatic speech.  If candidates
have already scrolled beyond the bounded screen, the count is a lower
bound, such as "at least N visible", not an invented total.

   Identical repeated lists, split output, bells, wrapping, and resizing
are coalesced without repeating the candidate text.  This works from
rendered results for local Readline, SSH, and unstructured router help.
No visible change means there is nothing reliable to announce.  On an
alternate screen, ‘<TAB>’ is navigation rather than shell completion.

   Use ‘C-e q c’ to read the retained candidate/help layout, or open
frozen review with ‘C-e q r’ and press ‘c’.  This is useful for large or
multi-column results and does not ask the terminal to complete again.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Screen Review,  Next: EAT Feedback Preferences,  Prev: EAT Terminal Completion,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.4 Explicit Review Commands
=============================

With the default speech prefix, ‘C-e q’ provides review commands in
every EAT input mode:

‘C-e q l’
     Speak the cursor row from the latest retained screen.
‘C-e q d’
     Speak the last classified change.
‘C-e q h’
     Speak the conservative likely-focus inference and its confidence.
‘C-e q c’
     Speak retained completion candidates or help rows.
‘C-e q s’
     Speak a bounded copy of the retained visible screen.
‘C-e q r’
     Open a read-only frozen copy for line-by-line review.
‘C-e q t’
     Speak retained progress, application, or shell-prompt status.
‘C-e q p’
     Speak the last identified shell prompt.
‘C-e q i’
     Speak the retained, sanitised title or working-directory change.
‘C-e q v’
     Cycle terse, normal, and verbose automatic feedback for this
     terminal.
‘C-e q m’
     Toggle content-free background monitoring for this terminal.

   These inspect retained snapshots with concealed content redacted;
they do not send terminal input.  Speech is bounded.  The retained
visible screen is not arbitrary scrollback or a promise that it still
matches a changing live screen.

12.4.1 Frozen Screen Review
---------------------------

‘C-e q r’ opens an immutable copy of the latest visible screen.  It does
not refresh, move the live cursor, or send input.  It preserves
normalised styling, cursor and likely-focus anchors, and retained
completion rows.  Opening and closing play distinct object cues and
announce the transition.

‘n, p; C-n, C-p; down, up’
     Move by captured terminal row and speak the destination,
     interrupting the previous row announcement.
‘SPC, RET, .’
     Speak the current captured row.
‘a’
     Speak a bounded form of the current screen or completion view.
‘s, c’
     Show captured screen rows or retained completion/help rows.
‘C’
     Go to the captured terminal cursor.
‘h’
     Go to retained likely focus when available.
‘[, ]’
     Move among inverse/background-styled, selection-like regions.  They
     are not labelled as confirmed focus.
‘t, i’
     Speak status or metadata copied when review opened.
‘v, m’
     Change verbosity or monitoring in the source terminal without
     sending terminal input.  The copied content stays frozen.
‘?’
     Describe frozen review mode.
‘q’
     Kill the content-bearing review buffer and restore its window.

   Secure input, terminal reset or restart, killing the source, or
leaving EAT mode also destroys its frozen review.  These events bound
the lifetime of copied terminal content.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Feedback Preferences,  Next: EAT Shell Integration,  Prev: EAT Screen Review,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.5 Verbosity And Background Monitoring
========================================

‘emacsvox-eat-verbosity’ is buffer-local:

‘terse’
     Retain routine output and metadata for explicit review without
     speaking them automatically.  Actionable failures and content-free
     lifecycle feedback remain available.
‘normal’
     The default: bounded command output, progress/status, completion,
     and actionable failures.
‘verbose’
     Also give bounded title and directory changes and successful
     shell-command status when EAT shell integration supplies it.

   Background monitoring starts off.  With ‘C-e q m’ enabled, an
unselected terminal gives only a rate-limited content-free cue.
Returning reports the unread burst count, never the terminal's identity
or output.  Cursor movement, style changes, repaint, resize, and
process-generation changes do not count.  Unlike Shell/comint background
reading, EAT monitoring neither requires foreground autospeak nor reads
background content.

12.5.1 Adjust and save focused terminal settings
------------------------------------------------

Use ‘C-e S’ or ‘C-e H j c’ from EAT or its frozen review.  ‘C-e H j a’
opens the integration chooser, and ‘C-e H /’ finds individual controls.

   • ‘s’ toggles automatic foreground speech.
   • ‘b’ toggles background cues and unread counts independently.
   • ‘p’ chooses ready-prompt cue, text, or silence; recognition still
     requires shell integration and automatic delivery obeys foreground
     autospeak.
   • ‘d’ chooses terse, normal, or verbose feedback.

   Arrows announce values and ‘<RET>’ activates a control.  Choice lists
start on the current value; browsing does not apply a choice, ‘<RET>’
applies and returns, and ‘q’ cancels.  Confirmations interrupt older
main speech.  Changes belong to the captured terminal, including
embedded Eshell EAT; from frozen review they belong to its source
terminal.

   ‘q’ returns to the originating menu entry.  From direct ‘C-e S’, it
closes the menu and announces the restored buffer and mode with a close
cue.  ‘w’ saves the four displayed values as defaults through Customize.
Terminals with local overrides keep them; others inherit the new
defaults.  Adjustment alone does not save.  ‘A’ opens advanced EAT
settings and ‘q’ there returns to the focused menu.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Shell Integration,  Next: EAT Passwords And Privacy,  Prev: EAT Feedback Preferences,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.6 Shell Integration And Command Status
=========================================

EAT's Bash or Zsh integration supplies prompt annotation, directory
tracking, and per-command running/success/failure status.  For Bash, EAT
documents:

     [ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
       source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/bash"

   For Zsh, use its script instead:

     [ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
       source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/zsh"

   With integration and ‘eat-enable-shell-prompt-annotation’ enabled,
Emacsvox retains running, successful, and failed command status.  A
failure is eligible for automatic feedback at every verbosity, subject
to foreground autospeak.  Success is automatic only at verbose level
after observed command activity; the initial successful prompt is not
announced as a completed command.  Running state stays silent.  ‘C-e q
t’ requests the retained current state.

   Status is not guessed from prompt characters.  A remote shell or
router usually does not provide this per-command integration.  Its
output and completion can still be accessible while individual exit
status remains unavailable.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Passwords And Privacy,  Next: EAT Clipboard Trust,  Prev: EAT Shell Integration,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.7 Passwords And Sensitive Content
====================================

Use ‘M-x eat-send-password’ for a terminal password.  EAT uses Emacs's
protected password reader rather than recording those keystrokes in
command lossage.  Emacsvox suppresses observation and bell speech during
the command, clears content-bearing retained state before and after it,
and announces only whether secure input was sent or cancelled.

   Ordinary no-echo input does not establish that boundary.  EAT exposes
no public echo-state signal, and a quiet repaint is not evidence of
password entry.  Typing a secret through normal terminal input can leave
keystrokes in Emacs lossage.  Backspace or Delete may give a
content-free deletion tone, but speech names content only when one
unambiguous visible removal is exposed.

   Selected foreground output can contain sensitive information and be
spoken.  Frozen review is a real in-memory copy until killed, although
visually concealed cells are redacted when captured.  Verbose title and
directory announcements can disclose host/path metadata.  Background
monitoring, by contrast, never reads content or terminal identity.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Clipboard Trust,  Next: EAT Full-screen Applications,  Prev: EAT Passwords And Privacy,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.8 Terminal-Controlled Clipboard Trust
========================================

EAT supports terminal selection-control sequences, commonly called OSC
52.  Trust therefore extends to any local program, SSH peer, router, or
other output source that reaches its parser.  Displaying an untrusted
file through a program that does not sanitise control sequences can also
exercise this interface.

   In the audited EAT 0.9.4 release, ‘eat-enable-kill-from-terminal’
defaults to non-‘nil’.  Despite its name, this is the terminal-to-Emacs
direction: a terminal program can place text in the kill ring.
‘eat-enable-yank-to-terminal’ defaults to ‘nil’ for security; enabling
it lets a terminal program request current kill-ring text.  Check the
installed defaults again after an EAT upgrade.

   For output you do not fully trust, disable both directions globally
through Customize or locally in the EAT buffer:

     (setq-local eat-enable-kill-from-terminal nil
                 eat-enable-yank-to-terminal nil)

   Evaluate this with ‘M-:’ in that buffer before processing untrusted
output.  Reserve kill-ring read-back for programs and remote endpoints
trusted with whatever the kill ring currently contains.

   Emacsvox changes neither option.  A terminal-originated selection
payload is not an accessibility command: its contents are neither spoken
nor retained as terminal screen text.  Content-free paste announcements
apply to explicit user paste commands, not these terminal-originated
sequences.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT Full-screen Applications,  Next: EAT In Eshell,  Prev: EAT Clipboard Trust,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.9 Full-screen Applications And Limitations
=============================================

Pagers, editors, menus, and refreshing dashboards often rewrite cells
instead of printing lines.  Emacsvox uses conservative cursor,
style-change, input- direction, and timing evidence.  Arrow movement and
clear paired highlight movement work best.  Printable keys such as ‘h’,
‘j’, ‘k’, and ‘l’ are not assumed to mean navigation: the application
may treat them as text.

   After a silent update, use ‘C-e q l’, ‘C-e q d’, or ‘C-e q h’.  For
systematic inspection, open ‘C-e q r’ and review rows or selection-like
regions.  Rapid dashboards are deliberately not read on every refresh.
EAT's current public API does not expose hyperlink targets, sixel/image
semantics, true application accessibility focus, or exact update causes;
pixels and faces are insufficient to infer them reliably.

   When ordinary output is unexpectedly silent, check the input mode,
selected buffer and live-cursor position, foreground autospeak, and
verbosity before changing recognition settings.  Retained review may
explain what was observed, but it is not a replacement for live output
or an unbounded transcript.

   Remote programs also need compatible terminal information.  Missing
remote terminfo can break cursor addressing and screen layout; speech
classification cannot repair that.  Consult EAT's manual when the
terminal itself renders incorrectly.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: EAT In Eshell,  Prev: EAT Full-screen Applications,  Up: EAT Terminal Access

12.10 EAT In Eshell
===================

With global ‘eat-eshell-mode’ enabled, Emacsvox uses the same screen
observer for EAT's terminal region inside Eshell.  Rendered command
output follows one speech path instead of also being repeated by generic
Eshell feedback.

   With ‘eat-eshell-visual-command-mode’, a visual command runs in a
separate EAT buffer.  Emacsvox announces its start, delivers eligible
final output before process-exit feedback, and identifies the exit as an
Eshell visual command.  EAT and Eshell still own window return.  In
particular, ‘eshell-destroy-buffer-when-process-dies’ determines whether
a successful visual-command buffer is killed and Eshell restored;
Emacsvox does not change it.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Running Terminal Based Applications,  Next: Speech Backends,  Prev: EAT Terminal Access,  Up: Top

13 Term Terminal Access
***********************

Term is the terminal emulator built into Emacs.  Its Emacsvox
integration is named ‘emacsvox-eterm’ for historical reasons; no
separate Eterm package is required.  Use Term when avoiding an extra
package matters or a particular application works better there.

   For a new full-terminal workflow, *note EAT Terminal Access: EAT
Terminal Access. provides the richer current model for bounded output,
terminal completion, scrollback, alternate screens, and asynchronous
rendering.  For ordinary line-oriented commands, ‘M-x shell’ is another
option.  These environments have different input and review keys; do not
transfer a key sequence merely because both are terminals.

* Menu:

* Starting Term::
* Term Input Modes::
* Term Speech Feedback::
* Reviewing The Term Screen::
* Term Help And Recovery::


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Starting Term,  Next: Term Input Modes,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

13.1 Starting Term
==================

Run ‘M-x term <RET>’.  At ‘Run program:’, accept the proposed shell or
enter another executable and press ‘<RET>’.  Emacsvox makes the selected
Term window fill the frame when ‘term’ or ‘ansi-term’ starts.  This
changes window layout, not the existence of buffers previously shown in
other windows.

   Term emulates the terminal, the child program handles input and
completion, and Emacsvox presents the rendered buffer.  See *note Term
Mode: (emacs)Term Mode. in _The GNU Emacs Manual_ for the emulator and
*note emacsvox-eterm: (emacsvox-reference)emacsvox-eterm. in the
_Emacsvox Reference Manual_ for the complete integration reference.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Term Input Modes,  Next: Term Speech Feedback,  Prev: Starting Term,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

13.2 Character And Line Input Modes
===================================

Term starts in character mode.  Choose according to who should receive
the keys:

Character mode
     Most keys are sent immediately to the terminal program.  This suits
     full-screen or key-driven applications.  ‘C-c C-j’ enters line
     mode.  Normal Emacs prefixes may belong to the child, so use ‘C-c
     C-h m’ for mode help and ‘C-c M-x’ to run an Emacs command.
Line mode
     Ordinary Emacs editing commands edit pending input; ‘<RET>’ sends
     it to the program.  Emacs Help keys such as ‘C-h m’ work directly.
     ‘C-c C-k’ returns to character mode.

   An interactive switch announces ‘Terminal line mode’ or ‘Terminal
character mode’.  In either mode, ‘C-t’ introduces Term's Emacsvox
review commands.  Send a literal ‘C-t’ to the child in character mode
with ‘C-t C-t’.

   Use the input-mode escape before trying ordinary Emacs settings or
Help keys that the child might otherwise receive.  In line mode, ‘C-e H’
opens the Emacsvox settings menu.  Its general settings do not replace
Term's own buffer-local automatic-speech control below.  Likewise, ‘q’
closes an Emacsvox menu or dedicated review only while that interface
owns the keys; it is not a general command to leave a running terminal
application.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Term Speech Feedback,  Next: Reviewing The Term Screen,  Prev: Term Input Modes,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

13.3 Automatic Speech Feedback
==============================

‘emacsvox-eterm-autospeak’ controls automatic feedback in the current
buffer and defaults to enabled.  ‘C-t C-q’ toggles it.  The older
spelling ‘eterm-autospeak’ is not an Emacsvox variable.

   A Term buffer must be displayed in a live window for automatic output
speech.  A hidden buffer can keep running silently.  This
displayed-window condition is not EAT's selected-buffer and
keyboard-focus policy.

   In line mode, speech reads newly inserted output as a range rather
than interrupting it for every character.  In character mode, rendered
cursor and screen changes determine whether a character, word, movement,
or reached line is presented.  New terminal activity can stop earlier
speech.

   This integration does not provide EAT's bounded screen transactions,
completion model, or alternate-screen policy.  When a redraw is
ambiguous or an announcement too brief, use the explicit review
commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Reviewing The Term Screen,  Next: Term Help And Recovery,  Prev: Term Speech Feedback,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

13.4 Reviewing The Term Screen
==============================

The review pointer is independent of the terminal cursor.  Moving it
does not send keys or move the child application.  It reviews the Term
buffer, not the immutable snapshot used by EAT's frozen review.  The
normal prefix is ‘C-t’:

‘C-t .’
     Place the review pointer at the terminal cursor and announce its
     coordinates.
‘C-t p’
‘C-t n’
     Move to the previous or next line and speak it.  ‘C-t <up>’ and
     ‘C-t <down>’ are equivalent.
‘C-t <left>’
‘C-t <right>’
     Move by a character and speak the destination.
‘C-t M-b’
‘C-t M-f’
     Move backward or forward by a word and speak it.
‘C-t l’
‘C-t w’
‘C-t c’
     Read the line, word, or character at the review pointer without
     moving.
‘C-t SPC’
     Read from the pointer to the current end of the Term buffer.
‘C-t s’
     Ask for text, search forward from the pointer, and read the
     matching line.

   In character mode, ‘C-t q’ enters dedicated review.  Ordinary keys
then stay in review rather than reaching the child.  Use ‘p’, ‘n’,
arrows, ‘M-b’, ‘M-f’, ‘l’, ‘w’, ‘c’, and ‘s’ without the prefix.  ‘q’
returns to character mode.  To copy text, set one endpoint with ‘m’,
move the pointer, then press ‘y’ to copy the region to the kill ring.

   Named rectangular regions and focus/filter windows are advanced,
coordinate-based facilities.  Consult the live mode Help and generated
reference before using them; remember the character-mode Help prefix
when ordinary Emacs keys would otherwise reach the child.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Term Help And Recovery,  Prev: Reviewing The Term Screen,  Up: Running Terminal Based Applications

13.5 Help And Recovery
======================

For visible but silent output, inspect ‘emacsvox-eterm-autospeak’ in the
current buffer and ensure that buffer is displayed.  Toggle with ‘C-t
C-q’ when necessary, then test new output.  An automatic-speech setting
does not prove that the program has produced a new readable screen
change.

   If an Emacs command goes to the child, check the input mode.  In
character mode use ‘C-c C-h m’, ‘C-c M-x’, or switch to line mode with
‘C-c C-j’.  If dedicated review owns the keys, ‘q’ returns to character
mode first.

   When review has moved away from current output, ‘C-t .’
resynchronises the pointer.  Leave dedicated review with ‘q’ before
using the normal prefix.  For a full-screen redraw that Term cannot
present reliably, try the workflow in *note EAT Terminal Access: EAT
Terminal Access. and use that emulator's explicit review commands.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech Backends,  Next: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands,  Prev: Running Terminal Based Applications,  Up: Top

14 Speech Backends
******************

Emacsvox sends speech through a speech server.  For a new installation,
use _Omnivox_.  The server communicates with the engine that produces
speech: normally eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux, AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS,
and WinRT SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

   Follow *note Installation: Installation. if you have not yet obtained
speech.  This chapter is for choosing a supported platform route,
installing or configuring a different backend, and diagnosing problems.
Omnivox's native build procedures, engine behaviour, and versioned
protocols belong to the Omnivox repository
(https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox).

   The menu routes here describe ‘ui-experiments’.  Release packages can
provide an earlier interface; use the matching manual and keep a working
installation available while testing the development branch.

* Menu:

* Supported Platform Routes::    Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Install Omnivox From A Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary.  Install Omnivox through Homebrew or a verified release archive.
* Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2.  Use the matching verified Windows archive without a build toolchain.
* Installing Native Windows Emacsvox: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox.  Install and verify supported native Windows x64 Emacsvox.
* Installing Omnivox From Source:: Build when a release archive does not match the host.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::    Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox:: Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers:: Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.  Locate failures and authoritative detail.


