14.1 Supported Platform Routes
==============================

The following table describes Emacsvox support, not every platform that
the Omnivox project can build.  "Host check required" means that
automated build and archive verification does not prove playback through
your audio device; an audible ‘omnivox --check’ on that machine remains
the acceptance test.

Emacs         Recommended route      Current support evidence
platform
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GNU/Linux     Omnivox 1.17.0         The published archive is built and
x86-64 with   release archive        verified on Ubuntu 24.04.
an Ubuntu     using eSpeak NG        Compatible glibc, libstdc++,
24.04-compatible                     libgcc, and ALSA runtime libraries
runtime                              are required; the audible host
                                     check remains required.
Other         Source-built Omnivox   Use the source route for Linux
GNU/Linux     using eSpeak NG        ARM64, an older distribution ABI,
systems                              or a host that cannot run the
                                     published x86-64 archive.
macOS on      Omnivox through the    Native ARM64 tap checks cover
Apple         official Homebrew      installation, synthesis, reinstall,
silicon       tap, using             a packaging-revision upgrade, and
              AVSpeechSynthesizer    removal; audible Emacs acceptance
                                     remains required.
macOS on      Omnivox through the    Native Intel tap checks cover
Intel         official Homebrew      installation, synthesis, reinstall,
              tap, using             a packaging-revision upgrade, and
              AVSpeechSynthesizer    removal; audible Emacs acceptance
                                     remains required.
Emacs in      Omnivox 1.17.0         The published archive is
WSL2 on       Windows x64 release    checksum-protected and release CI
x86-64        archive using WinRT    verifies its architecture, adjacent
Windows                              data, WinRT and eSpeak synthesis.
                                     The audible host check remains
                                     required.
Emacs in      Omnivox 1.17.0         Release CI verifies the ARM64
WSL2 on       Windows ARM64          archive, adjacent data, WinRT and
Windows on    release archive        eSpeak synthesis, and the guided
ARM           using WinRT            installer selects it automatically.
                                     A complete WSL2 host and audible
                                     check remains required.
Native        Guided PowerShell      Supported.  Native installer,
Windows x64   installation with      complete byte-code compilation,
Emacs 30.2    Omnivox 1.17.0 using   fresh-process verification, and
or newer      WinRT                  two-lane speech checks cover this
                                     route.  Confirm audible speech on
                                     the installed host.  Native Windows
                                     ARM64 is not yet supported.

   Use Homebrew on macOS, the guided installer under WSL2, or the
matching release archive on GNU/Linux.  Manual Mac archive installation
remains available.  A bare executable, repository tag, or unpackaged CI
artifact is not a complete installation: keep the release's runtime data
and notices beside the executable.  Homebrew preserves that layout for
you.

   Do not set ‘OMNIVOX_ENGINE’ during initial setup.  Its empty value
selects the normal platform engine above.  Engine routing and optional
Piper, Eloquence, or DECtalk support are later customisations, not
prerequisites for first speech.

   For Emacs running on a remote SSH host, see *note Remote Workstation
Speech: Remote workstation speech setup.

14.1.1 Version and capability requirements
------------------------------------------

Omnivox 1.17.0 is the minimum supported release.  It supplies named
punctuation profiles, palette-aware character speech and editable engine
configuration, alongside managed voice libraries, eSpeak variants,
engine-specific voice controls, individual fallback tuning and complete
voice previews.  A newer compatible release may be used.  The manual
archive examples and guided installers pin the 1.17.0 core and matching
optional engines exactly; the macOS Homebrew tap follows its maintained
stable formula.

   Upgrade an existing Omnivox installation separately; updating
Emacsvox does not replace the selected speech server.  Voice-library
management uses the bundled local management route.  Remote and
direct-native launches do not acquire the WSL launcher's file or
release-management capabilities merely by connecting to an Omnivox
worker.  The individual procedures identify their required host and
capabilities.


