14.2 Install Omnivox From A Release Binary
==========================================

14.2.1 macOS with Homebrew
--------------------------

On macOS (formerly OS X), install Homebrew (https://brew.sh) and follow
its shell setup instructions.  Then install Omnivox from the official
Homebrew tap (https://github.com/bartbunting/homebrew-omnivox):

     brew install bartbunting/omnivox/omnivox
     omnivox_program="$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox"
     "$omnivox_program" --version
     "$omnivox_program" --check
     "$omnivox_program" --list-voices

   Homebrew selects the Apple silicon or Intel archive and verifies its
checksum.  The version must be 1.17.0 or newer, and the check must
produce both a tone and spoken confirmation.  Apple system speech and
eSpeak are included.  You do not need a Rust build or Omnivox source
checkout.  Install Emacs 30.2 or newer separately; this tap does not
install Emacs or Emacsvox.

   To select this Homebrew installation persistently for Emacsvox,
record the stable executable path:

     omnivox_config_home="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}"
     omnivox_config_file="${EMACSVOX_OMNIVOX_CONFIG_FILE:-$omnivox_config_home/emacsvox/omnivox-program}"
     mkdir -p "$(dirname "$omnivox_config_file")"
     printf '%s\n' "$omnivox_program" > "$omnivox_config_file"

   This replaces any previous executable selection in that file.  The
path uses Homebrew's current-version link and continues to work after
upgrades.  Do not substitute a versioned ‘Cellar/omnivox/...’ path.  The
bundled launcher reads this file even when Emacs does not inherit the
shell's Homebrew ‘PATH’, as can happen when starting from Finder.  An
explicit ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ override still takes precedence; remove or
update an override selecting another installation.  If using
‘EMACSVOX_OMNIVOX_CONFIG_FILE’ or ‘XDG_CONFIG_HOME’, make sure the
launching Emacs sees the same location.

   Emacsvox uses its bundled Lisp adapter.  Do not load Homebrew's
‘libexec/omnivox-voices.el’, intended for upstream Emacspeak.  Continue
with *note Quick Installation: Quick Installation. to compile Emacsvox,
run ‘./bin/emacsvox --diagnose’ and ‘./bin/emacsvox --check’, and start
an isolated speaking session.  Confirm the selected server and audible
output on your Mac.

   To upgrade Omnivox later:

     brew update
     brew upgrade omnivox
     "$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox" --version
     "$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox" --check

   Restart Emacsvox after upgrading so both speech processes use the new
executable.  Updating Emacsvox alone does not upgrade Homebrew's
package.  ‘brew uninstall omnivox’ removes it; select another server
before restarting Emacsvox because the saved executable path remains in
your configuration.

   Optional Piper, Flite, and RuTTS companions and voice models are
separate.  The bundled RHVoice bridge still needs a compatible
user-installed runtime and data; its presence does not establish macOS
runtime support.  Keep optional companions, downloaded voices, and
configuration outside Homebrew's Cellar, and use explicit paths as
described in the Omnivox engine guides
(https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/README.md).
Companions must match core release and architecture.  The WSL
optional-engine installer is not a Homebrew package manager.

   The tap installs published binaries without adding Apple Developer ID
signing or notarisation.  If local policy refuses the verified binary,
use *note Installing Omnivox From Source: Installing Omnivox From
Source.  Native tap checks cover WAV synthesis and package operations;
they do not replace listening acceptance in Emacs or prove an upgrade
between different upstream versions.

14.2.2 Manual archive installation
----------------------------------

Use this route for GNU/Linux x86-64 with an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible
runtime, macOS on Apple silicon, or macOS on Intel.  The Omnivox 1.17.0
release (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/tag/v1.17.0)
supplies:

Host                 Set ‘platform’ to   Archive
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
GNU/Linux x86-64     ‘linux-x64’         ‘omnivox-1.17.0-linux-x64.tar.gz’
macOS Apple          ‘macos-arm64’       ‘omnivox-1.17.0-macos-arm64.tar.gz’
silicon
macOS Intel          ‘macos-x64’         ‘omnivox-1.17.0-macos-x64.tar.gz’

   On a Mac, ‘uname -m’ identifies ‘arm64’ for Apple silicon or ‘x86_64’
for Intel.  Create a download directory, select the platform, and fetch
the archive and published checksums:

     mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.17.0"
     cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.17.0"
     omnivox_version=1.17.0
     platform=linux-x64
     archive="omnivox-${omnivox_version}-${platform}.tar.gz"
     release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v${omnivox_version}"
     curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
     curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"

   Replace ‘linux-x64’ with the appropriate Mac value when needed.
Verify before extraction.  On GNU/Linux:

     grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -

   On macOS:

     grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | shasum -a 256 --check

   The command must name the archive and report ‘OK’.  Otherwise remove
the download and do not run it.  Extract the verified payload into a
versioned directory, keeping every supplied directory beside the
executable:

     install_dir="$HOME/.local/opt/omnivox-${omnivox_version}"
     mkdir -p "$install_dir"
     tar -xzf "$archive" -C "$install_dir"
     chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox"
     export PATH="$install_dir:$PATH"

   Add that ‘PATH’ entry to the shell startup used to launch Emacs for
subsequent sessions.  The directory contains ‘omnivox’,
‘espeak-ng-data’, ‘third-party-licenses’, and ‘omnivox-voices.el’.  Do
not load or copy the last file into Emacsvox, which has its own adapter.

   Check the binary before starting Emacsvox:

     omnivox --version
     omnivox --check
     omnivox --list-voices

   The version must be at least 1.17.0 and the check must produce tone
and speech.  Mac archives are neither code-signed nor notarised.  If
security policy refuses the verified binary, use the source route rather
than weakening policy.  On GNU/Linux, missing libraries or incompatible
glibc also call for a source build.

   For WSL2, use *note Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2:
Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2., which verifies a
Windows archive without requiring a compiler or Docker.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2,  Next: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox,  Prev: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary,  Up: Speech Backends

14.3 Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2
==================================================

This is the ordinary WSL2 route for x86-64 Windows and Windows on ARM.
From the Emacsvox checkout, first inspect without changing anything:

     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check

   The doctor detects Windows architecture, selects its Omnivox 1.17.0
archive, and reports Emacs and per-user paths.  Missing Linux packages
are reported with an ‘apt’ command for you to review and run.  Working
‘powershell.exe’ and ‘wslpath’ interoperation are required.  Resolve
prerequisites, then run:

     ./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install

   ‘etc/wsl-install.conf’ pins the Omnivox version, release URL,
x64/ARM64 archive names, and both SHA-256 hashes.  The installer queries
Windows architecture and verifies the matching published archive; it
does not build Omnivox.

   The full payload is installed below the Windows user's
‘LocalAppData’.  Its absolute WSL executable path is recorded in
‘$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program’, normally
‘~/.config/emacsvox/omnivox-program’.  The installer does not invoke
‘sudo’ or edit shell startup.  It compiles Emacsvox, runs a non-audible
launcher diagnostic, and finishes with the audible server check.
Confirm both tone and speech.

   No Omnivox checkout, Docker, Rust, MinGW, Visual Studio, Windows .NET
Framework, or optional/proprietary runtime is needed.  WinRT speech is
included in Windows.  Use ‘./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --no-audio-check’
only for controlled/headless installation with a separate audible check
later.

* Menu:

* Optional Omnivox Engines: Optional Omnivox Engine Modules.  Install, check, remove, and reinstall optional engines from Emacs.

14.3.1 Manual Release Installation
----------------------------------

Use this equivalent manual procedure to inspect installer actions, work
around a restriction, or repair one layer.  It needs ‘curl’, ‘unzip’,
‘sha256sum’, ‘powershell.exe’, and ‘wslpath’.  The example uses Windows
x64.  On Windows on ARM, use ‘windows-arm64’ in the archive and
installation directory and verify that archive's checksum.

   Download from WSL:

     mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.17.0"
     cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.17.0"
     omnivox_version=1.17.0
     archive="omnivox-${omnivox_version}-windows-x64.zip"
     release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v${omnivox_version}"
     curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
     curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"
     grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -

   The last command must name the archive and report ‘OK’.  Otherwise
remove it and do not run it.  Install all files on the Windows
filesystem:

     windows_local_app_data="$(powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive \
       -Command '[Environment]::GetFolderPath("LocalApplicationData")' | tr -d '\r')"
     install_dir="$(wslpath -u "$windows_local_app_data")/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/$omnivox_version-windows-x64"
     mkdir -p "$install_dir"
     unzip -q -o "$archive" -d "$install_dir"
     chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox.exe"
     config_home=${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-"$HOME/.config"}
     mkdir -p "$config_home/emacsvox"
     printf '%s\n' "$install_dir/omnivox.exe" > \
       "$config_home/emacsvox/omnivox-program"

   Keep ‘omnivox.exe’, ‘espeak-ng-data’, and ‘third-party-licenses’
together.  Do not load the archive's ‘omnivox-voices.el’; Emacsvox
supplies its adapter.  This data-only file persists selection without
shell initialisation.  Respect ‘XDG_CONFIG_HOME’ when set.
‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ remains an explicit temporary override with precedence
over the file.

   Verify through the checkout's launcher:

     ./servers/omnivox --version
     ./servers/omnivox --list-voices
     ./servers/omnivox --check

   Confirm version 1.17.0 or newer, tone, and speech.  Release CI
verifies archive, x64 architecture, adjacent eSpeak data, discovery, and
headless WinRT/eSpeak synthesis; only listening checks your audio path.
The Windows archive is not code-signed.  Do not weaken Windows security
or local policy to install it.

   The generic 1.17.0 archive includes TGSpeechBox profiles/language
data and a portable RHVoice helper.  RHVoice's runtime and
voice/language packages are separate.  Optional Piper, Flite, and RuTTS
companions are not required for WinRT; matching 1.17.0 companions are
published separately.

   The clean source-build route verifies/stages the official Piper
companion and builds RHVoice, Flite, and RuTTS from matching source.
Piper models and the RHVoice runtime/data are not bundled by that build.
A compatible local voice management service can separately download the
reviewed voice packages below; this does not acquire the RHVoice
runtime.  Flite/RuTTS need no external runtime in the source-built
bundle.  Eloquence/DECtalk runtimes remain separately licensed and
supplied.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Optional Omnivox Engine Modules,  Up: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2

14.3.2 Optional Omnivox Engines
-------------------------------

The ordinary WSL2 archive is the core, not a claim that every supported
engine is installed.  Companion archives provide larger, experimental,
or architecture-specific engines.  Some require a separately supplied
runtime or chosen voice data before they can speak.

  1. Inspect running engines

     Open ‘C-e H v b’ to browse engines.  The equivalent command is:

          M-x emacsvox-omnivox-manage-components RET

     Opening uses the current speech inventory without waiting for file
     checks or fresh discovery.  Optional engines missing from that
     inventory stay visible as _not reported_ so management controls
     remain reachable.  ‘v’ browses voices; ‘<RET>’ opens details.

     Details begin with a short status and voice actions, with _Browse
     voices_ selected.  Matching main/notification evidence is combined
     and problems remain near the top.  _Startup settings_ and
     _Diagnostics_ start folded; ‘<RET>’ toggles sections.  Selection
     and folding are remembered per engine.

     _Available_ describes discovery from the current worker.  An older
     check does not alone make it unavailable.  _Previously available_
     belongs to a stopped or replaced worker.  Details announce the
     engine before requesting newer inventory in the background when its
     check is over five minutes old or belongs to another worker.  File
     checks likewise run in the background where requested.  _Streams
     differ_ identifies different availability, policy, health, or voice
     identities.  A bridge's presence does not establish its runtime;
     _Not checked_ does not mean missing.  Diagnostics shows each
     worker's paths, inventory time, runtime discovery, and health.

     _Required runtime_ appears near the top when native libraries are
     needed.  Successful DECtalk discovery, for example, confirms DLL
     and dictionary loading.  Matching worker evidence is combined,
     differences shown separately, and failures retain their reasons.
     Disabled does not mean missing, and retired-worker evidence does
     not establish current status.  Opening/refreshing details uses
     worker discovery without invoking the separate release installer.

  2. Browse voices and variants

     _Browse voices_ combines reported voices and the installed library,
     including disabled downloads.  This dedicated browser preserves
     Workbench's filters and staged edits.  Languages have expandable
     headings with counts; ‘<RET>’ toggles.  A one-language list starts
     expanded and selection/folding are remembered.  ‘<RET>’ or ‘P’ on a
     voice auditions an exact sample, ‘x’ shows details, and ‘t’ opens
     an unsaved experiment.  Saving asks for a palette destination;
     browsing or previewing changes no palette.  ‘q’ returns to selected
     engine details.

     Library data loads asynchronously with quiet refresh.  _Installed_
     identifies library entries versus supplied voices.  _Enabled_ is
     the desired selection for the next Apply.  _Active_ describes
     eligibility on both streams, main only, notification only, or
     neither.  _Unknown_ means missing/inconsistent evidence; ‘R’
     refreshes.  Active does not measure model residency or prove
     listening.  Samples use main speech; inactive downloads need
     enablement and Apply first.

     _Voice variants_ in eSpeak details lists the running worker's
     bundled variants.  Choose a base with ‘b’, browse with arrows or
     ‘C-n~/~C-p’, and audition with ‘P’ or ‘<RET>’.  Sorting is
     alphabetical by displayed name, ignoring capitalisation.  The older
     ‘p’ preview key also remains.  Variants require no download,
     enablement, saving, or speech restart.

     ‘u’, _Use in palette_, asks for palette and named entry, then opens
     the voice editor with the variant preferred, retaining fallbacks
     and tuning.  Preview with ‘P’, then ‘w’ for Save and apply, or save
     to the collection without selecting.  Editing a built-in creates a
     personal copy; _Use at Startup_ remains separate.  For Max as
     ordinary reading, choose the English base with ‘b’, select ‘Max’,
     press ‘u’, and select the palette's ‘default’.  Preview/save there.
     The same ‘default’ is alongside ‘indent’ and other entries in
     normal palette editing.

     ‘V’ instead proposes the base/variant for every named voice.
     Create or update a personal palette, compare, then save; shared
     adjustments and existing fallback tuning survive.  *note Editing
     Named Voices: (emacsvox)Editing Named Voices. explains review and
     recovery.  ‘q’ returns to variants with selection retained; ‘v’
     opens the base browser.  Saved references remain valid.  Older
     workers lacking on-demand variants report the need for an update.

  3. Install and activate voice packages

     Installing files, enabling a voice, and activating it are separate
     steps.  _Get more voices_ in Piper, Flite, MBROLA, or RHVoice
     details opens the reviewed catalogue.  Piper groups language, voice
     name, and quality; Flite offers AWB/RMS.  Single-quality voices
     appear directly under their language, with another group only for
     multiple qualities.  ‘<RET>’ opens a group/model and ‘q’ returns.
     ‘/’ searches all models by name or language beyond the current
     group.  Details show size, source, terms, and storage.  ‘s’
     shows/hides speakers sharing a model; it downloads once while
     individual speakers can be enabled separately.

     ‘i’ installs asynchronously without speaking each progress update.
     Omnivox checks files and validates the voice before adding it
     disabled.  In the catalogue, ‘e’ toggles enablement, asking which
     speaker for a shared model, and ‘a’ reviews Apply to both streams.
     Enablement alone does not restart.  Model details also show these
     actions near installed/enabled status.

     Installation/Apply confirmations offer ‘yes’ and ‘no’ through
     completion: ‘y <TAB> <RET>’ confirms, empty ‘<RET>’ declines, and
     ‘C-g’ cancels.  ‘c’ requests cancellation; wait for cleanup.  The
     shipped catalogue contains reviewed, pinned, natively validated
     models.  Unresolved terms or unsupported configurations are
     withheld; it is not a live catalogue of every upstream download.

     MBROLA offers US1, US2, and US3, with British English en1 included.
     Downloads need the updated separately configured development
     companion on Linux/Windows.  Older hosts keep Piper/Flite
     catalogues and report missing MBROLA support.  Each database
     carries its licence and README.  Initial installation retains en1
     enabled unless explicitly disabled.  Multiple enabled databases can
     serve different entries/streams; each request opens its selected
     database rather than keeping all enabled databases resident.

     RHVoice offers Alan (Scottish English), BDL/CLB (American English),
     and Ksp (Indian English), requiring an updated host and separately
     installed compatible RHVoice runtime selected in engine settings.
     Each package contains its English data and upstream notices.
     Enable and Apply combines managed voices with external ones,
     including SLT.  A conflicting external copy is reported, not
     replaced.  Removal affects the managed package, not external voices
     or the runtime.

     In _Browse voices_, ‘+’ changes desired enablement, and ‘a’ reviews
     Apply.  The operation checks both replacement workers, restarts
     main and notification speech, and verifies them before committing.
     Failure restores both previous configurations.  Disabling preserves
     files and palette references.  _Enabled_ changes immediately but
     _Active_ awaits Apply.

     After installation, ‘l’ browses, ‘+’ enables, and ‘a’ reviews
     managed engines and both streams.  Installation alone neither loads
     a voice nor changes speech.  Downloads belong to you in Omnivox's
     per-user storage across executable upgrades.  Failed/interrupted
     operations retain files for inspection; automatic resume and
     package updates are not provided.  Uninstall cleanup has its
     separate explicit resume action below.

  4. Remove or import managed voices

     Disable every speaker in a downloaded Piper, Flite, MBROLA, or
     RHVoice package and Apply before ‘C-c u’ in its voice browser.
     Review voices, size, and retained palette references before
     confirming.  Files still needed by other sessions remain.  Built-in
     SLT/en1, imported originals, and engine runtimes cannot be removed
     here.  ‘C-c U’ reviews incomplete cleanup for explicit resumption.

     The voice browser's ‘d’ opens downloads and ‘b’ includes built-in
     Flite SLT.  ‘i’ imports a completed native-validation operation by
     ID, initially disabled.  Imported files remain at their validated
     paths and must stay there.  This is an advanced import, not the
     catalogue installation or a file-selection wizard.  ‘C-c r’ shows
     the last Apply result, ‘R’ refreshes, and ‘q’ returns.

     Apply needs separate main/notification streams from the updated
     local host.  Clear explicit Piper/Flite file overrides before
     applying those engines' library voices.  Uncertain native cleanup
     or commit blocks another Apply and stays visible in its result.

  5. Configure and check an engine

     Under _Startup settings_, _Edit file settings_ opens the bundled
     local launcher's engine option in Customize.  Piper accepts one
     ONNX model and matching JSON; Eloquence/DECtalk accept runtime
     DLLs; Flite accepts external ‘.flitevox’ files with individual
     _Load file_ switches.  Use absolute paths as seen by Emacs; WSL
     converts them for the selected Windows executable.  Keep companion
     files beside their runtime/model.  Remote settings must be changed
     on the speech host.

     These are explicit overrides, not the complete installed-voice
     inventory.  No manually selected files can still mean library
     voices or launcher defaults are available.  A manual Flite file
     list overrides its installed library.

     _Check configured engine_ runs independent discovery using the
     local launcher's current configuration, limited to 30 seconds.  It
     retains target, voices, and diagnostics but neither speaks a sample
     nor replaces live workers.  A failed Flite file can leave built-in
     SLT usable; incomplete discovery reports fewer than the selected
     files plus SLT.  Duplicate external voice names are unsupported.

     File settings affect newly started workers.  Save in Customize,
     then explicitly choose _Restart local speech_ to interrupt this
     session's two streams when ready.  Refresh live voices afterward.
     Disabling a Flite file preserves data/references; new helpers omit
     it, while existing helpers keep it until restart.  Built-in SLT
     stays loaded and each worker loads its own enabled external voices.
     These switches are not a general per-voice policy or memory
     measurement.

     ‘OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL’, ‘OMNIVOX_FLITE_VOICES’, runtime environment
     settings, and legacy ‘EMACSVOX_ECI_DLL=/=EMACSVOX_DECTALK_DLL’
     override file options and are identified in details.  Clearing an
     option returns to discovery/defaults.  Direct-native executable
     launches do not consume the bundled launcher's options.

  6. Manage the installed Omnivox release

     This manager changes versioned release files, not the running
     workers or a voice library's active configuration.  On WSL, choose
     _Manage installed release_ and check _Installation being managed_
     and _Location_.  That target can differ from a development build or
     remote speech server.  _Installed engine files_ reports presence;
     _Check release engine_ tests runtime initialisation and voice
     discovery for that release.

     Release installation and removal keep current speech running and do
     not select another speech server.  They do not automatically
     restart workers.  A helper in use causes removal to fail visibly;
     close sessions using that release before retrying.  This differs
     from the library's explicit Apply and the file editor's _Restart
     local speech_.  Legacy direct-operation restart preferences do not
     change the boundary of this explicit installed-release view.

     Use _Install engine_ and confirm to verify and install a
     version-matched component.  Progress belongs to the release view
     while current-worker status stays separate.  _Refresh release
     files_ runs a bounded asynchronous file check; _Check release
     engine_ discovers voices without auditioning.  Refresh after a
     changed target before retrying.  Completion gives a short notice
     without selecting another buffer; _Read last result_ opens retained
     complete output.

     Within details, ‘<TAB>~/~S-<TAB>’ moves among actions and sections,
     ‘<RET>’ activates or toggles, and arrows read visible fields.  ‘q’
     returns to the selected engine row, ‘h’ to Emacsvox settings, and
     ‘?’ to Help.  Selection and expansion survive background refresh.
     _Refresh status_ or ‘g’ in current-worker details asynchronously
     requests both live inventories, not a release-file check.

     _Uninstall engine_ under _Engine installation_ removes only
     manager-installed Flite, RuTTS, Piper, or TGSpeechBox components.
     It does not remove the core, built-in engines, or runtime bridges.
     The whole managed component directory is removed: move any personal
     files elsewhere before confirming.  External runtimes/models
     outside it are untouched and retain their supplier's removal
     procedure.  Verified downloads remain cached; _Install engine_
     reuses a valid cache for reinstallation.

     Operations are asynchronous.  The view shows _uninstalling_ or
     _testing_ and refreshes on completion or failure.  A failed listing
     retains its last records and the error while live discovery remains
     independently available.  If a helper is locked, close the sessions
     using it and invoke the removal action again.  An interrupted
     removal with only the expected helper left can resume; directories
     containing unrecognised files remain protected.

     ‘etc/omnivox-components.conf’ pins names, sizes, and SHA-256 hashes
     to the installed core version.  The WSL installer rejects
     mismatched cores, unsafe archive layout, missing source provenance,
     and helpers that fail staged discovery.  Components are installed
     under the same versioned Windows ‘LocalAppData’ release.  It does
     not invoke ‘sudo’ or edit shell startup.

     Component and data boundaries differ:

        • Flite includes compact English SLT without an external
          runtime.
        • RuTTS includes compact Russian voices without an external
          runtime.
        • Piper installs engine support; a usable voice also needs a
          reviewed ONNX model and model card, configured separately or
          through supported library management.
        • TGSpeechBox is in the Windows x64/ARM64 core with multilingual
          formant voices, calibrated rate, and exact anchors for
          capitalisation/timed presentation.  Matching standalone
          components allow reinstallation.
        • RHVoice, Eloquence, and DECtalk bridges do not acquire their
          external runtimes.  Supply compatible runtimes under their
          distribution/licensing terms.  RHVoice's reviewed catalogue
          can separately download voice and language packages on a
          supporting local host, as described above.  This is distinct
          from runtime acquisition and preserves external installations.
          Eloquence and DECtalk runtime, dictionary, and licence
          material remain user-supplied.

     For terminal management, use ‘./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components
     --list’, then, for example, ‘./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components
     --install flite’.  Multiple engines can follow ‘--install’ or
     ‘--uninstall’.  ‘./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components --uninstall
     flite’ removes the managed directory while retaining its archive.
     Restart deliberately when the running session needs newly installed
     configuration, and use ‘--test ENGINE’ or the release view's _Check
     release engine_ to check that target.  Direct release assets remain
     available for maintenance/recovery, but the manager is the ordinary
     component-selection route.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox,  Next: Installing Omnivox From Source,  Prev: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2,  Up: Speech Backends

14.4 Installing Native Windows Emacsvox
=======================================

Native Windows x64 is supported with Emacs 30.2 or newer.  A development
Setup executable supplies an installation wizard.  Otherwise use the
guided PowerShell installer from a Windows checkout.  The advanced
manual route requires an existing native Emacs.  Neither Setup nor the
guided installation requires WSL, Docker, Rust, or a system MSYS2
installation.

14.4.1 Using a Windows Setup Build
----------------------------------

Run the development Setup executable to install ‘Emacsvox Windows’ for
your account.  The wizard identifies Emacsvox and its included
Emacs/Omnivox versions.  No administrator privileges or network
connection are required.  A desktop shortcut is optional; a Start menu
group is created unless disabled for an unattended installation.  The
final page explains startup/removal and offers Test speech for two
announcements and Start Emacsvox.  Finish runs selected actions and
closes Setup.

   The default directory is ‘%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Desktop Dev’.
Launch through Start or its shortcut.  Rerun Setup to repair missing
files or upgrade a development build, closing the Emacsvox using that
installation first.  Setup does not close it.  Locally edited
application files prevent replacement so they can be preserved.

   Windows Settings or the Start uninstall entry removes the application
while retaining personal data by default.  Optional cleanup separately
lists sizes for logs/cache, this Emacs profile (including welcome
preference), and unused downloads in the shared Omnivox library.  Leave
choices unchecked for reinstallation.  Shared palettes/settings outside
the profile stay.  Unattended removal always preserves personal data.

   Active voices, voices referenced by another profile, and files
retained by a speech session stay with an explanation.  Disable and
Apply active downloads, then close the sessions using them before
cleanup.  Imported originals and system voices are never removed, and
directory links are not followed.  Development Setup builds are test
artifacts; these instructions do not imply that a stable release
installer includes this branch's interface.

14.4.2 Guided Windows x64 Installation
--------------------------------------

You need PowerShell 5.1 or newer, Git, network access, and working
Windows audio with a speech voice.  Put the checkout on a local Windows
drive, not a WSL or network path.  To follow this development manual,
select its branch:

     git clone --branch ui-experiments https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox.git
     Set-Location emacsvox
     .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop -Check
     .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop

   Skip cloning for an existing checkout and inspect its branch/local
changes before updating it.  The check reports selected Emacs and paths
without downloads, configuration writes, or audio.

   The installer reuses native Emacs 30.2 or newer.  For a
checksum-verified complete GNU Emacs 31.1 Windows release, use:

     .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -DownloadEmacs -Check
     .\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -DownloadEmacs

   This includes runtime libraries without a compiler/MSYS2.
Alternatively select a separately installed prebuilt Windows Emacs
(https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html) with ‘-Emacs’ and its
‘bin/emacs.exe’.  ‘-BuildEmacs’ explicitly builds 31.1 through a private
MSYS2 UCRT64 toolchain; it can require several gigabytes and substantial
downloading.  No source build begins without that choice.  The installer
then prepares the pinned Omnivox core and Emacsvox, reusing installed
components and verified cache on later runs.

   ‘-Emacs "C:\path\to\Emacs\bin\emacs.exe"’ selects an existing
executable.  Explicit ‘-Emacs’, environment ‘EMACS’, and ‘local.mk’ must
agree and require native 30.2 or newer.  ‘-DownloadEmacs’ and
‘-BuildEmacs’ choose their managed Emacs instead of saved/PATH
selections, reuse a matching complete installation, and cannot replace
an explicit choice.  Choose only one.

   Per-user defaults are:

   • Emacs, Omnivox, isolated profile, and logs under
     ‘%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Native’.
   • Private build tools under
     ‘%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Toolchains\msys2-20260611’.
   • Verified archives under ‘%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Downloads’.
   • Data-only ‘native-install.json’ in the checkout, ignored by Git.

   ‘-InstallRoot’, ‘-ToolchainRoot’, and ‘-CacheDirectory’ choose other
local paths.  The MSYS2 tool path must have no spaces; set
‘-ToolchainRoot’ if needed.  Checkout and Emacs paths may contain
spaces.  Personal initialisation, system PATH, Windows services, and
JAWS settings remain unchanged.

   ‘-Offline -CacheDirectory "C:\path\to\archives"’ forbids downloads
and verifies required cached archives before changes.  Missing/damaged
archives stop it.  Offline supports prebuilt/existing Emacs, not
‘-BuildEmacs’.  Read-only ‘-Check’ reports the plan without testing
cached archives or playing audio.

   Source archives are checksum-pinned.  MSYS2 compiler packages use
signed rolling repositories, and installed versions are recorded.
Managed source builds disable Lisp native compilation and include
runtime DLLs, so launch needs no MSYS2 on PATH.

   The final test opens a temporary native Emacs, verifies both streams,
speaks two announcements, and closes that test.  Confirm both by
listening.  After ‘-NoAudioCheck’, perform the explicit check before
acceptance:

     .\bin\emacsvox.ps1 -Diagnose
     .\bin\emacsvox.ps1 -Check
     .\bin\emacsvox.ps1

   The last command opens the isolated desktop with Windows speech by
default.  ‘OMNIVOX_ENGINE’ remains an explicit selection for separately
configured engines.  Native optional-engine installation, ARM64, and
other native roles are not supplied by this guided installer.

   After source updates, rerun it before launching.  It preserves source
edits and refreshes compiled files.  Missing/stale compilation stops
launch with that instruction.  Keep named build logs after failure.
Inspect incomplete installations before moving or choosing another
directory; unknown partial trees are not overwritten.

   Muting JAWS speech does not disable its shortcuts.  If ‘M-<’ or ‘M->’
is intercepted, try ‘JAWSKey+3’ before the key.  Bart's
‘workstation-config’ repository supplies the separate Emacs-specific
JAWS override used on the test workstation.

14.4.3 Manual Native Installation
---------------------------------

This route requires native Emacs 30.2 or newer already installed.  It
does not acquire Emacs or replace the guided x64 checks.  Set
‘$Emacsvox’ to the absolute checkout path and continue in one PowerShell
window.

14.4.4 Download And Verify Omnivox
----------------------------------

Set ‘$Platform’ for the host.  The normal Intel/AMD x64 example is:

     $Version = "1.17.0"
     $Platform = "windows-x64" # Use windows-arm64 only on Windows on ARM.
     $DownloadDir = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE "Downloads\omnivox-$Version"
     $Archive = "omnivox-$Version-$Platform.zip"
     $ReleaseUrl = "https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$Version"

     New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $DownloadDir | Out-Null
     $ArchivePath = Join-Path $DownloadDir $Archive
     $ChecksumPath = Join-Path $DownloadDir "sha256sums.txt"
     Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/$Archive" -OutFile $ArchivePath
     Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/sha256sums.txt" -OutFile $ChecksumPath

     $Pattern = '\s+\*?' + [regex]::Escape($Archive) + '$'
     $ChecksumLine = Get-Content $ChecksumPath |
       Where-Object { $_ -match $Pattern } |
       Select-Object -First 1
     if (-not $ChecksumLine) { throw "No published checksum for $Archive" }
     $Expected = ($ChecksumLine -split '\s+')[0].ToLowerInvariant()
     $Actual = (Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 $ArchivePath).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
     if ($Actual -ne $Expected) { throw "Checksum mismatch for $Archive" }
     Write-Host "Checksum OK: $Archive"

   Do not extract/run unless the final line reports ‘Checksum OK’.  This
unsigned Windows release may be rejected by policy; stop rather than
weakening security.  The existence of an ARM64 Omnivox archive does not
extend Emacsvox's supported native desktop beyond x64.

14.4.5 Install And Check The Native Server
------------------------------------------

In the same PowerShell, extract everything beneath per-user
‘LocalAppData’:

     $InstallDir = Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA "Emacsvox\Omnivox\releases\$Version-$Platform"
     New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $InstallDir | Out-Null
     Expand-Archive -LiteralPath $ArchivePath -DestinationPath $InstallDir -Force
     $Omnivox = Join-Path $InstallDir "omnivox.exe"

     foreach ($Required in @(
       $Omnivox,
       (Join-Path $InstallDir "espeak-ng-data\phontab"),
       (Join-Path $InstallDir "third-party-licenses\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md")
     )) {
       if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $Required -PathType Leaf)) {
         throw "Incomplete Omnivox installation: $Required"
       }
     }

     & $Omnivox --version
     if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Omnivox version check failed" }
     & $Omnivox --list-voices
     if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Omnivox voice discovery failed" }
     & $Omnivox --check
     if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Omnivox audio check failed" }

   Require version 1.17.0 or newer, at least one Windows voice, and both
tone and speech.  Keep executable, ‘espeak-ng-data’, and
‘third-party-licenses’ together.  Do not copy/load ‘omnivox-voices.el’
from the archive; that is the upstream Emacspeak adapter, not Emacsvox's
bundled one.

14.4.6 Start Native Emacsvox
----------------------------

Keep the window so ‘$Omnivox’ still identifies the verified binary.  Set
‘$Emacs’ to native ‘emacs.exe’ and ‘$Emacsvox’ if the checkout was not
set above:

     $Emacs = "C:\path\to\emacs\bin\emacs.exe"
     # If not set above, uncomment and edit the next line:
     # $Emacsvox = "C:\path\to\emacsvox"

     & $Emacs --version
     if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Could not run native Emacs" }
     $env:TTS_PROGRAM = $Omnivox
     & $Emacs -Q --no-splash --load "$Emacsvox\lisp\emacsvox-setup.el"

   Require Emacs 30.2 or newer.  Use ‘emacs.exe’ instead of
‘runemacs.exe’ for first launch so startup errors remain in PowerShell.
Direct setup loading is intentional: ‘bin/emacsvox’ is POSIX;
‘bin/emacsvox.ps1’ is the guided native entry.

14.4.7 Verify Speech And Report Problems
----------------------------------------

Check the isolated native session:

  1. Confirm the startup introduction/ready checkpoint is audible.
  2. ‘C-h v tts-program <RET>’ should identify the verified executable's
     absolute path.
  3. ‘M-x tts-speak-version <RET>’ speaks the version; ‘M-x
     omnivox-list-voices <RET>’ displays Windows voices.
  4. ‘C-n~/~C-p’ speak line movement, ‘C-e s’ stops current speech, and
     ‘M-x tts-restart <RET>’ restores it without process errors.
  5. Toggle icons twice with ‘M-x emacsvox-toggle-icons <RET>’.  In a
     session starting with icons enabled, the second toggle restores
     them and plays an icon in addition to speech.  Preserve the initial
     preference when testing a customised session.

   If direct ‘--check’ fails, diagnose Omnivox/Windows audio first.  If
it passes but the server cannot be found in Emacs, confirm the same
PowerShell's ‘$env:TTS_PROGRAM’ is absolute and ‘Test-Path
$env:TTS_PROGRAM’ returns ‘True’.  For a process that exits, retain
PowerShell errors, ‘*Messages*’, Windows version/architecture, and full
‘emacs.exe --version’ and ‘omnivox.exe --version’.

   After acceptance, put the same selection before setup in your init
file:

     (setenv "TTS_PROGRAM"
             "C:/Users/NAME/AppData/Local/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/1.17.0-windows-x64/omnivox.exe")
     (load-file "C:/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")

   Replace both paths.  This deliberately starts with an absolute
executable.  Adding its directory to ‘exec-path’ is optional until
selecting ‘omnivox’ by short name.  Native desktop support remains x64;
remote speech is a separate preview.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Installing Omnivox From Source,  Next: Building Omnivox For WSL2,  Prev: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox,  Up: Speech Backends

14.5 Installing Omnivox From Source
===================================

On GNU/Linux/macOS, a source build needs network access for clone,
toolchain, and locked dependencies.  Prepare:

   • rustup (https://rustup.rs/), with the checked-in
     ‘rust-toolchain.toml’ selecting Rust.
   • Python 3, C/C++, CMake, and Clang/libclang for eSpeak NG and
     generated data.
   • GNU/Linux ‘pkg-config’ and ALSA development headers, commonly
     ‘libasound2-dev’ on Debian/Ubuntu or ‘alsa-lib-devel’ on Fedora.
   • macOS Xcode command-line tools, installed with ‘xcode-select
     --install’.

   Keep Omnivox beside Emacsvox, also matching the WSL layout:

     cd /path/containing/emacsvox
     git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
     cd omnivox
     make build
     make install

   ‘make build’ runs the locked release build and stages matching eSpeak
data and notices.  ‘make install’ installs binary/data normally under
‘~/.cargo/bin’.  Set ‘OMNIVOX_INSTALL_BIN’ only for another Cargo binary
directory.  A bare ‘cargo build’ does not produce the complete
installation payload.

   Make the installed directory visible on ‘PATH’.  No proprietary
runtime is needed for the recommended Linux/Mac route.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Building Omnivox For WSL2,  Next: Selecting And Checking Omnivox,  Prev: Installing Omnivox From Source,  Up: Speech Backends

14.6 Building Omnivox For WSL2
==============================

This advanced path maintains the reproducible Windows bundle or optional
engine integrations.  Ordinary WSL2 installations should use the *note
prebuilt Windows release: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under
WSL2.  This source target produces x86-64 Windows.

   Requirements are:

   • WSL2 with working ‘powershell.exe’ and ‘wslpath’, plus writable
     Windows ‘LocalAppData’.
   • A Docker client/daemon supporting Linux/AMD64 containers, runnable
     by the WSL user without ‘sudo’.  Docker Desktop's WSL integration
     is one option.
   • Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer, not merely a modern ‘dotnet’
     SDK.  Visual Studio is unnecessary.
   • ‘git’, GNU ‘make’, ‘curl’, ‘unzip’, ‘sha256sum’, and standard GNU
     utilities including ‘awk’, ‘cmp’, ‘find’, ‘readlink’, ‘sed’,
     ‘sort’, and ‘xargs’.
   • Network for the initial Docker image, Rust dependencies, and two
     checksum-pinned NuGet packages.  Subsequent builds reuse caches.
   • Sibling ‘emacsvox’ and ‘omnivox’ checkouts, or explicit
     ‘OMNIVOX_DIR’.  The clean release target requires the Omnivox
     source commit recorded for the pinned Piper companion in
     ‘servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock’; mismatches fail rather
     than combining versions.  The development route accepts current
     source by omitting Piper.  Allow disk space for Docker,
     native/cross builds, and caches.

   Rust, MinGW, CMake, Clang/libclang, eSpeak NG, Roslyn, Visual Studio,
and .NET reference assemblies need not be installed in WSL; the
controlled toolchain pins/builds them.  Eloquence, DECtalk, and RHVoice
runtime/data remain optional and unnecessary for WinRT.

   Check prerequisites with:

     cd /path/to/emacsvox
     make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain

   The first run downloads pinned .NET compiler packages, builds the
pinned Docker image, and checks exact Rust, MinGW, libclang, Roslyn, and
Framework versions.  It is not a read-only presence check.  Failures
identify missing host tools or unsupported Framework before the full
build.

   With clean Emacsvox and sibling Omnivox tracked files, run:

     cd /path/to/emacsvox
     make windows-omnivox

   This reproducible release path builds a pinned Windows-GNU executable
and stages a content-addressed runtime under ‘servers/omnivox-bin’.
When the Piper lock identifies the matching integrations, it includes
the checksum-pinned Piper companion, RHVoice helper without runtime,
Flite with CMU US SLT, and RuTTS with male/female Russian voices.  It
bundles no Piper model or RuLex.  The payload is also copied to
Windows-local storage to avoid WSL filesystem cost during discovery.  It
does not download Eloquence, DECtalk, or RHVoice runtimes or require
them for WinRT.

   The final live gate starts that exact runtime, discovers Flite SLT
and both RuTTS voices, and requires nonempty WAV synthesis from each
helper.  Repeat without rebuilding using ‘make
verify-windows-omnivox-live’.

   To register Piper, review its model/card and keep the adjacent
‘.onnx.json’ or ‘.json’.  Supply the absolute model path while staging:

     OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx make windows-omnivox

   Model/configuration are copied to separate content-addressed Windows
‘LocalAppData’ storage with recorded hashes, not bundled into the
runtime.  Omitting that variable installs the companion but leaves Piper
unregistered.  After staging, check with a fresh process:

     ./servers/omnivox --engine piper --list-voices

   Restart both speech processes or Emacs, then inspect running engines
with ‘C-e H v b’.  Piper requires the newly staged server and
successfully loaded model/helper.  The direct ‘C-e d e’ preference
chooser is not a read-only inventory command; use the browser to inspect
without selecting a preference.

   For tracked development changes, use the route in *note Integration
Maintenance: Integration Maintenance. rather than bypassing the
clean-release guard:

     OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice.dll \
       OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice/data \
       make windows-omnivox-dev
     ./servers/omnivox --engine flite --list-voices
     ./servers/omnivox --engine rutts --list-voices

   It builds current source, stages RHVoice's helper, Flite SLT, and
RuTTS 6.3.3 male/female voices, deliberately omitting the older pinned
Piper companion.  RuTTS needs no separate install; RuLex is excluded.
RHVoice needs compatible ‘RHVoice.dll’ and language/voice data.
Supplied library/data variables are validated and Windows path pointers
retained beside the local runtime; the launcher restores them for fresh
sessions.  Also set ‘OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG’ for a separate
configuration directory.  These external files are not copied into the
payload.  Omnivox's ‘docs/RHVOICE.md’ and ‘docs/RUTTS.md’ give
validation details.  Restart after restaging/changing inputs, then
inspect with ‘C-e H v b’.

   Piper tests against newer committed Omnivox use
‘windows-omnivox-piper-dev’ and a native Windows CI artifact.  It
requires the published SHA-256 and a reviewed external model, rejecting
dirty or different source.  See *note Integration Maintenance:
Integration Maintenance. for provenance and the complete command.  This
does not alter the reproducible release's pinned companion.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Selecting And Checking Omnivox,  Next: Compatibility Speech Servers,  Prev: Building Omnivox For WSL2,  Up: Speech Backends

14.7 Selecting And Checking Omnivox
===================================

Test the installed server outside Emacs on GNU/Linux/macOS:

     omnivox --version
     omnivox --check
     omnivox --list-voices

   In WSL2, the launcher selects ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ first, the per-user
recorded installation next, then a content-addressed source runtime,
then ‘omnivox’ on ‘PATH’:

     ./servers/omnivox --check

   The check initialises engine/output and plays tone then speech.  Its
success message is conditional on hearing both.  Exit zero alone is not
audible acceptance.

   Select before loading Emacsvox:

     export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox

   An export affects that shell and children; a shell-startup entry
persists it.  On POSIX, ‘servers/omnivox’ finds ‘omnivox’ on ‘PATH’;
‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’ can explicitly override it.  The WSL per-user
selection persists across shells and outranks staged source runtimes.
Native Windows resolves ‘omnivox.exe’ through ‘exec-path’ or the
absolute ‘TTS_PROGRAM’.  Follow *note Installing Native Windows
Emacsvox: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox. for the supported native x64
route.

   In a running session, ‘M-x omnivox’ temporarily selects that server.
‘M-x tts-speak-version’ identifies the live adapter and ‘M-x
tts-restart’ restarts after a deliberate configuration change.  The
complete isolated start, expected speech, and recovery remain in *note
Installation: Installation.

14.7.1 Remote Workstation Speech (Preview)
------------------------------------------

Emacs can run on an SSH host while Omnivox, installed voices, and
playback stay on your workstation.  This uses Omnivox 1.17.0's ‘--serve’
and Emacsvox's remote transport.  Remote access remains a preview
feature.

   Create a private token on the workstation.  The tool refuses to
overwrite or print an existing token:

     mkdir -p "$HOME/.config/omnivox"
     python3 /path/to/omnivox/tools/create_remote_token.py \
         "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token"

   Leave the installed server running in a terminal, with the matching
Emacsvox sound-pack directory:

     omnivox --serve --token-file "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token" \
         --sound-root /path/to/emacsvox/sounds

   For a Windows service through the WSL launcher, translate both paths:

     /path/to/emacsvox/servers/omnivox --serve \
         --token-file "$(wslpath -w "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token")" \
         --sound-root "$(wslpath -w /path/to/emacsvox/sounds)"

   In another workstation terminal, copy the token through verified SSH
and open the reverse forward, replacing ‘user@emacs-host’:

     ssh user@emacs-host 'mkdir -p ~/.config/omnivox; chmod 700 ~/.config/omnivox'
     scp "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token" user@emacs-host:.config/omnivox/remote-token
     ssh user@emacs-host 'chmod 600 ~/.config/omnivox/remote-token'
     ssh -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o ServerAliveInterval=10 \
         -o ServerAliveCountMax=2 \
         -R 127.0.0.1:6417:127.0.0.1:6417 user@emacs-host

   When Omnivox runs on Windows, use Windows OpenSSH for the final
connection: ‘ssh.exe’ from WSL or ‘ssh’ from PowerShell.  It must reach
Windows loopback; a WSL client under NAT reaches separate WSL loopback
instead.  Windows SSH uses its Windows keys and host-trust
configuration.

   *note Installing Remote Emacsvox: Installing Remote Emacsvox.
provides the remote-only installation and isolated launcher.  ‘--remote’
configures transport before speech starts and needs no local Omnivox
binary.  For personal configuration, set before startup:

     (setq tts-program "omnivox"
           omnivox-remote-host "127.0.0.1"
           omnivox-remote-port 6417
           omnivox-remote-token-file "~/.config/omnivox/remote-token")

   ‘M-x omnivox-remote-connect’ connects/retries, ‘M-x
omnivox-remote-status’ inspects, and ‘M-x omnivox-remote-disconnect’
stops and suspends retries.  Reconnection refreshes voices/routing and
discards unfinished speech.  Main and notification retain separate
workers.  Only one Emacs session owns a service at a time.

   If main connects but notification fails, main remains available while
the missing connection retries.  Recovery does not replace working main
speech.  ‘omnivox-remote-auto-reconnect’ set to nil disables automatic
retry; explicit disconnect suspends it until another connection.  Status
retains retry and error information.

   Keep service and forwarded endpoint on loopback; do not enable SSH
‘GatewayPorts’.  SSH encrypts the transport and the token excludes other
remote host users.  To rotate, stop the service, replace both private
token files, and restart.  Typing ‘quit’ or interrupting the service
terminal stops workers.

   Inventory belongs to the workstation; install/manage engines there.
Bundled icons resolve by pack-relative name.  Personal sounds outside
that directory and sound uploads are unsupported; no ‘--sound-root’
means no file-based icons.  For an occupied remote port, change the left
side of ‘-R’ and match ‘omnivox-remote-port’.  Authentication failure
indicates differing tokens or excessively broad file permissions; a busy
service belongs to another connection.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Compatibility Speech Servers,  Next: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership,  Prev: Selecting And Checking Omnivox,  Up: Speech Backends

14.8 Compatibility Speech Servers
=================================

These retained direct servers support existing installations, not the
recommended first setup or platform matrix:

‘espeak’
     Direct Linux Tcl server.  ‘make espeak’ needs Tclx at runtime, Tcl
     8.6 headers, eSpeak NG development libraries, and C++.  Useful for
     compatibility testing; new installations should use Omnivox.
‘swiftmac’
     Direct Swift macOS server, needing Swift 5.9 or newer and ‘make
     swiftmac’.  See ‘servers/mac-swiftmac/Readme.org’ for this
     alternative to Omnivox's AVSpeechSynthesizer engine.
‘mac’
     Legacy Python/PyObjC server.  The unset Darwin selector still names
     it as a compatibility default, not an initial-user route.  It needs
     Foundation, AppKit, and PyObjCTools.
‘outloud’
     Direct Linux IBM TTS/Eloquence adapter with separately licensed
     compatible runtime.  Emacsvox does not distribute it.  Voxin
     availability is described at <https://voxin.oralux.net>.
‘dtk-soft’
     Direct Software DECtalk adapter with separately installed runtime.
     See ‘servers/software-dtk/Readme.org’.  Licensed runtimes are not
     distributed by Emacsvox.

   The native Windows audio, standalone Eloquence, and standalone
DECtalk bridges under ‘servers/’ are advanced WSL compatibility paths.
Piper is an optional Omnivox engine/model workflow, not a primary
Emacsvox server.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership,  Prev: Compatibility Speech Servers,  Up: Speech Backends

14.9 Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities
=============================================

Local server messages/errors remain in diagnostics buffers, hidden from
the ordinary buffer list by default.  Use ‘C-e H D m’ for main or ‘C-e H
D n’ for notification diagnostics, or ‘M-x tts-show-diagnostics’ with
‘Speaker’ or ‘Notify’.  Missing local diagnostics are reported rather
than invented.

   Set ‘tts-hide-diagnostic-buffers’ to nil in Customize and restart
speech to include them in the ordinary list.  Existing diagnostics are
retained; custom buffer switchers may show hidden buffers independently.

   Choose the diagnostic at the failed layer:

‘omnivox’ is not found
     In WSL, run ‘./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check’ and inspect its
     saved path.  On other POSIX systems check ‘PATH’ or an absolute
     ‘OMNIVOX_PROGRAM’.  Keep ‘espeak-ng-data’ and
     ‘third-party-licenses’ beside the executable.
‘omnivox --check’ errors or is inaudible
     Diagnose below Emacsvox using Omnivox's build guide
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md),
     platform status
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/STATUS.md),
     and diagnostics guide
     (https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/guides/diagnostics.md).
     Do not change Emacs configuration before tone/speech work.
The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
     Confirm ‘TTS_PROGRAM’ was ‘omnivox’ before setup loaded, then use
     ‘M-x tts-speak-version’.  Rerun the WSL installer check for a
     release installation.  For development source staging, run ‘make
     verify-windows-omnivox-runtime’ and restart after restaging.
A process starts and later fails
     The POSIX/WSL launcher retains private bounded stderr logs at
     ‘$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox’, normally
     ‘~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox’.  Spoken text is excluded unless
     ‘OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT’ is explicitly enabled; that can expose
     passwords, mail, and documents.
Speech works but is delayed
     ‘C-e d D’ enables a private diagnostic log; reproduce one short
     presentation and toggle it off.  Default
     ‘~/.emacsvox-aural-diagnostics.log’ can be changed with ‘C-u C-e d
     D’ when enabling.  Records ‘dispatch-sent’,
     ‘mixer-source-observed’, and ‘playback-terminal-observed’ share a
     dispatch ID.  Match it in Omnivox logs to separate submission,
     queueing, synthesis, and mixer-source delay.  This Emacs log is off
     by default because submitted speech text is complete.  A mixer
     observation is not physical output; device/hardware buffering can
     add delay.  The diagnostics menu's ‘l’ also exposes the explicit
     session logging control with its privacy warning.

   Emacsvox owns selection, launcher, Windows staging, and its bundled
Lisp adapter.  Omnivox owns native builds, engines, CLI, runtime layout,
and protocols.  Backend-local READMEs document compatibility adapters
and licensed runtimes.  Use those owning references for versioned
details instead of duplicating their contracts in first-installation
instructions.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands,  Next: Finding Tasks,  Prev: Speech Backends,  Up: Top

15 Emacsvox Keyboard Commands.
******************************

Use this chapter to recall an everyday command and check its important
conditions before acting.  *note Read the key instructions: Emacs Key
Notation. explains modifiers, capital letters and successive keypresses.
*note Basic Usage: (emacsvox)Basic Usage. teaches the reading and
editing workflows; the links beside each reminder lead to their details.

   These are default keys, used without a prefix argument unless one is
shown.  Application keymaps and personal bindings can change their
meaning.  A short reference cannot replace a terminal's or editor's own
instructions.  Use *note live Help: Using Online Help. in the affected
buffer when a binding differs, and *note the generated keymap reference:
(emacsvox-reference)Emacsvox Keymaps. for the full default inventory.

* Menu:

* Reading reminders: Everyday Reading Commands.  Choose a reading unit and check its effect on point and windows.
* Editing reminders: Everyday Editing Commands.  Select, copy, cut, paste and recover with the right scope.
* Files, buffers and windows: Everyday Workspace Commands.  Open and save documents without confusing buffers and views.
* Help and orientation: Everyday Help Commands.  Find commands and return to the intended working buffer.
* Speech and settings: Everyday Speech Commands.  Find controls and distinguish navigation from undo and saving.
* How prefixes and menus fit together: Emacsvox Key Prefixes.  Understand the speech prefix, menu entry and application overrides.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Everyday Reading Commands,  Next: Everyday Editing Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.1 Reading reminders
======================

These commands apply to the selected buffer's accessible text.  In Info
that means the current node, not the whole manual.  Use the
application's own reader for a table, terminal or other specialised
view.

Read from here (‘C-e i’)
     Read towards the accessible buffer end with tracked position.
     Requires a supporting speech server; point advances by chunks, and
     another command stops reading before performing its action.  See
     *note Stopping and restarting tracked reading: (emacsvox)Reading
     From Point.
Stop current speech (‘C-e s’)
     Stop main and notification speech without disabling future
     feedback.  During tracked reading this retains a chunk boundary,
     not an exact spoken word.  See *note Where stopping leaves point:
     (emacsvox)Reading From Point.
Repeat the current line (‘C-e l’)
     Inspect the line without moving point.  ‘C-u C-e l’ reads from
     point to the line's end.  See *note Short reading and prefix
     variants: (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point.
Read or spell the word (‘C-e w’)
     Read the word at point; repeat at the same position to spell it.
     See *note Inspecting short passages: (emacsvox)Reading Without
     Moving Point.
Inspect the character (‘C-e c’)
     Read the character at point, using its phonetic name where
     available.  Point does not move.  See *note Character and word
     reading: (emacsvox)Reading.
Read the paragraph (‘C-e [’)
     Read the paragraph around point without moving it.  The mode
     determines its boundaries.  Sentence reading is also available by
     name.  See *note Sentence and paragraph inspection:
     (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point.
Read a checked selection (‘C-e r’)
     Read between point and mark.  A short region read preserves its
     endpoints and activation; it does not establish a new selection for
     you.  See *note Selecting and checking text: (emacsvox)Selecting
     Text With Speech.
Read the buffer (‘C-e b’)
     Read its accessible contents without tracked interruption position.
     Requests of 40,000 characters or more fall back to windowful
     reading at the current default limit, removing other windows.  The
     same limit applies to region reading.  See *note Buffer-reading
     limits and alternatives: (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point.

   Page reading and the complete reading-key list remain under *note
Reading and navigating: (emacsvox)Reading.  Page reading changes the
mark; it is not a substitute for inspecting an existing selection.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Everyday Editing Commands,  Next: Everyday Workspace Commands,  Prev: Everyday Reading Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.2 Editing reminders
======================

Use these in ordinary text, after leaving any prompt or special reader.
Check the buffer and the affected text first.  *note Selection and
editing terms: (emacsvox)Editing Terms. defines Emacs's names for
copying, cutting and pasting.

Start a selection (‘C-<SPC>’)
     Set the mark, then move point to its other end.  Extend it by
     moving rather than repeatedly setting the mark.  See *note
     Selection boundaries and newline checks: (emacsvox)Selecting Text
     With Speech.
Inspect the other end (‘C-x C-x’)
     Exchange point and mark.  The selected text is unchanged until you
     move an endpoint.  See *note Adjusting a checked selection:
     (emacsvox)Selecting Text With Speech.
Copy (‘M-w’)
     Copy the checked region to the kill ring without removing the
     source text.  See *note Copying into another buffer:
     (emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers.
Cut (‘C-w’)
     Remove the checked region and retain it in the kill ring.  This
     edits the buffer, not the saved file.  See *note Moving text and
     verifying both locations: (emacsvox)Moving Text Safely.
Paste (‘C-y’)
     Insert the current kill-ring entry at point; clipboard integration
     can supply a more recent external copy instead.  Point ends after
     the inserted text.  See *note Checking the destination and pasted
     text: (emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers.
Use an earlier copy (‘M-y’)
     Immediately after a yank, replace that insertion with an earlier
     entry.  After a separate command, including a reading command,
     choose an entry for another insertion instead.  See *note Earlier
     copies and yank sequencing: (emacsvox)Choosing Earlier Copied Text.
Undo while listening (‘M-x undo-only <RET>’)
     Undo a change in the selected buffer, inspect it, and repeat only
     as needed.  Deactivate an unrelated selection first.  Unlike
     ordinary ‘C-/’, this avoids undoing an earlier undo after an
     intervening reading command.  See *note Undo, inspection and
     recovery limits: (emacsvox)Recovering Text Edits.
Find text (‘C-s’ or ‘C-r’)
     Search forwards or backwards.  Finish with ‘<RET>’ before typing an
     edit; text entered during search changes the query.  See *note
     Search direction and failure recovery: (emacsvox)Searching And
     Replacing Text.
Review replacements (‘M-%’)
     Query-replace in the checked active region, or ordinarily from
     point onwards when no region is active.  The replacement questions
     have their own keys; stopping does not undo accepted changes.  See
     *note Scoped replacement and match-by-match decisions:
     (emacsvox)Reviewing Text Replacements.

   Copying is not a permanent backup.  Message repetition and filename
speech can also change the kill ring; *note the transfer guide:
(emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers. explains how to inspect it
without accidentally replacing the next paste.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Everyday Workspace Commands,  Next: Everyday Help Commands,  Prev: Everyday Editing Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.3 Files, buffers and windows
===============================

A buffer contains work; a window displays it.  Identify the selected
buffer with ‘C-e m’ before editing, saving or closing anything.  For
definitions, see *note Buffers, windows and frames: (emacsvox)Working
Context Terms.

Open a file (‘C-x C-f’)
     Visit a pathname, creating a new unsaved buffer if the file does
     not exist.  ‘C-x C-r’ visits read-only instead.  See *note Checking
     the document and its destination: (emacsvox)Opening A Document.
Save the current file (‘C-x C-s’)
     Write this buffer to its file; a new buffer may need a filename.
     An error is not a completed save.  See *note Saving destinations
     and failure recovery: (emacsvox)Saving A Working Copy.
Save under another filename (‘C-x C-w’)
     Write the current text, including unsaved edits, and change where
     this buffer's later saves go.  Cancel an unexpected overwrite
     prompt.  See *note Working copies versus copies of saved files:
     (emacsvox)Saving A Working Copy.
Switch to another buffer (‘C-x b’)
     Select content by buffer name in the current window.  The buffer
     you leave stays available.  See *note Buffer selection:
     (emacsvox)Desktop Objects.
Display a reference (‘C-x 4 b’)
     Display and select an existing buffer in another window, creating
     or reusing a view.  See *note Keeping a reference beside a draft:
     (emacsvox)Reference And Draft Windows.
Select another window (‘C-x o’)
     Select the next view; lowercase o is not zero.  With more than two
     windows, the next one need not be the draft.  See *note Identifying
     the selected view: (emacsvox)Working With Windows.
Remove a window (‘C-x 0’)
     Remove this view, not its buffer.  The last ordinary window cannot
     be removed this way.  ‘C-x 1’ instead keeps only the selected view.
     See *note Closing windows without losing documents:
     (emacsvox)Closing Windows Safely.
Close a buffer (‘C-x k’)
     Kill the named buffer, checking any unsaved-work prompt.  This is
     not the same as removing its window or deleting its file.  See
     *note Leaving document buffers safely: (emacsvox)Opening A
     Document.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Everyday Help Commands,  Next: Everyday Speech Commands,  Prev: Everyday Workspace Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.4 Help and orientation
=========================

Help can change the selected buffer and window arrangement.  Before your
next editing command, check that you have returned to the intended
document.

Identify your context (‘C-e m’)
     Speak the mode line, including the current buffer and major mode.
     See *note Position and status commands: (emacsvox)Status
     Information.
Describe the windows (‘C-e C-w’)
     Describe the current arrangement.  This does not select a different
     view.  See *note Working with windows through speech:
     (emacsvox)Working With Windows.
Explain a key (‘C-h k’ followed by the key sequence)
     Describe its effective binding without invoking it as the requested
     action.  See *note Discovering a key, command or setting:
     (emacsvox)Using Online Help.
Discover local commands (‘C-h m’)
     Describe the current modes.  ‘C-h b’ lists effective bindings;
     invoke these from the application you are asking about.  See *note
     Help for the current buffer: (emacsvox)Using Online Help.
Read Help again (‘C-e h’)
     Select and read Help using a windowful helper that removes other
     windows without killing their buffers.  Lowercase h is different
     from capital H for settings.  See *note Reading Help and returning
     to work: (emacsvox)Using Online Help.
Open the manual (‘C-e C-i’)
     Open this manual directly in Info.  Use ‘i’ in Info to look up a
     subject and ‘l’ to retrace a link.  See *note Manual lookup and
     return navigation: (emacsvox)Finding Help In Info.

   ‘C-e f’ speaks a filename or default directory, and ‘C-e a’ repeats a
message.  Both also put text in the kill ring and can affect the next
paste.  See *note Status commands and their side effects:
(emacsvox)Status Information.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Everyday Speech Commands,  Next: Emacsvox Key Prefixes,  Prev: Everyday Help Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.5 Speech and settings
========================

These routes use the current settings interface.  A live adjustment, a
saved default and a retained draft are distinct; follow the linked scope
and saving instructions before making a persistent change.

Open speech controls (‘C-e H s’)
     Browse rate, punctuation and related controls for the captured
     context.  Inspect the displayed scope; global defaults need not
     override local values.  See *note Speech controls and scope:
     (emacsvox)Speech System.
Open settings for this task (‘C-e S’)
     Use the integration's focused menu when available, otherwise
     general buffer settings.  ‘C-e H .’ always opens the latter.  See
     *note Finding the appropriate settings menu: (emacsvox)Using Online
     Help.
Find a setting (‘C-e H /’)
     Choose a matching name; accepting the search selects its control
     without changing the value.  Activate it separately.  See *note
     Settings search and action search: (emacsvox)Finding Settings And
     Actions.
Explain this item's feedback (‘C-e E’)
     Inspect the presentation chosen for the object at point.  The
     report is not itself a repair.  See *note Understanding voices,
     sounds and rules: (emacsvox)Explaining Presentation.

   In an Emacsvox settings menu, ‘q’ goes back and ‘C-q’ closes the
sequence.  In an ordinary unfinished prompt, ‘C-g’ cancels.  None of
these reverses an already applied change.  Editors, tables and terminals
have their own return commands; see *note Returning from settings:
(emacsvox)Returning From Settings. and *note Recovering an unfinished
edit: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural Changes.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emacsvox Key Prefixes,  Prev: Everyday Speech Commands,  Up: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands

15.6 How prefixes and menus fit together
========================================

‘C-e’
     The main Emacsvox keymap, including reading, status, and navigation
     commands.
‘C-e d’
     The text-to-speech keymap, including direct rate, punctuation, and
     echo controls.
‘C-e H’
     Open the general settings menu.  Its displayed keys and contextual
     Help provide discoverable routes to speech, voices, sounds, rules,
     and diagnostics.
‘C-e S’
     Open focused settings for the captured integration when available,
     otherwise general buffer settings.  C-e H .  always opens general
     buffer settings.

   A prefix such as ‘C-e’ waits for another key; ‘C-e H’ is a complete
command that opens a settings menu.  Release Control-e before typing
capital H.  Once the menu is open, its own displayed letters choose
actions.  They are not additional keys to hold with Control.

   Inside an Emacsvox settings menu, arrows browse and ‘<RET>’ activates
a choice.  ‘?’ followed by an item's key explains it; ‘q’ goes back and
‘C-q’ closes the sequence.  A voice editor, terminal, table reader or
another Transient application retains its own keys.  The same letter can
mean Save in an editor, Export in a palette manager or ordinary input in
a terminal.  Check which view has focus before applying a command from
another view's instructions.

   Integration-specific and convenience maps depend on major/minor
modes, window system or terminal, and personal configuration.  Live Help
resolves those contexts.  The manual uses the default speech prefix; a
customised prefix changes its entry sequences without changing a menu's
own displayed shortcuts.

15.7 Choose a different speech prefix
=====================================

Set ‘emacsvox-prefix’ before loading Emacsvox.  For example:

     (setq emacsvox-prefix (kbd "C-c e"))

   Then ‘C-c e l’ reads the line and ‘C-c e e’ provides end-of-line
recovery.  Evil, EXWM, widgets, and Term use the prefix followed by ‘e’
for their corresponding recovery command when it contains multiple
events.  Repeating such a prefix is also bound if unused or already
assigned to that recovery.  Existing commands and submaps take
precedence: ‘C-c e C-c’ retains selective display, so ‘C-c e C-c e’ is
not installed as recovery.

   Single-event prefixes keep their repeated-key behaviour, including
default ‘C-e C-e’.  Mode-local bindings can still shadow the prefix;
inspect them before assuming a key is unavailable or broken.

15.8 Inspect the effective bindings
===================================

‘C-h m’ describes the current modes and their commands.  ‘C-h b’ lists
effective bindings, and ‘C-h k’ followed by a key identifies its
command.  After typing a prefix such as ‘C-e’, ‘C-h’ shows that prefix's
current bindings.

   When live Help differs from this manual, check personal configuration
and installed package versions, then compare the generated defaults.
For speech feedback problems rather than binding discovery, see *note
Troubleshooting And Recovery: (emacsvox)Presentation Troubleshooting.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Tasks,  Next: Emacsvox Terminology,  Prev: Emacsvox Keyboard Commands,  Up: Top

16 Find a task
**************

Choose the result you want.  Each link leads to the procedure, its
conditions and its recovery instructions; following a link changes no
settings.  *note Something is wrong: Finding Problem Tasks. starts from
a symptom rather than an editor's name.  For a key reminder rather than
a worked procedure, use *note Everyday commands and key prefixes:
(emacsvox)Emacsvox Keyboard Commands.

   If the keyboard instructions are unfamiliar, begin with *note Read
the key instructions: Emacs Key Notation.  For terms such as palette,
profile or draft, use *note Terms used in this manual: Emacsvox
Terminology.

* Menu:

* Read and navigate: Finding Reading Tasks.  Recover speech, read text, find commands, and move between tasks.
* Edit and rearrange text: Finding Editing Tasks.  Select, copy, move, replace and recover text through speech.
* Change voices and sounds: Finding Voice Tasks.  Choose reading voices, preview changes, and adjust individual cues.
* Keep, inspect, or reverse settings: Finding Settings Tasks.  Understand persistence, retained drafts, notifications, and recovery.
* Work in an application: Finding Application Tasks.  Find file, document, web, mail, terminal and agent tasks.
* Something is wrong: Finding Problem Tasks.  Start with a symptom and inspect the cause before changing more settings.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Reading Tasks,  Next: Finding Editing Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.1 Read and navigate
======================

   • *note Understand the keyboard instructions: (emacsvox)Emacs Key
     Notation.: learn modifiers, separate steps, uppercase letters and
     command names before using a shortcut.
   • *note Know where to type an answer: Answering Emacs Prompts.:
     distinguish minibuffer prompts, completion, document text and
     command examples.
   • *note Get speech working again: (emacsvox)Recovering From No
     Speech.: start with the no-speech recovery procedure before
     changing presentation rules or voices.
   • *note Stop current speech: (emacsvox)Everyday Reading Commands.:
     recall the stop key and its effect on tracked reading without
     disabling feedback from subsequent commands.
   • *note Inspect a line, sentence or paragraph: Reading Without Moving
     Point.: choose the reading unit and preserve the editing position,
     with the limits of large requests explained.
   • *note Read from here and recover my place: Reading From Point.: use
     ‘C-e i’ for supported tracked reading and understand stopping,
     repeated chunks and backend limitations.
   • *note Switch documents without closing my work: (emacsvox)Desktop
     Objects.: choose a buffer by name or cycle among related buffers;
     window selection is a separate operation.
   • *note Keep a reference beside my draft: Reference And Draft
     Windows.: select the intended view and move between tasks without
     closing either document.
   • *note Remove a window without losing the document: Closing Windows
     Safely.: distinguish layout changes from killing a buffer or saving
     a file.
   • *note Find out what a key does: (emacsvox)Using Online Help.:
     inspect the binding in the current buffer rather than assuming a
     manual's default overrides your configuration.
   • *note Read a table by row or column: (emacsvox)Reading Markdown And
     Org Tables.: use the shared table reader for Markdown and Org.
     *note Agent Shell tables: (emacsvox)Agent Shell Tables. have their
     own contextual keys and spoken units.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Editing Tasks,  Next: Finding Voice Tasks,  Prev: Finding Reading Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.2 Edit and rearrange text
============================

   • *note Hear exactly what I selected: Selecting Text With Speech.:
     establish point and mark and inspect the newline boundary before an
     operation.
   • *note Copy text into another document: Copying Text Between
     Buffers.: keep the source intact and check the insertion and
     kill-ring contents at the destination.
   • *note Move a paragraph: Moving Text Safely.: cut a checked passage
     and verify both its old and new positions, including blank lines.
   • *note Paste something I copied earlier: Choosing Earlier Copied
     Text.: distinguish the kill-ring chooser from replacing the last
     yank with an earlier entry.
   • *note Undo and listen between corrections: Recovering Text Edits.:
     use the right buffer's undo history without accidentally redoing
     earlier undos.
   • *note Copy between Emacs and another application: Copying Through
     The Clipboard.: test the clipboard boundary without assuming that
     Windows, WSL and terminal sessions work identically.
   • *note Replace only the occurrences I choose: Reviewing Text
     Replacements.: limit the operation to a checked region and review
     each proposed change before saving.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Voice Tasks,  Next: Finding Settings Tasks,  Prev: Finding Editing Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.3 Change voices and sounds
=============================

   • *note Choose which voice or feedback setting to change:
     (emacsvox)Choosing A Presentation Change.: compare a shared voice
     edit, a matching rule, a palette-wide change and an engine
     preference.
   • *note Change my ordinary reading voice: (emacsvox)Editing Named
     Voices.: work with the palette's ‘default’ named voice.  Preview,
     saving, live selection, and startup selection are distinct.
   • *note Change the voice used for headings: (emacsvox)Heading Voice
     Walkthrough.: practise with a temporary buffer rule before making a
     wider change.
   • *note Try an installed voice without saving anything:
     (emacsvox)Previewing An Installed Voice.: follow the listening
     exercise through the current settings menus and return to your
     source.
   • *note Choose a preferred speech engine: (emacsvox)Speech Engine
     Preferences.: change engine preference rather than assuming that a
     palette edit changes every engine-selection policy.
   • *note Find and install another voice: (emacsvox)Installed Voices
     And Engines.: distinguish availability, downloaded files,
     enablement, and application to the running speech workers.
   • *note Replace or silence one unwanted sound: (emacsvox)Changing One
     Sound.: target the cue you want to change rather than disabling all
     auditory icons.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Settings Tasks,  Next: Finding Application Tasks,  Prev: Finding Voice Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.4 Keep, inspect, or reverse settings
=======================================

   • *note Make a setting survive restarting Emacs: (emacsvox)Saving And
     Startup.: identify which resource owns the setting and use its own
     saving or startup action.
   • *note Understand why Save did not change what I hear:
     (emacsvox)Saved Voice Change Not Audible.: inspect the saved voice,
     its selected palette and the result of application before editing
     again.
   • *note Resume an unfinished edit: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural
     Changes.: use the retained-draft route to return to the original
     editor.  A retained draft is not a saved setup.
   • *note Find out why an item sounded that way: (emacsvox)Explaining
     Presentation.: inspect the chosen voices, cues, and rules;
     distinguish recorded feedback from a simulation.
   • *note Undo a temporary feedback change: (emacsvox)Heading Voice
     Walkthrough.: the heading exercise includes checking its scope and
     removing the practice rule.  For broader problems, use *note
     presentation recovery: (emacsvox)Recovering Aural Changes.
   • *note Adjust notifications without changing ordinary reading:
     (emacsvox)Notification Routing.: choose where notifications play
     and distinguish selecting a route from restarting it.  *note Output
     volumes: (emacsvox)Output Volumes. controls loudness separately.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Application Tasks,  Next: Finding Problem Tasks,  Prev: Finding Settings Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.5 Work in an application
===========================

   • *note Open the right file: Opening A Document.: check the buffer
     and full pathname before editing.
   • *note Keep the original and work on a copy: Saving A Working Copy.:
     distinguish current buffer text from the last saved file, and check
     where later saves will go.
   • *note Find a file and hear its details: Browsing Files With Dired.:
     browse names, inspect size or date, and return without deleting or
     moving files.
   • *note Write a note I can reopen later: Writing An Org Document.:
     create a real Org file rather than leaving the text in a disposable
     buffer.
   • *note Review a document and correct a word: Reviewing An Org
     Document.: reveal headings and body text, inspect a small edit, and
     save the intended version.
   • *note Read a web page: Opening A Web Page.: enter an address and
     identify the rendered browser buffer before using navigation keys.
   • *note Find information within a page: Navigating A Web Page.: use
     its HTML structure or search the loaded text, without submitting a
     new web search.
   • *note Follow a link and get back: Following Web Links.: distinguish
     browser history, separate buffers and saved website bookmarks.
   • *note Limit the mail details spoken automatically:
     (emacsvox)Notmuch Speech And Privacy.: choose fields, bounds, and
     completion feedback.  Explicit full-detail reading bypasses
     automatic bounds.
   • *note Hear a terminal's completion candidates: (emacsvox)EAT
     Terminal Completion.: keep terminal-side completion separate from
     Emacs completion and inspect retained candidates when needed.
   • *note Review terminal output without sending input: (emacsvox)EAT
     Screen Review.: use EAT's frozen review for a retained screen.
     *note Term review: (emacsvox)Reviewing The Term Screen. follows a
     different, live-buffer model.
   • *note Enter a terminal password safely: (emacsvox)EAT Passwords And
     Privacy.: use EAT's protected input operation and understand why
     ordinary no-echo input is not the same boundary.
   • *note Read the last completed agent response in full:
     (emacsvox)Reading Agent Responses.: explicitly inspect the
     successful response rather than changing the automatic speech
     policy.
   • *note Copy code from an agent response: (emacsvox)Agent Shell
     Source Blocks.: navigate to its source block and use the logical
     copy operation rather than selecting renderer padding.

   For other applications, begin with *note Applications and
Integrations: (emacsvox)Emacs Packages. and the active buffer's mode
Help.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Finding Problem Tasks,  Prev: Finding Application Tasks,  Up: Finding Tasks

16.6 Something is wrong
=======================

Start with the closest symptom.  These routes preserve existing work and
help you decide what to inspect before attempting a repair.  A failed
preview, an unsaved draft and a failed application are different
problems.

   • *note There is no speech at all: (emacsvox)Recovering From No
     Speech.: restore a working speech route before using tools that
     depend on spoken inspection.
   • *note Reading restarted part of a sentence: Reading From Point.:
     distinguish chunk-based tracking from exact-word pause/resume and
     inspect the retained position.
   • *note Text was pasted in the wrong place: Recovering Text Edits.:
     undo in the affected buffer and check the result before trying the
     transfer again.
   • *note A paste inserted a filename or message instead of my passage:
     (emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers.: inspect which command
     changed the kill ring before choosing the earlier copy.
   • *note Help replaced my other windows: (emacsvox)Using Online Help.:
     check the selected buffer and restore the desired views; a
     collapsed layout need not mean lost documents.
   • *note I saved a voice, but it still sounds the same:
     (emacsvox)Saved Voice Change Not Audible.: check selection,
     matching rules, individual tuning and engine support in that order.
   • *note My settings disappeared after restarting: (emacsvox)Settings
     Lost After Restart.: distinguish temporary changes from saved
     resources and their startup selection.
   • *note Only one application or document sounds wrong:
     (emacsvox)Feedback Differs Between Buffers.: compare the same kind
     of action and inspect local settings, integration policy and
     matching rules.
   • *note There is too much talking: (emacsvox)Feedback Differs Between
     Buffers.: distinguish automatic content, training explanations,
     extra labels and the notification stream before silencing it.
   • *note I cannot tell whether a change succeeded:
     (emacsvox)Unconfirmed Speech Changes.: keep the saved result and
     unfinished work while checking stream acknowledgements or a
     timeout.
   • *note I cannot get back to what I was reading: (emacsvox)Returning
     From Settings.: leave the current editor using its own return
     action, then use the menu's captured-source return.
   • *note A web page is cluttered or missing content: Recovering A Web
     Page.: restore its reading view, distinguish a fresh request from
     history, or use an external browser.
   • *note I need to report an unresolved problem: (emacsvox)Reporting
     Speech Problems.: collect a reproducible example and a reviewed
     diagnostic report without exposing private speech or paths.

   For a missing cue, an unfamiliar profile status or another symptom,
use *note Troubleshooting and recovery: (emacsvox)Presentation
Troubleshooting.  Avoid resetting unrelated settings to test one
explanation; change only the cause you have identified and repeat the
original action to check the result.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Emacsvox Terminology,  Next: Copyright,  Prev: Finding Tasks,  Up: Top

17 Terms used in this manual
****************************

These definitions help you choose a procedure.  They do not replace a
control's specific scope, save, or recovery instructions.  *note Find a
task: Finding Tasks. starts from what you want to accomplish rather than
the names below.  For a key reminder, use *note Everyday commands and
key prefixes: (emacsvox)Emacsvox Keyboard Commands.

   *note Working context: (emacsvox)Working Context Terms. covers
buffers and views; *note selection and editing: (emacsvox)Editing Terms.
covers mark, region, copying and recovery.  *note Presentation
resources: (emacsvox)Presentation Resource Terms. and *note changes and
speech output: (emacsvox)Speech And Settings Terms. explain
configuration.

* Menu:

* Your working context: Working Context Terms.  Distinguish buffers, views, editing positions and restrictions.
* Selection, copying and editing: Editing Terms.  Translate selection, cut and paste into mark, region, kill and yank.
* Voices and presentation resources: Presentation Resource Terms.  Distinguish physical voices, named voices, palettes, rules and profiles.
* Changes and speech output: Speech And Settings Terms.  Distinguish preview, drafts, saving, application and speech workers.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Working Context Terms,  Next: Editing Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.1 Your working context
=========================

Buffer
     An Emacs object containing a document or application view.  It is
     not necessarily a file, and switching buffers does not close the
     one you leave.  A window displays a buffer.  See *note Buffers hold
     your work: (emacsvox)Desktop Objects.
Window
     A view of a buffer inside an Emacs frame.  Removing a window does
     not kill its buffer.  Two windows showing the same buffer are views
     of the same text, not independent copies.  See *note Working with
     windows: (emacsvox)Working With Windows.
Frame
     An outer Emacs application window, or the corresponding display in
     a terminal session.  It contains Emacs windows.  Selecting another
     window within a frame differs from switching applications in the
     operating system.  See *note Windows and frames: (emacsvox)Working
     With Windows.
Point
     The current editing position, between characters.  Text is normally
     inserted there.  Short reading often leaves it unchanged; tracked
     reading advances it.  See *note Reading position and interruption:
     (emacsvox)Reading From Point.
Mode
     The commands and behaviour associated with a buffer.  Its major
     mode supplies its principal tools; minor modes add other behaviour.
     See *note Let modes give each buffer its tools: (emacsvox)Object
     Oriented Desktop.
Read-only
     A buffer state that prevents ordinary text edits.  It does not mean
     the buffer has no unsaved changes, and making a buffer writable
     does not grant permission to overwrite a protected file.  Special
     applications may still provide their own operations.  See *note
     Opening a document safely: (emacsvox)Opening A Document.
Narrowing
     Restricting access to part of a buffer without deleting the rest.
     Reading, movement and many editing commands stay within that
     portion; widening removes the restriction.  Ordinary file saving
     still saves the entire buffer.  See *note Reading accessible text:
     (emacsvox)Reading Without Moving Point. and *note Narrowing:
     (emacs)Narrowing. in the GNU Emacs Manual.
Captured source
     The buffer and position retained when a settings workflow starts.
     Contextual actions continue to refer to that source rather than
     whichever menu or editor is currently selected.  A changed or
     closed source may need to be captured again.  See *note Getting
     started with settings menus: (emacsvox)Aural Home.
Scope
     Where a setting or rule applies, such as one buffer, a session, or
     global defaults.  Scope is not the same as persistence: a global
     change need not be saved, and a saved resource need not be active.
     See *note Choose what persists: (emacsvox)Saving And Startup.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Editing Terms,  Next: Presentation Resource Terms,  Prev: Working Context Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.2 Selection, copying and editing
===================================

Mark
     A remembered position in a buffer.  Point and mark delimit the
     region, whichever comes first.  The mark can remain after the
     selection is deactivated; it is not copied text.  See *note
     Selecting and checking text: (emacsvox)Selecting Text With Speech.
Region or selection
     The text between point and mark, including any selected spaces and
     newlines.  The character at its end is excluded.  The active region
     is the current selection; some commands can also use an inactive
     mark.  Establish a fresh region before cutting.  See *note
     Selection boundaries and activation: (emacsvox)Selecting Text With
     Speech.
Transient Mark mode
     Emacs's usual active-selection behaviour, used by the editing
     examples.  It is unrelated to the Transient package that supplies
     settings menus.  See *note Using the region through speech:
     (emacsvox)Selecting Text With Speech. and *note The mark and the
     region: (emacs)Mark. in the GNU Emacs Manual.
Copy
     Store a passage in the kill ring while leaving its source text
     unchanged.  The destination becomes an independent text copy when
     you paste it.  See *note Copying between buffers: (emacsvox)Copying
     Text Between Buffers.
Kill or cut
     Remove text and keep it in the kill ring for later insertion.  Some
     deletion commands do not keep that copy.  Killing a buffer is a
     different operation: it closes the buffer rather than copying its
     contents.  See *note Moving text safely: (emacsvox)Moving Text
     Safely.
Yank or paste
     Insert text from the kill ring, or through configured clipboard
     integration, at point.  It changes the destination buffer, not
     automatically its saved file.  See *note Checking a paste:
     (emacsvox)Copying Text Between Buffers.
Kill ring
     Emacs's shared history of copied and cut text.  It can contain more
     than the latest passage, including text placed there by inspection
     commands.  It is not a permanent backup or the same store as the
     system clipboard.  See *note Choosing an earlier copy:
     (emacsvox)Choosing Earlier Copied Text.
Clipboard
     The operating system's text-transfer facility.  Its connection to
     Emacs depends on the display session and configuration; a new
     external copy can affect the next paste.  See *note Testing
     clipboard exchange: (emacsvox)Copying Through The Clipboard.
Undo
     Reverse recorded edits in the selected buffer.  It does not reverse
     a settings save or an action already sent to another application.
     When listening between successive corrections, use the recovery
     guide's ‘undo-only’ procedure.  See *note Undo and spoken
     inspection: (emacsvox)Recovering Text Edits.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Presentation Resource Terms,  Next: Speech And Settings Terms,  Prev: Editing Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.3 Voices and presentation resources
======================================

Physical voice
     A voice reported by a speech engine, such as an installed system
     voice or a downloaded model's voice.  Installation alone does not
     prove that it is enabled, active, or audible.  See *note Installed
     voices and engines: (emacsvox)Installed Voices And Engines.
Named voice
     A presentation name such as ‘default’ or ‘bolden’, resolved through
     a voice palette to physical choices and adjustments.  Editing a
     shared named voice can affect several kinds of text that use it.
     See *note Edit a named voice: (emacsvox)Editing Named Voices.
Voice palette
     A collection defining named voices, including their physical
     choices, fallbacks, and adjustments.  Palettes can inherit values;
     editing one is different from changing an engine preference.  See
     *note Voices and routing: (emacsvox)Voices And Routing.
Presentation rule
     A match between application meaning or context and the feedback to
     produce, such as a voice, cue, or silence.  A rule chooses
     presentation; it does not install a physical voice.  See *note
     Manage feedback rules: (emacsvox)Managing Feedback Rules.
Auditory icon or cue
     A short sound conveying an event or state.  A sound pack supplies
     cues, while presentation rules determine when to use them.  Tones
     can convey information without a recorded sound file.  See *note
     Sounds and space: (emacsvox)Sounds And Space.
Presentation profile
     A saved selection of presentation resources and options.  It refers
     to resources rather than copying everything into a full
     configuration backup.  Applying a profile also retains its
     selection for startup; individual palette operations have different
     rules.  See *note Presentation profiles: (emacsvox)Presentation
     Profiles.


File: emacsvox.info,  Node: Speech And Settings Terms,  Prev: Presentation Resource Terms,  Up: Emacsvox Terminology

17.4 Changes and speech output
==============================

Preview
     An audition or comparison of a proposed result.  Previewing does
     not by itself save the draft or make it the ordinary live
     configuration.
Retained draft
     An unfinished edit kept in the current Emacs session for return to
     its editor.  Retaining it is not a promise of recovery after Emacs
     exits, and discarding it does not undo a save already completed.
Save
     Record data in the store owned by that editor or setting.  Read the
     action's label: _Save and apply_ and _Save to collection without
     selecting_ intentionally have different effects.
Apply
     Put a chosen configuration into use.  The operation may require
     confirmation, validation, or a worker restart.  Its exact effect
     depends on the resource; it is not a universal synonym for saving.
Startup selection
     The configuration to select when a later session starts.  Selecting
     something for this session does not always select it for the next
     one.  *note Choose what persists: (emacsvox)Saving And Startup.
     compares these states and their owners.
Speech server, engine, and worker
     Emacsvox talks to a speech server; the server uses engines to
     synthesise speech.  The manual uses _worker_ for a running speech
     instance whose configuration and reported voices can differ from
     another instance or from installed files.  See *note Speech
     Backends: (emacsvox)Speech Backends.
Main and notification speech
     Separate output paths for foreground reading and other
     announcements.  An integration decides which path to use for an
     event; their existence is not a guarantee that all background text
     is private.  See *note Speech output and notifications:
     (emacsvox)Aural Speech Output.


